\shading10000\cfpat10 \b\f35\fs48\cf8\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid10252087 Sixth Canto\line }{\cf7\insrsid10252087 Prescribed Duties for Mankind
\par }\pard\plain \s97\ql \fi-605\li720\ri720\nowidctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp20\brdrcf14 \brdrl\brdrs\brdrw45\brsp20\brdrcf13 \tx1080\faauto\rin720\lin720\rtlgutter\itap0 \shading10000\cfpat7 \f43\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {
\fs28\cf13\insrsid10252087  \'c7r\'e9mad-Bh\'e4gavatam Canto 6: "Prescribed Duties for Mankind"}{\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid10252087\charrsid10252087 \chftn {\footnote\ftnalt \pard\plain \s1217\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 
\shading10000\cfpat7 \b\f35\fs24\cf13\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid5731824\charrsid10252087 \chftn }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.1 - Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Par\'e9k\'f1it said: O my lord, O \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9
, you have already described [in the Second Canto] the path of liberation [niv\'e5tti-m\'e4rga]. By following that path, one is certainly elevated gradually to the highest pla
netary system, Brahmaloka, from which one is promoted to the spiritual world along with Lord Brahm\'e4. Thus one's repetition of birth and death in the material world ceases.
\par }\pard \s1217\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.2 - O great sage \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9, unless the living entity is freed from th
e infection of the material modes of nature, he receives different types of bodies in which to enjoy or suffer, and according to the body, he is understood to have various inclinations. By following these inclinations he traverses the path called prav\'e5
tti-m\'e4rga, by which one may be elevated to the heavenly planets, as you have already described [in the Third Canto].
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.3 - You have also described [at the end of the Fifth Canto] the varieties of hellish life that result from impious activities, and you have described [in the Fourth Canto] the first manvantara, which was presided over by Sv
\'e4yambhuva Manu, the son of Lord Brahm\'e4.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.4-5 - My dear lord, you have described the dynasties and characteristics of King Priyavrata and King Utt\'e4nap\'e4da. The Sup
reme Personality of Godhead created this material world with various universes, planetary systems, planets and stars, with varied lands, seas, oceans, mountains, rivers, gardens and trees, all with different characteristics. These are divided among this p
lanet earth, the luminaries in the sky and the lower planetary systems. You have very clearly described these planets and the living entities who live on them.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.6 - O greatly fortunate and opulent \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9, now kindly tell me how human beings may be saved from having to enter hellish conditions in which they suffer terrible pains.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.7 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 replied: My dear King, if before one's next death whatever impious acts one has performed in this life with his mind, words and body are
 not counteracted through proper atonement according to the description of the Manu-sa\'e0hit\'e4 and other dharma-\'e7\'e4
stras, one will certainly enter the hellish planets after death and undergo terrible suffering, as I have previously described to you.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.8 - Therefore, before one's next death comes, as long as one's body is strong enough, one should quickly adopt the process of atonement according to \'e7\'e4
stra; otherwise one's time will be lost, and the reactions of his sins will increase. As an expert physician diagnoses and treats a disease according to its gravity, one should undergo atonement according to the severity of one's sins.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.9 - Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Par\'e9k\'f1it said: One may know that sinful activity is injurious for him because he actually sees that a crim
inal is punished by the government and rebuked by people in general and because he hears from scriptures and learned scholars that one is thrown into hellish conditions in the next life for committing sinful acts. Nevertheless, in spite of such knowledge,
 one is forced to commit sins again and again, even after performing acts of atonement. Therefore, what is the value of such atonement?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.10 - Sometimes one who is very alert so as not to commit sinful acts is victimized by sinful life again. I theref
ore consider this process of repeated sinning and atoning to be useless. It is like the bathing of an elephant, for an elephant cleanses itself by taking a full bath, but then throws dust over its head and body as soon as it returns to the land.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.11 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9, the son of Vedavy\'e4
sa, answered: My dear King, since acts meant to neutralize impious actions are also fruitive, they will not release one from the tendency to act fruitively. Persons who subject themselves to the rules and regulations 
of atonement are not at all intelligent. Indeed, they are in the mode of darkness. Unless one is freed from the mode of ignorance, trying to counteract one action through another is useless because this will not uproot one's desires. Thus even though one 
may superficially seem pious, he will undoubtedly be prone to act impiously. Therefore real atonement is enlightenment in perfect knowledge, Ved\'e4nta, by which one understands the Supreme Absolute Truth.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.12 - My dear King, if a diseased person eats t
he pure, uncontaminated food prescribed by a physician, he is gradually cured, and the infection of disease can no longer touch him. Similarly, if one follows the regulative principles of knowledge, he gradually progresses toward liberation from material 
contamination.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 
SB 6.1.13-14 - To concentrate the mind, one must observe a life of celibacy and not fall down. One must undergo the austerity of voluntarily giving up sense enjoyment. One must then control the mind and senses, give charity, be truthful, cle
an and nonviolent, follow the regulative principles and regularly chant the holy name of the Lord. Thus a sober and faithful person who knows the religious principles is temporarily purified of all sins performed with his body, words and mind. These sins 
are like the dried leaves of creepers beneath a bamboo tree, which may be burned by fire although their roots remain to grow again at the first opportunity.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.15 - Only a rare person who has adopted complete, unalloyed devotional service to K\'e5\'f1\'eba can 
uproot the weeds of sinful actions with no possibility that they will revive. He can do this simply by discharging devotional service, just as the sun can immediately dissipate fog by its rays.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.16 - My dear King, if a sinful person engages in the service of a bona fide devotee of the Lord and thus learns how to dedicate his life unto the lotus feet of K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a, he can be completely purified. One cannot be purified merely by undergoing austerity, penance, brahmacarya and the other methods of atonement I have previously described.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.17 - The path followed by pure devotees, who are well behaved and fully endowed with the best qualifications, is certainly the most auspicious path in this material world. It is free from fear, and it is authorized by the 
\'e7\'e4stras.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.18 - My dear King, as a pot containing liquor cannot be purified even if washed in the waters of many rivers, nondevotees cannot be purified by processes of atonement even if they perform them very well.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.19 - Although not having fully realized K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a, persons who have even once surrendered completely unto His lotus feet and who have become attracted to His name, form, qualities and pastimes are completely freed of all sinful reactions, for they have thus accepted the true method of
 atonement. Even in dreams, such surrendered souls do not see Yamar\'e4ja or his order carriers, who are equipped with ropes to bind the sinful.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.20 - In this regard, learned scholars and saintly persons describe a very old historical incident involving a discussion between the order carriers of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu and those of Yamar\'e4ja. Please hear of this from me.

\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.21 - In the city known as K\'e4nyakubja there was a br\'e4hma\'eba named Aj\'e4mila who married a prostitute maidservant and lost all his brahminical qualities because of the association of that low-class woman.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.22 - This fallen br\'e4hma\'eba, Aj\'e4mila, gave trouble to others by arresting them, by cheating them in gambling or by directly plundering them. This was the way he earned his livelihood and mai
ntained his wife and children.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.23 - My dear King, while he thus spent his time in abominable, sinful activities to maintain his family of many sons, eighty-eight years of his life passed by.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.24 - That old man Aj\'e4mila had ten sons, of whom the youngest was a baby named N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba. Since N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba was the youngest of all the sons, he was naturally very dear to both his father and his mother.

\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.25 - Because of the child's broken language and awkward movements, old Aj\'e4mila was very much attached to him. He always took care of the child and enjoyed the child's activities.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.26 - When Aj\'e4mila chewed food and ate it, he called the child to chew and eat, and when he drank he called the child to drink also. Always engaged in taking care of the child and calling his name, N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba, Aj\'e4
mila could not understand that his own time was now exhausted and that death was upon him.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.27 - When the time of death arrived for the foolish Aj\'e4mila, he began thinking exclusively of his son N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.28-29 - Aj\'e4mila then saw three awkward persons with deformed bodily features, fierce, twisted faces, and hair standing erect on their bodies. With ropes in their hands, they had come to take him away to the abode of Yamar
\'e4ja. When he saw them he was extremely bewildered, and because of attachment to his child, who was playing a short distance away, Aj\'e4
mila began to call him loudly by his name. Thus with tears in his eyes he somehow or other chanted the holy name of N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.30 - My dear King, the order carriers of Vi\'f1\'ebu, the Vi\'f1\'ebud\'fctas, immediately arrived when they heard the holy name of their master from the mouth of the dying Aj\'e4
mila, who had certainly chanted without offense because he had chanted in complete anxiety.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.31 - The order carriers of Yamar\'e4ja were snatching the soul from the core of the heart of Aj\'e4mila, the husband of the prostitute, but with resounding voices the messengers of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, the Vi\'f1\'ebud\'fc
tas, forbade them to do so.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.32 - When the order carriers of Yamar\'e4ja, the son of the sun-god, were thus forbidden, they replied: Who are you, sirs, that have the audacity to challenge the jurisdiction of Yamar\'e4ja?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.33 - Dear sirs, whose servants are you, where have you come from, and why are you forbidding us to touch the body of Aj\'e4
mila? Are you demigods from the heavenly planets, are you sub-demigods, or are you the best of devotees?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.34-36 - The order carriers of Yamar\'e4ja said: Your eyes are just like the petals of lotus flowers. Dressed in yellow silk
en garments, decorated with garlands of lotuses, and wearing very attractive helmets on your heads and earrings on your ears, you all appear fresh and youthful. Your four long arms are decorated with bows and quivers of arrows and with swords, clubs, conc
hshells, discs and lotus flowers. Your effulgence has dissipated the darkness of this place with extraordinary illumination. Now, sirs, why are you obstructing us?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.37 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: Being thus addressed by the messengers of Yamar\'e4ja, the servants of V\'e4sudeva smiled and spoke the following words in voices as deep as the sound of rumbling clouds.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.38 - The blessed messengers of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, the Vi\'f1\'ebud\'fctas, said: If you are actually servants of Yamar\'e4ja, you must explain to us the meaning of religious principles and the symptoms of irreligion.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.39 - What is the process of punishing others? Who are the actual candidates for punishment? Are all karm\'e9s engaged in fruitive activities punishable, or only some of them?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.40 - The Yamad\'fctas replied: That which is prescribed in the Vedas constitutes dharma, the religious principles, and the opposite of that is irreligion. The Vedas are directly the Supreme Personality of Godhead, N\'e4r\'e4
ya\'eba, and are self-born. This we have heard from Yamar\'e4ja.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.41 - The supreme cause of all causes, N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eb
a, is situated in His own abode in the spiritual world, but nevertheless He controls the entire cosmic manifestation according to the three modes of material nature-sattva-gu\'eba, rajo-gu\'eba and tamo-gu\'eb
a. In this way all living entities are awarded different qualities, different names [such as br\'e4hma\'eba, k\'f1atriya and vai\'e7ya], different duties according to the var\'eb\'e4\'e7rama institution, and different forms. Thus N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eb
a is the cause of the entire cosmic manifestation.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.42 - The sun, fire, sky, air, demigods, moon, evening, day, night, directions, water, land and Supersoul Himself all witness the activities of the living entity.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.43 - The candidates for punishment are those who are conf
irmed by these many witnesses to have deviated from their prescribed regulative duties. Everyone engaged in fruitive activities is suitable to be subjected to punishment according to his sinful acts.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.44 - O inhabitants of Vaiku\'eb\'f6ha, you are sinless, but those within this material world are all karm\'e9
s, whether acting piously or impiously. Both kinds of action are possible for them because they are contaminated by the three modes of nature and must act accordingly. One who has accepted a material body
 cannot be inactive, and sinful action is inevitable for one acting under the modes of material nature. Therefore all the living entities within this material world are punishable.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.45 - In proportion to the extent of one's religious or irreligious actions in this life, one must enjoy or suffer the corresponding reactions of his karma in the next.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.46 - O best of the demigods, we can see three different varieties of life, which are due to the contamination of the three modes of nature. The liv
ing entities are thus known as peaceful, restless and foolish; as happy, unhappy or in-between; or as religious, irreligious and semireligious. We can deduce that in the next life these three kinds of material nature will similarly act.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.47 - Just a
s springtime in the present indicates the nature of springtimes in the past and future, so this life of happiness, distress or a mixture of both gives evidence concerning the religious and irreligious activities of one's past and future lives.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.48 - The omnipotent Yamar\'e4ja is as good as Lord Brahm\'e4, for while situated in his own abode or in everyone's heart like the Param\'e4tm\'e4
, he mentally observes the past activities of a living entity and thus understands how the living entity will act in future lives.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 
SB 6.1.49 - As a sleeping person acts according to the body manifested in his dreams and accepts it to be himself, so one identifies with his present body, which he acquired because of his past religious or irreligious actions, and is unable to know h
is past or future lives.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.50 - Above the five senses of perception, the five working senses and the five objects of the senses is the mind, which is the sixteenth element. Above the mind is the seventeenth element, the soul, the living being himself
, who, in cooperation with the other sixteen, enjoys the material world alone. The living being enjoys three kinds of situations, namely happy, distressful and mixed.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.51 - The subtle body is endowed with sixteen parts-the five knowledge-acquiring s
enses, the five working senses, the five objects of sense gratification, and the mind. This subtle body is an effect of the three modes of material nature. It is composed of insurmountably strong desires, and therefore it causes the living entity to trans
m
igrate from one body to another in human life, animal life and life as a demigod. When the living entity gets the body of a demigod, he is certainly very jubilant, when he gets a human body he is always in lamentation, and when he gets the body of an anim
al, he is always afraid. In all conditions, however, he is actually miserable. His miserable condition is called sa\'e0s\'e5ti, or transmigration in material life.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.52 - The foolish embodied living entity, inept at controlling his senses and mind, is force
d to act according to the influence of the modes of material nature, against his desires. He is like a silkworm that uses its own saliva to create a cocoon and then becomes trapped in it, with no possibility of getting out. The living entity traps himself
 in a network of his own fruitive activities and then can find no way to release himself. Thus he is always bewildered, and repeatedly he dies.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.53 - Not a single living entity can remain unengaged even for a moment. One must act by his natural tende
ncy according to the three modes of material nature because this natural tendency forcibly makes him work in a particular way.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.54 - The fruitive activities a living being performs, whether pious or impious, are the unseen cause for the fulfillment 
of his desires. This unseen cause is the root for the living entity's different bodies. Because of his intense desire, the living entity takes birth in a particular family and receives a body which is either like that of his mother or like that of his fat
her. The gross and subtle bodies are created according to his desire.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.55 - Since the living entity is associated with material nature, he is in an awkward position, but if in the human form of life he is taught how to associate with the Supreme Pers
onality of Godhead or His devotee, this position can be overcome.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.56-57 - In the beginning this br\'e4hma\'eba named Aj\'e4mila studied all the Vedic literatures. He was a reservoir of good character, good conduct and good qualities. Firmly established in e
xecuting all the Vedic injunctions, he was very mild and gentle, and he kept his mind and senses under control. Furthermore, he was always truthful, he knew how to chant the Vedic mantras, and he was also very pure. Aj\'e4
mila was very respectful to his spiri
tual master, the fire-god, guests, and the elderly members of his household. Indeed, he was free from false prestige. He was upright, benevolent to all living entities, and well behaved. He would never speak nonsense or envy anyone.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.58-60 - Once this br\'e4hma\'eba Aj\'e4mila, following the order of his father, went to the forest to collect fruit, flowers and two kinds of grass, called samit and ku\'e7a. On the way home, he came upon a \'e7\'fc
dra, a very lusty, fourth-class man, who was shamelessly embracing and kissing a prostitute. The \'e7\'fcdra was smiling, singing and enjoying as if this were proper behavior. Both the \'e7\'fc
dra and the prostitute were drunk. The prostitute's eyes were rolling in intoxication, and her dress had become loose. Such was the condition in which Aj\'e4mila saw them.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.61 - The \'e7\'fcdra, his arm decorated with turmeric powder, was embracing the prostitute. When Aj\'e4mila saw her, the dormant lusty desires in his heart awakened, and in illusion he fell under their control.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.62 - As far as possible he patiently tried to remember the instructions of the \'e7\'e4
stras not even to see a woman. With the help of this knowledge and his intellect, he tried to control his lusty desires, but because of the force of Cupid within his heart, he failed to control his mind.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.63 - In the same way that the sun and moon are eclipsed by a low planet, the br\'e4hma\'eb
a lost all his good sense. Taking advantage of this situation, he always thought of the prostitute, and within a short time he took her as a servant in his house and abandoned all the regulative principles of a br\'e4hma\'eba.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.64 - Thus Aj\'e4mila began spending whatever money he had inherited from his father to satisfy the prostitute with various material presentations so that she would remain pleased with
 him. He gave up all his brahminical activities to satisfy the prostitute.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.65 - Because his intelligence was pierced by the lustful glance of the prostitute, the victimized br\'e4hma\'eba Aj\'e4mila engaged in sinful acts in her association. He even gave up
 the company of his very beautiful young wife, who came from a very respectable br\'e4hma\'eba family.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.66 - Although born of a br\'e4hma\'eba family, this rascal, bereft of intelligence because of the prostitute's association, earned money somehow or other, re
gardless of whether properly or improperly, and used it to maintain the prostitute's sons and daughters.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.67 - This br\'e4hma\'eba irresponsibly spent his long lifetime transgressing all the rules and regulations of the holy scripture, living extravagantl
y and eating food prepared by a prostitute. Therefore he is full of sins. He is unclean and is addicted to forbidden activities.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.68 - This man Aj\'e4mila did not undergo atonement. Therefore because of his sinful life, we must take him into the presence of Yamar\'e4
ja for punishment. There, according to the extent of his sinful acts, he will be punished and thus purified.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }}}{\fs28\cf13\insrsid10252087 
\par }\pard \s97\ql \fi-605\li720\ri720\nowidctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp20\brdrcf14 \brdrl\brdrs\brdrw45\brsp20\brdrcf13 \tx1080\faauto\outlinelevel0\rin720\lin720\rtlgutter\itap0\pararsid10252087 \shading10000\cfpat16 {
\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid10252087\charrsid10252087 \chftn {\footnote\ftnalt \pard\plain \s1217\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \shading10000\cfpat16 \b\f35\fs24\cf13\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {
\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid5731824\charrsid10252087 \chftn }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.1 - Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Par\'e9k\'f1it said: O my lord, O \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9, you have already described [in the Second Canto] the path of liberation [niv\'e5tti-m
\'e4rga]. By following that path, one is certainly elevated gradually to the highest planetary system, Brahmaloka, from which one is promoted to the spiritual world along with Lord Brahm\'e4. Thus one's repetition of birth and death in the materi
al world ceases.
\par }\pard \s1217\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.2 - O great sage \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9
, unless the living entity is freed from the infection of the material modes of nature, he receives different types of bodies in which to enjoy or suffer, and according to the body, he is understood
 to have various inclinations. By following these inclinations he traverses the path called prav\'e5tti-m\'e4rga, by which one may be elevated to the heavenly planets, as you have already described [in the Third Canto].
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.3 - You have also described [at the end of the Fifth Canto] the varieties of hellish life that result from impious activities, and you have described [in the Fourth Canto] the first manvantara, which was presided over by Sv
\'e4yambhuva Manu, the son of Lord Brahm\'e4.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.4-5 - My dear lord, you have described the dynasties and characteristics of King Priyavrata and King Utt\'e4nap\'e4
da. The Supreme Personality of Godhead created this material world with various universes, planetary systems, planets and stars, with varied lands, seas, oceans, moun
tains, rivers, gardens and trees, all with different characteristics. These are divided among this planet earth, the luminaries in the sky and the lower planetary systems. You have very clearly described these planets and the living entities who live on t
hem.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.6 - O greatly fortunate and opulent \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9, now kindly tell me how human beings may be saved from having to enter hellish conditions in which they suffer terrible pains.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.7 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 replied: My dear King, if before on
e's next death whatever impious acts one has performed in this life with his mind, words and body are not counteracted through proper atonement according to the description of the Manu-sa\'e0hit\'e4 and other dharma-\'e7\'e4
stras, one will certainly enter the hellish planets after death and undergo terrible suffering, as I have previously described to you.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.8 - Therefore, before one's next death comes, as long as one's body is strong enough, one should quickly adopt the process of atonement according to \'e7\'e4stra; 
otherwise one's time will be lost, and the reactions of his sins will increase. As an expert physician diagnoses and treats a disease according to its gravity, one should undergo atonement according to the severity of one's sins.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.9 - Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Par\'e9k\'f1
it said: One may know that sinful activity is injurious for him because he actually sees that a criminal is punished by the government and rebuked by people in general and because he hears from scriptures and learned scholars that one is thrown into helli
sh conditions in the next life for committing sinful acts. Nevertheless, in spite of such knowledge, one is forced to commit sins again and again, even after performing acts of atonement. Therefore, what is the value of such atonement?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.10 - Sometim
es one who is very alert so as not to commit sinful acts is victimized by sinful life again. I therefore consider this process of repeated sinning and atoning to be useless. It is like the bathing of an elephant, for an elephant cleanses itself by taking 
a full bath, but then throws dust over its head and body as soon as it returns to the land.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.11 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9, the son of Vedavy\'e4sa, answered: My dear King, since acts meant to neutralize impious actions are also fruitive, they will not release 
one from the tendency to act fruitively. Persons who subject themselves to the rules and regulations of atonement are not at all intelligent. Indeed, they are in the mode of darkness. Unless one is freed from the mode of ignorance, trying to counteract on
e action through another is useless because this will not uproot one's desires. Thus even though one may superficially seem pious, he will undoubtedly be prone to act impiously. Therefore real atonement is enlightenment in perfect knowledge, Ved\'e4
nta, by which one understands the Supreme Absolute Truth.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.12 - My dear King, if a diseased person eats the pure, uncontaminated food prescribed by a physician, he is gradually cured, and the infection of disease can no longer touch him. Similarly, if one foll
ows the regulative principles of knowledge, he gradually progresses toward liberation from material contamination.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.13-14 - To concentrate the mind, one must observe a life of celibacy and not fall down. One must undergo the austerity of voluntarily
 giving up sense enjoyment. One must then control the mind and senses, give charity, be truthful, clean and nonviolent, follow the regulative principles and regularly chant the holy name of the Lord. Thus a sober and faithful person who knows the religiou
s
 principles is temporarily purified of all sins performed with his body, words and mind. These sins are like the dried leaves of creepers beneath a bamboo tree, which may be burned by fire although their roots remain to grow again at the first opportunity
.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.15 - Only a rare person who has adopted complete, unalloyed devotional service to K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a can uproot the weeds of sinful actions with no possibility that they will revive. He can do this simply by discharging devotional service, just as the sun can immediately dissipate fog by its rays.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.16 - My dear King, if a sinful person engages in the service of a bona fide devotee of the Lord and thus learns how to dedicate his life unto the lotus feet of K\'e5\'f1\'eba, he can be completely purified. One cannot 
be purified merely by undergoing austerity, penance, brahmacarya and the other methods of atonement I have previously described.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.17 - The path followed by pure devotees, who are well behaved and fully endowed with the best qualifications, is certainly the most auspicious path in this material world. It is free from fear, and it is authorized by the 
\'e7\'e4stras.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.18 - My dear King, as a pot containing liquor cannot be purified even if washed in the waters of many rivers, nondevotees cannot be purified by processes of atonement even if they perform them very well.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.19 - Although not having fully realized K\'e5\'f1\'eba, persons who have even once surrendered completely unto His lotus feet and who have become attracted to His name, form, qualities and 
pastimes are completely freed of all sinful reactions, for they have thus accepted the true method of atonement. Even in dreams, such surrendered souls do not see Yamar\'e4ja or his order carriers, who are equipped with ropes to bind the sinful.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.20 - In this regard, learned scholars and saintly persons describe a very old historical incident involving a discussion between the order carriers of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu and those of Yamar\'e4ja. Please hear of this from me.

\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.21 - In the city known as K\'e4nyakubja there was a br\'e4hma\'eba named Aj\'e4mila who married a prostitute maidservant and lost all his brahminical qualities because of the association of that low-class woman.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.22 - This fallen br\'e4hma\'eba, Aj\'e4mila, gave trouble to others by arresting them, by cheati
ng them in gambling or by directly plundering them. This was the way he earned his livelihood and maintained his wife and children.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.23 - My dear King, while he thus spent his time in abominable, sinful activities to maintain his family of many sons, eighty-eight years of his life passed by.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.24 - That old man Aj\'e4mila had ten sons, of whom the youngest was a baby named N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba. Since N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba was the youngest of all the sons, he was naturally very dear to both his father and his mother.

\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.25 - Because of the child's broken language and awkward movements, old Aj\'e4mila was very much attached to him. He always took care of the child and enjoyed the child's activities.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.26 - When Aj\'e4mila chewed food and ate it, he called the child to chew and eat, and when he drank he called the child to drink also. Always engaged in taking care of the child and calling his name, N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba, Aj\'e4
mila could not understand that his own time was now exhausted and that death was upon him.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.27 - When the time of death arrived for the foolish Aj\'e4mila, he began thinking exclusively of his son N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.28-29 - Aj\'e4mila then saw three awkward persons with deformed bodily features, fierce, twisted faces, and hair standing erect on their bodies. With ropes in their hands, they had come to take him away to the abode of Yamar
\'e4ja. When he saw them he was extremely bewildered, and because of attachment to his child, who was playing a short distance away, Aj\'e4mila began to call him loudly by his name. Thus wit
h tears in his eyes he somehow or other chanted the holy name of N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.30 - My dear King, the order carriers of Vi\'f1\'ebu, the Vi\'f1\'ebud\'fctas, immediately arrived when they heard the holy name of their master from the mouth of the dying Aj\'e4mila, who ha
d certainly chanted without offense because he had chanted in complete anxiety.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.31 - The order carriers of Yamar\'e4ja were snatching the soul from the core of the heart of Aj\'e4mila, the husband of the prostitute, but with resounding voices the messengers of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, the Vi\'f1\'ebud\'fc
tas, forbade them to do so.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.32 - When the order carriers of Yamar\'e4ja, the son of the sun-god, were thus forbidden, they replied: Who are you, sirs, that have the audacity to challenge the jurisdiction of Yamar\'e4ja?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.33 - Dear sirs, whose servants are you, where have you come from, and why are you forbidding us to touch the body of Aj\'e4
mila? Are you demigods from the heavenly planets, are you sub-demigods, or are you the best of devotees?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.34-36 - The order carriers of Yamar\'e4
ja said: Your eyes are just like the petals of lotus flowers. Dressed in yellow silken garments, decorated with garlands of lotuses, and wearing very attractive helmets on your heads and earrings on your ears, you all appear fresh and yout
hful. Your four long arms are decorated with bows and quivers of arrows and with swords, clubs, conchshells, discs and lotus flowers. Your effulgence has dissipated the darkness of this place with extraordinary illumination. Now, sirs, why are you obstruc
ting us?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.37 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: Being thus addressed by the messengers of Yamar\'e4ja, the servants of V\'e4sudeva smiled and spoke the following words in voices as deep as the sound of rumbling clouds.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.38 - The blessed messengers of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, the Vi\'f1\'ebud\'fctas, said: If you are actually servants of Yamar\'e4ja, you must explain to us the meaning of religious principles and the symptoms of irreligion.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.39 - What is the process of punishing others? Who are the actual candidates for punishment? Are all karm\'e9s engaged in fruitive activities punishable, or only some of them?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.40 - The Yamad\'fctas replied: That which is prescribed in the Vedas constitutes dharma, the religious principles, and the opposite of that is irreligion. The Vedas are directly the Supreme Personality of Godhead, N\'e4r\'e4
ya\'eba, and are self-born. This we have heard from Yamar\'e4ja.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.41 - The supreme cause of all causes, N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba, is situated in His own abode in the spiritual world, but nevertheless He controls the 
entire cosmic manifestation according to the three modes of material nature-sattva-gu\'eba, rajo-gu\'eba and tamo-gu\'eba. In this way all living entities are awarded different qualities, different names [such as br\'e4hma\'eba, k\'f1atriya and vai\'e7
ya], different duties according to the var\'eb\'e4\'e7rama institution, and different forms. Thus N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba is the cause of the entire cosmic manifestation.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.42 - The sun, fire, sky, air, demigods, moon, evening, day, night, directions, water, land and Supersoul Himself all witness the activities of the living entity.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.43 - The candidates for punishment are those who are confirmed by these many witnesses to have deviated from their prescribed regulative duties. Everyone engaged in fruitive activities is suitable to be subjected
 to punishment according to his sinful acts.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.44 - O inhabitants of Vaiku\'eb\'f6ha, you are sinless, but those within this material world are all karm\'e9s, whether acting piously or impiously. Both kinds of action are possible for them because they are con
taminated by the three modes of nature and must act accordingly. One who has accepted a material body cannot be inactive, and sinful action is inevitable for one acting under the modes of material nature. Therefore all the living entities within this mate
rial world are punishable.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.45 - In proportion to the extent of one's religious or irreligious actions in this life, one must enjoy or suffer the corresponding reactions of his karma in the next.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.46 - O best of the demigods, we can see three 
different varieties of life, which are due to the contamination of the three modes of nature. The living entities are thus known as peaceful, restless and foolish; as happy, unhappy or in-between; or as religious, irreligious and semireligious. We can ded
uce that in the next life these three kinds of material nature will similarly act.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.47 - Just as springtime in the present indicates the nature of springtimes in the past and future, so this life of happiness, distress or a mixture of both gives evid
ence concerning the religious and irreligious activities of one's past and future lives.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.48 - The omnipotent Yamar\'e4ja is as good as Lord Brahm\'e4, for while situated in his own abode or in everyone's heart like the Param\'e4tm\'e4, he mentally observes the
 past activities of a living entity and thus understands how the living entity will act in future lives.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.49 - As a sleeping person acts according to the body manifested in his dreams and accepts it to be himself, so one identifies with his present 
body, which he acquired because of his past religious or irreligious actions, and is unable to know his past or future lives.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.50 - Above the five senses of perception, the five working senses and the five objects of the senses is the mind, which is
 the sixteenth element. Above the mind is the seventeenth element, the soul, the living being himself, who, in cooperation with the other sixteen, enjoys the material world alone. The living being enjoys three kinds of situations, namely happy, distressfu
l and mixed.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 
SB 6.1.51 - The subtle body is endowed with sixteen parts-the five knowledge-acquiring senses, the five working senses, the five objects of sense gratification, and the mind. This subtle body is an effect of the three modes of material nature.
 It is composed of insurmountably strong desires, and therefore it causes the living entity to transmigrate from one body to another in human life, animal life and life as a demigod. When the living entity gets the body of a demigod, he is certainly very 
jubilant, when he gets a human body he is always in lamentation, and when he gets the body of an animal, he is always afraid. In all conditions, however, he is actually miserable. His miserable condition is called sa\'e0s\'e5
ti, or transmigration in material life.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 
SB 6.1.52 - The foolish embodied living entity, inept at controlling his senses and mind, is forced to act according to the influence of the modes of material nature, against his desires. He is like a silkworm that uses its own saliva to create a cocoon
 and then becomes trapped in it, with no possibility of getting out. The living entity traps himself in a network of his own fruitive activities and then can find no way to release himself. Thus he is always bewildered, and repeatedly he dies.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.53 -
 Not a single living entity can remain unengaged even for a moment. One must act by his natural tendency according to the three modes of material nature because this natural tendency forcibly makes him work in a particular way.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.54 - The fruitive ac
tivities a living being performs, whether pious or impious, are the unseen cause for the fulfillment of his desires. This unseen cause is the root for the living entity's different bodies. Because of his intense desire, the living entity takes birth in a 
particular family and receives a body which is either like that of his mother or like that of his father. The gross and subtle bodies are created according to his desire.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.55 - Since the living entity is associated with material nature, he is in an a
wkward position, but if in the human form of life he is taught how to associate with the Supreme Personality of Godhead or His devotee, this position can be overcome.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.56-57 - In the beginning this br\'e4hma\'eba named Aj\'e4mila studied all the Vedic litera
tures. He was a reservoir of good character, good conduct and good qualities. Firmly established in executing all the Vedic injunctions, he was very mild and gentle, and he kept his mind and senses under control. Furthermore, he was always truthful, he kn
ew how to chant the Vedic mantras, and he was also very pure. Aj\'e4
mila was very respectful to his spiritual master, the fire-god, guests, and the elderly members of his household. Indeed, he was free from false prestige. He was upright, benevolent to all liv
ing entities, and well behaved. He would never speak nonsense or envy anyone.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.58-60 - Once this br\'e4hma\'eba Aj\'e4mila, following the order of his father, went to the forest to collect fruit, flowers and two kinds of grass, called samit and ku\'e7a. On the way home, he came upon a \'e7\'fc
dra, a very lusty, fourth-class man, who was shamelessly embracing and kissing a prostitute. The \'e7\'fcdra was smiling, singing and enjoying as if this were proper behavior. Both the \'e7\'fcdra and the prostitute were drunk. The prostitut
e's eyes were rolling in intoxication, and her dress had become loose. Such was the condition in which Aj\'e4mila saw them.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.61 - The \'e7\'fcdra, his arm decorated with turmeric powder, was embracing the prostitute. When Aj\'e4mila saw her, the dormant lusty desires in his heart awakened, and in illusion he fell under their control.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.62 - As far as possible he patiently tried to remember the instructions of the \'e7\'e4stras not even to see a woman. With the help of this knowledge and his intellect, he tried t
o control his lusty desires, but because of the force of Cupid within his heart, he failed to control his mind.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.63 - In the same way that the sun and moon are eclipsed by a low planet, the br\'e4hma\'eba lost all his good sense. Taking advantage of this 
situation, he always thought of the prostitute, and within a short time he took her as a servant in his house and abandoned all the regulative principles of a br\'e4hma\'eba.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.64 - Thus Aj\'e4mila began spending whatever money he had inherited from his fathe
r to satisfy the prostitute with various material presentations so that she would remain pleased with him. He gave up all his brahminical activities to satisfy the prostitute.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.65 - Because his intelligence was pierced by the lustful glance of the prostitute, the victimized br\'e4hma\'eba Aj\'e4
mila engaged in sinful acts in her association. He even gave up the company of his very beautiful young wife, who came from a very respectable br\'e4hma\'eba family.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.66 - Although born of a br\'e4hma\'eba family, this rasc
al, bereft of intelligence because of the prostitute's association, earned money somehow or other, regardless of whether properly or improperly, and used it to maintain the prostitute's sons and daughters.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.67 - This br\'e4hma\'eba irresponsibly spent his
 long lifetime transgressing all the rules and regulations of the holy scripture, living extravagantly and eating food prepared by a prostitute. Therefore he is full of sins. He is unclean and is addicted to forbidden activities.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.68 - This man Aj\'e4mila did not undergo atonement. Therefore because of his sinful life, we must take him into the presence of Yamar\'e4
ja for punishment. There, according to the extent of his sinful acts, he will be punished and thus purified.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }}}{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  1. }{\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid10252087\charrsid10252087 \chftn {\footnote\ftnalt \pard\plain \s1217\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 
\b\f35\fs24\cf13\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid5731824\charrsid10252087 \chftn }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.1 - Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Par\'e9k\'f1it said: O my lord, O \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9
, you have already described [in the Second Canto] the path of liberation [niv\'e5tti-m\'e4rga]. By following that path, one is certainly elevated gradually to the highest planetary system, Brahmaloka, from which one is promoted 
to the spiritual world along with Lord Brahm\'e4. Thus one's repetition of birth and death in the material world ceases.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.2 - O great sage \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9, unless the living entity is freed from the infection of the material modes of nature, he receiv
es different types of bodies in which to enjoy or suffer, and according to the body, he is understood to have various inclinations. By following these inclinations he traverses the path called prav\'e5tti-m\'e4
rga, by which one may be elevated to the heavenly planets, as you have already described [in the Third Canto].
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.3 - You have also described [at the end of the Fifth Canto] the varieties of hellish life that result from impious activities, and you have described [in the Fourth Canto] the first manvantara, which was presided over by Sv
\'e4yambhuva Manu, the son of Lord Brahm\'e4.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.4-5 - My dear lord, you have described the dynasties and characteristics of King Priyavrata and King Utt\'e4nap\'e4da. The Supreme Personality of Godhead created this material worl
d with various universes, planetary systems, planets and stars, with varied lands, seas, oceans, mountains, rivers, gardens and trees, all with different characteristics. These are divided among this planet earth, the luminaries in the sky and the lower p
lanetary systems. You have very clearly described these planets and the living entities who live on them.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.6 - O greatly fortunate and opulent \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9, now kindly tell me how human beings may be saved from having to enter hellish conditions in which they suffer terrible pains.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.7 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 replied: My dear King, if before one's next death whatever impious acts one has performed in this life with his mind, words and body are not counteracted through proper atonement according t
o the description of the Manu-sa\'e0hit\'e4 and other dharma-\'e7\'e4stras, one will certainly enter the hellish planets after death and undergo terrible suffering, as I have previously described to you.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.8 - Therefore, before one's next death comes, as long as one's body is strong enough, one should quickly adopt the process of atonement according to \'e7\'e4
stra; otherwise one's time will be lost, and the reactions of his sins will increase. As an expert physician diagnoses and treats a disease according to its gravity, one should undergo atonement according to the severity of one's sins.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.9 - Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Par\'e9k\'f1it said: One may know that sinful activity is injurious for him because he actually sees that a criminal is punished by the government and rebuked by peop
le in general and because he hears from scriptures and learned scholars that one is thrown into hellish conditions in the next life for committing sinful acts. Nevertheless, in spite of such knowledge, one is forced to commit sins again and again, even af
ter performing acts of atonement. Therefore, what is the value of such atonement?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.10 - Sometimes one who is very alert so as not to commit sinful acts is victimized by sinful life again. I therefore consider this process of repeated sinning and aton
ing to be useless. It is like the bathing of an elephant, for an elephant cleanses itself by taking a full bath, but then throws dust over its head and body as soon as it returns to the land.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.11 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9, the son of Vedavy\'e4sa, answered: M
y dear King, since acts meant to neutralize impious actions are also fruitive, they will not release one from the tendency to act fruitively. Persons who subject themselves to the rules and regulations of atonement are not at all intelligent. Indeed, they
 
are in the mode of darkness. Unless one is freed from the mode of ignorance, trying to counteract one action through another is useless because this will not uproot one's desires. Thus even though one may superficially seem pious, he will undoubtedly be p
rone to act impiously. Therefore real atonement is enlightenment in perfect knowledge, Ved\'e4nta, by which one understands the Supreme Absolute Truth.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.12 - My dear King, if a diseased person eats the pure, uncontaminated food prescribed by a physician
, he is gradually cured, and the infection of disease can no longer touch him. Similarly, if one follows the regulative principles of knowledge, he gradually progresses toward liberation from material contamination.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.13-14 - To concentrate the mind,
 one must observe a life of celibacy and not fall down. One must undergo the austerity of voluntarily giving up sense enjoyment. One must then control the mind and senses, give charity, be truthful, clean and nonviolent, follow the regulative principles a
n
d regularly chant the holy name of the Lord. Thus a sober and faithful person who knows the religious principles is temporarily purified of all sins performed with his body, words and mind. These sins are like the dried leaves of creepers beneath a bamboo
 tree, which may be burned by fire although their roots remain to grow again at the first opportunity.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.15 - Only a rare person who has adopted complete, unalloyed devotional service to K\'e5\'f1\'eba can uproot the weeds of sinful actions with no possibility
 that they will revive. He can do this simply by discharging devotional service, just as the sun can immediately dissipate fog by its rays.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.16 - My dear King, if a sinful person engages in the service of a bona fide devotee of the Lord and thus learns how to dedicate his life unto the lotus feet of K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a, he can be completely purified. One cannot be purified merely by undergoing austerity, penance, brahmacarya and the other methods of atonement I have previously described.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.17 - The path followed by pure devotees, who are well behaved and fully endowed with the best qualifications, is certainly the most auspicious path in this material world. It is free from fear, and it is authorized by the 
\'e7\'e4stras.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.18 - My dear King, as a pot containing liquor cannot be purified even if washed in the waters of many rivers, nondevotees cannot be purified by processes of atonement even if they perform them very well.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.19 - Although not having fully realized K\'e5\'f1\'eba, persons who have even once surre
ndered completely unto His lotus feet and who have become attracted to His name, form, qualities and pastimes are completely freed of all sinful reactions, for they have thus accepted the true method of atonement. Even in dreams, such surrendered souls do
 not see Yamar\'e4ja or his order carriers, who are equipped with ropes to bind the sinful.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.20 - In this regard, learned scholars and saintly persons describe a very old historical incident involving a discussion between the order carriers of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu and those of Yamar\'e4ja. Please hear of this from me.

\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.21 - In the city known as K\'e4nyakubja there was a br\'e4hma\'eba named Aj\'e4mila who married a prostitute maidservant and lost all his brahminical qualities because of the association of that low-class woman.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.22 - This fallen br\'e4hma\'eba, Aj\'e4
mila, gave trouble to others by arresting them, by cheating them in gambling or by directly plundering them. This was the way he earned his livelihood and maintained his wife and children.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.23 - My dear King, while he thus spent his time in abominable, sinful activities to maintain his family of many sons, eighty-eight years of his life passed by.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.24 - That old man Aj\'e4mila had ten sons, of whom the youngest was a baby named N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba. Since N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba was the youngest of all the sons, he was naturally very dear to both his father and his mother.

\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.25 - Because of the child's broken language and awkward movements, old Aj\'e4mila was very much attached to him. He always took care of the child and enjoyed the child's activities.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.26 - When Aj\'e4mila chewed food and ate it, he called the child to chew and eat, and when he drank he called the child to drink also. Always engaged in taking care of the child and calling his name, N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba, Aj\'e4
mila could not understand that his own time was now exhausted and that death was upon him.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.27 - When the time of death arrived for the foolish Aj\'e4mila, he began thinking exclusively of his son N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.28-29 - Aj\'e4mila then saw three awkward persons with deformed bodily features, fierce, twisted faces, and hair standing erect on their bodies. With ropes in their hands, they had come to take him away to the abode of Yamar
\'e4ja. When he saw them he was extremely bewildered, and because of attachment to his child, who was playing a short distance away, Aj\'e4
mila began to call him loudly by his name. Thus with tears in his eyes he somehow or other chanted the holy name of N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.30 - My dear King, the order carriers of Vi\'f1\'ebu, the Vi\'f1\'ebud\'fctas, immediately arrived when they heard the holy name of their master from the mouth of the dying Aj\'e4
mila, who had certainly chanted without offense because he had chanted in complete anxiety.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.31 - The order carriers of Yamar\'e4ja were snatching the soul from the core of the heart of Aj\'e4mila, the husband of the prostitute, but with resounding voices the messengers of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, the Vi\'f1\'ebud\'fc
tas, forbade them to do so.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.32 - When the order carriers of Yamar\'e4ja, the son of the sun-god, were thus forbidden, they replied: Who are you, sirs, that have the audacity to challenge the jurisdiction of Yamar\'e4ja?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.33 - Dear sirs, whose servants are you, where have you come from, and why are you forbidding us to touch the body of Aj\'e4mila? Are you demigods from the
 heavenly planets, are you sub-demigods, or are you the best of devotees?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.34-36 - The order carriers of Yamar\'e4ja said: Your eyes are just like the petals of lotus flowers. Dressed in yellow silken garments, decorated with garlands of lotuses, and w
earing very attractive helmets on your heads and earrings on your ears, you all appear fresh and youthful. Your four long arms are decorated with bows and quivers of arrows and with swords, clubs, conchshells, discs and lotus flowers. Your effulgence has 
dissipated the darkness of this place with extraordinary illumination. Now, sirs, why are you obstructing us?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.37 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: Being thus addressed by the messengers of Yamar\'e4ja, the servants of V\'e4sudeva smiled and spoke the following words in voices as deep as the sound of rumbling clouds.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.38 - The blessed messengers of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, the Vi\'f1\'ebud\'fctas, said: If you are actually servants of Yamar\'e4ja, you must explain to us the meaning of religious principles and the symptoms of irreligion.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.39 - What is the process of punishing others? Who are the actual candidates for punishment? Are all karm\'e9s engaged in fruitive activities punishable, or only some of them?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.40 - The Yamad\'fctas replied: That which is prescribed in the Vedas constitutes dharma, the religious principles, and the opposite of that is irreligion. The Vedas are directly the Supreme Personality of Godhead, N\'e4r\'e4
ya\'eba, and are self-born. This we have heard from Yamar\'e4ja.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.41 - The supreme cause of all causes, N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eb
a, is situated in His own abode in the spiritual world, but nevertheless He controls the entire cosmic manifestation according to the three modes of material nature-sattva-gu\'eba, rajo-gu\'eba and tamo-gu\'eba. In this way all living entities are award
ed different qualities, different names [such as br\'e4hma\'eba, k\'f1atriya and vai\'e7ya], different duties according to the var\'eb\'e4\'e7rama institution, and different forms. Thus N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba is the cause of the entire cosmic manifestation.

\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.42 - The sun, fire, sky, air, demigods, moon, evening, day, night, directions, water, land and Supersoul Himself all witness the activities of the living entity.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.43 - The candidates for punishment are those who are confirmed by these many witnesses to have deviated from th
eir prescribed regulative duties. Everyone engaged in fruitive activities is suitable to be subjected to punishment according to his sinful acts.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.44 - O inhabitants of Vaiku\'eb\'f6ha, you are sinless, but those within this material world are all karm\'e9s,
 whether acting piously or impiously. Both kinds of action are possible for them because they are contaminated by the three modes of nature and must act accordingly. One who has accepted a material body cannot be inactive, and sinful action is inevitable 
for one acting under the modes of material nature. Therefore all the living entities within this material world are punishable.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.45 - In proportion to the extent of one's religious or irreligious actions in this life, one must enjoy or suffer the corresponding reactions of his karma in the next.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.46 - O best of the demigods, we can see three different varieties of life, which are due to the contamination of the three modes of nature. The living entities are thus known as peaceful, restless and 
foolish; as happy, unhappy or in-between; or as religious, irreligious and semireligious. We can deduce that in the next life these three kinds of material nature will similarly act.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.47 - Just as springtime in the present indicates the nature of sp
ringtimes in the past and future, so this life of happiness, distress or a mixture of both gives evidence concerning the religious and irreligious activities of one's past and future lives.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.48 - The omnipotent Yamar\'e4ja is as good as Lord Brahm\'e4, for while situated in his own abode or in everyone's heart like the Param\'e4tm\'e4
, he mentally observes the past activities of a living entity and thus understands how the living entity will act in future lives.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.49 - As a sleeping person acts according t
o the body manifested in his dreams and accepts it to be himself, so one identifies with his present body, which he acquired because of his past religious or irreligious actions, and is unable to know his past or future lives.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.50 - Above the five s
enses of perception, the five working senses and the five objects of the senses is the mind, which is the sixteenth element. Above the mind is the seventeenth element, the soul, the living being himself, who, in cooperation with the other sixteen, enjoys 
the material world alone. The living being enjoys three kinds of situations, namely happy, distressful and mixed.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.51 - The subtle body is endowed with sixteen parts-the five knowledge-acquiring senses, the five working senses, the five objects of se
nse gratification, and the mind. This subtle body is an effect of the three modes of material nature. It is composed of insurmountably strong desires, and therefore it causes the living entity to transmigrate from one body to another in human life, animal
 
life and life as a demigod. When the living entity gets the body of a demigod, he is certainly very jubilant, when he gets a human body he is always in lamentation, and when he gets the body of an animal, he is always afraid. In all conditions, however, h
e is actually miserable. His miserable condition is called sa\'e0s\'e5ti, or transmigration in material life.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.52 - The foolish embodied living entity, inept at controlling his senses and mind, is forced to act according to the influence of the modes of ma
terial nature, against his desires. He is like a silkworm that uses its own saliva to create a cocoon and then becomes trapped in it, with no possibility of getting out. The living entity traps himself in a network of his own fruitive activities and then 
can find no way to release himself. Thus he is always bewildered, and repeatedly he dies.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.53 - Not a single living entity can remain unengaged even for a moment. One must act by his natural tendency according to the three modes of material nature be
cause this natural tendency forcibly makes him work in a particular way.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.54 - The fruitive activities a living being performs, whether pious or impious, are the unseen cause for the fulfillment of his desires. This unseen cause is the root for the 
living entity's different bodies. Because of his intense desire, the living entity takes birth in a particular family and receives a body which is either like that of his mother or like that of his father. The gross and subtle bodies are created according
 to his desire.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 
SB 6.1.55 - Since the living entity is associated with material nature, he is in an awkward position, but if in the human form of life he is taught how to associate with the Supreme Personality of Godhead or His devotee, this position can be overcome.

\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.56-57 - In the beginning this br\'e4hma\'eba named Aj\'e4
mila studied all the Vedic literatures. He was a reservoir of good character, good conduct and good qualities. Firmly established in executing all the Vedic injunctions, he was very mild a
nd gentle, and he kept his mind and senses under control. Furthermore, he was always truthful, he knew how to chant the Vedic mantras, and he was also very pure. Aj\'e4
mila was very respectful to his spiritual master, the fire-god, guests, and the elderly mem
bers of his household. Indeed, he was free from false prestige. He was upright, benevolent to all living entities, and well behaved. He would never speak nonsense or envy anyone.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.58-60 - Once this br\'e4hma\'eba Aj\'e4mila, following the order of his father, went to the forest to collect fruit, flowers and two kinds of grass, called samit and ku\'e7a. On the way home, he came upon a \'e7\'fc
dra, a very lusty, fourth-class man, who was shamelessly embracing and kissing a prostitute. The \'e7\'fcdra was smiling, singing and enjoying as if this were proper behavior. Both the \'e7\'fc
dra and the prostitute were drunk. The prostitute's eyes were rolling in intoxication, and her dress had become loose. Such was the condition in which Aj\'e4mila saw them.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.61 - The \'e7\'fcdra, his arm decorated with turmeric powder, was embracing the prostitute. When Aj\'e4mila saw her, the dormant lusty desires in his heart awakened, and in illusion he fell under their control.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.62 - As far as possible he patiently tried to remember the instructions of the \'e7\'e4
stras not even to see a woman. With the help of this knowledge and his intellect, he tried to control his lusty desires, but because of the force of Cupid within his heart, he failed to control his mind.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.63 - In the same way that the sun and moon are eclipsed by a low planet, the br\'e4hma\'eb
a lost all his good sense. Taking advantage of this situation, he always thought of the prostitute, and within a short time he took her as a servant in his house and abandoned all the regulative principles of a br\'e4hma\'eba.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.64 - Thus Aj\'e4
mila began spending whatever money he had inherited from his father to satisfy the prostitute with various material presentations so that she would remain pleased with him. He gave up all his brahminical activities to satisfy the prostitute.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.65 - Because his intelligence was pierced by the lustful glance of the prostitute, the victimized br\'e4hma\'eba Aj\'e4
mila engaged in sinful acts in her association. He even gave up the company of his very beautiful young wife, who came from a very respectable br\'e4hma\'eba family.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.66 - Although born of a br\'e4hma\'eba family, this rascal, bereft of intelligence because of the prostitute's association, earned money somehow or other, regardless of whether properly or improperly, and used i
t to maintain the prostitute's sons and daughters.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.67 - This br\'e4hma\'eba irresponsibly spent his long lifetime transgressing all the rules and regulations of the holy scripture, living extravagantly and eating food prepared by a prostitute. Therefore 
he is full of sins. He is unclean and is addicted to forbidden activities.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.68 - This man Aj\'e4mila did not undergo atonement. Therefore because of his sinful life, we must take him into the presence of Yamar\'e4ja for punishment. There, according to the
 extent of his sinful acts, he will be punished and thus purified.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }}}{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  The History of the Life of Aj\'e4mila
\par }\pard \s97\ql \fi-605\li720\ri720\nowidctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp20\brdrcf14 \brdrl\brdrs\brdrw45\brsp20\brdrcf13 \tx1080\faauto\rin720\lin720\rtlgutter\itap0 \shading10000\cfpat8 {\fs28\cf2\insrsid10252087   \bullet   }{
\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid10252087\charrsid10252087 \chftn {\footnote\ftnalt \pard\plain \s1217\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\f35\fs24\cf13\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {
\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid5731824\charrsid10252087 \chftn }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.1 - Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Par\'e9k\'f1it said: O my lord, O \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9, you have already described [in the Second Canto] the path of liberation [niv\'e5tti-m
\'e4rga]. By following that path, one is certainly elevated gradually to the highest planetary system, Brahmaloka, from which one is promoted to the spiritual world along with Lord Brahm\'e4
. Thus one's repetition of birth and death in the material world ceases.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.2 - O great sage \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9
, unless the living entity is freed from the infection of the material modes of nature, he receives different types of bodies in which to enjoy or suffer, and according to the body, he is understood to have various inclinations. By following these incl
inations he traverses the path called prav\'e5tti-m\'e4rga, by which one may be elevated to the heavenly planets, as you have already described [in the Third Canto].
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.3 - You have also described [at the end of the Fifth Canto] the varieties of hellish life that result from impious activities, and you have described [in the Fourth Canto] the first manvantara, which was presided over by Sv
\'e4yambhuva Manu, the son of Lord Brahm\'e4.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.4-5 - My dear lord, you have described the dynasties and characteristics of King Priyavrata and King Utt\'e4nap\'e4
da. The Supreme Personality of Godhead created this material world with various universes, planetary systems, planets and stars, with varied lands, seas, oceans, mountains, rivers, gardens and trees, all with different c
haracteristics. These are divided among this planet earth, the luminaries in the sky and the lower planetary systems. You have very clearly described these planets and the living entities who live on them.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.6 - O greatly fortunate and opulent \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9, now kindly tell me how human beings may be saved from having to enter hellish conditions in which they suffer terrible pains.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.7 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 replied: My dear King, if before one's next death whatever impious acts one has performed
 in this life with his mind, words and body are not counteracted through proper atonement according to the description of the Manu-sa\'e0hit\'e4 and other dharma-\'e7\'e4
stras, one will certainly enter the hellish planets after death and undergo terrible suffering, as I have previously described to you.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.8 - Therefore, before one's next death comes, as long as one's body is strong enough, one should quickly adopt the process of atonement according to \'e7\'e4stra; otherwise one's time will be lost, and the reactions o
f his sins will increase. As an expert physician diagnoses and treats a disease according to its gravity, one should undergo atonement according to the severity of one's sins.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.9 - Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Par\'e9k\'f1it said: One may know that sinful activity is injurio
us for him because he actually sees that a criminal is punished by the government and rebuked by people in general and because he hears from scriptures and learned scholars that one is thrown into hellish conditions in the next life for committing sinful 
acts. Nevertheless, in spite of such knowledge, one is forced to commit sins again and again, even after performing acts of atonement. Therefore, what is the value of such atonement?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.10 - Sometimes one who is very alert so as not to commit sinful ac
ts is victimized by sinful life again. I therefore consider this process of repeated sinning and atoning to be useless. It is like the bathing of an elephant, for an elephant cleanses itself by taking a full bath, but then throws dust over its head and bo
dy as soon as it returns to the land.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.11 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9, the son of Vedavy\'e4sa, answered: My dear King, since acts meant to neutralize impious actions are also fruitive, they will not release one from the tendency to act fruitively. Persons who s
ubject themselves to the rules and regulations of atonement are not at all intelligent. Indeed, they are in the mode of darkness. Unless one is freed from the mode of ignorance, trying to counteract one action through another is useless because this will 
not uproot one's desires. Thus even though one may superficially seem pious, he will undoubtedly be prone to act impiously. Therefore real atonement is enlightenment in perfect knowledge, Ved\'e4nta, by which one understands the Supreme Absolute Truth.

\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1
.12 - My dear King, if a diseased person eats the pure, uncontaminated food prescribed by a physician, he is gradually cured, and the infection of disease can no longer touch him. Similarly, if one follows the regulative principles of knowledge, he gradua
lly progresses toward liberation from material contamination.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.13-14 - To concentrate the mind, one must observe a life of celibacy and not fall down. One must undergo the austerity of voluntarily giving up sense enjoyment. One must then control the 
mind and senses, give charity, be truthful, clean and nonviolent, follow the regulative principles and regularly chant the holy name of the Lord. Thus a sober and faithful person who knows the religious principles is temporarily purified of all sins perfo
rmed with his body, words and mind. These sins are like the dried leaves of creepers beneath a bamboo tree, which may be burned by fire although their roots remain to grow again at the first opportunity.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.15 - Only a rare person who has adopted complete, unalloyed devotional service to K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a can uproot the weeds of sinful actions with no possibility that they will revive. He can do this simply by discharging devotional service, just as the sun can immediately dissipate fog by its rays.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.16 - My dear King, if a sinful person engages in the service of a bona fide devotee of the Lord and thus learns how to dedicate his life unto the lotus feet of K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a, he can be completely purified. One cannot be purified merely by undergoing austerity, penance, brahmacarya and the other methods of atonement I have previously described.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.17 - The path followed by pure devotees, who are well behaved and fully endowed with the best qualifications, is certainly the most auspicious path in this material world. It is free from fear, and it is authorized by the 
\'e7\'e4stras.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.18 - My dear King, as a pot containing liquor cannot be purified even if washed in the waters of many rivers, nondevotees cannot be purified by processes of atonement even if they perform them very well.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.19 - Although not having fully realized K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a, persons who have even once surrendered completely unto His lotus feet and who have become attracted to His name, form, qualities and pastimes are completely freed of all sinful reactions,
 for they have thus accepted the true method of atonement. Even in dreams, such surrendered souls do not see Yamar\'e4ja or his order carriers, who are equipped with ropes to bind the sinful.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.20 - In this regard, learned scholars and saintly persons describe a very old historical incident involving a discussion between the order carriers of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu and those of Yamar\'e4ja. Please hear of this from me.

\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.21 - In the city known as K\'e4nyakubja there was a br\'e4hma\'eba named Aj\'e4mila who married a prostitute maidservant and lost all his brahminical qualities because of the association of that low-class woman.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.22 - This fallen br\'e4hma\'eba, Aj\'e4mila, gave trouble to others by arresting them, by cheating them in gambling or by directly plundering them. Th
is was the way he earned his livelihood and maintained his wife and children.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.23 - My dear King, while he thus spent his time in abominable, sinful activities to maintain his family of many sons, eighty-eight years of his life passed by.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.24 - That old man Aj\'e4mila had ten sons, of whom the youngest was a baby named N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba. Since N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba was the youngest of all the sons, he was naturally very dear to both his father and his mother.

\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.25 - Because of the child's broken language and awkward movements, old Aj\'e4mila was very much attached to him. He always took care of the child and enjoyed the child's activities.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.26 - When Aj\'e4mila chewed food and ate it, he called the child to chew and eat, and when he drank he called the child to drink also. Always engaged in taking care of the child and calling his name, N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba, Aj\'e4
mila could not understand that his own time was now exhausted and that death was upon him.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.27 - When the time of death arrived for the foolish Aj\'e4mila, he began thinking exclusively of his son N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.28-29 - Aj\'e4mila then saw three awkward persons with deformed bodily features, fierce, twisted faces, and hair standing erect on their bodies. With ropes in their hands, they had come to take him away to the abode of Yamar
\'e4ja. When he saw them he was extremely bewildered, and because of attachment to his child, who was playing a short distance away, Aj\'e4mila began to call him loudly by his name. Thus with tears in his eyes he somehow or other chanted the ho
ly name of N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.30 - My dear King, the order carriers of Vi\'f1\'ebu, the Vi\'f1\'ebud\'fctas, immediately arrived when they heard the holy name of their master from the mouth of the dying Aj\'e4
mila, who had certainly chanted without offense because he had chanted in complete anxiety.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.31 - The order carriers of Yamar\'e4ja were snatching the soul from the core of the heart of Aj\'e4mila, the husband of the prostitute, but with resounding voices the messengers of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, the Vi\'f1\'ebud\'fc
tas, forbade them to do so.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.32 - When the order carriers of Yamar\'e4ja, the son of the sun-god, were thus forbidden, they replied: Who are you, sirs, that have the audacity to challenge the jurisdiction of Yamar\'e4ja?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.33 - Dear sirs, whose servants are you, where have you come from, and why are you forbidding us to touch the body of Aj\'e4
mila? Are you demigods from the heavenly planets, are you sub-demigods, or are you the best of devotees?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.34-36 - The order carriers of Yamar\'e4ja said: Your eyes are just like the 
petals of lotus flowers. Dressed in yellow silken garments, decorated with garlands of lotuses, and wearing very attractive helmets on your heads and earrings on your ears, you all appear fresh and youthful. Your four long arms are decorated with bows and
 quivers of arrows and with swords, clubs, conchshells, discs and lotus flowers. Your effulgence has dissipated the darkness of this place with extraordinary illumination. Now, sirs, why are you obstructing us?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.37 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: Being thus addressed by the messengers of Yamar\'e4ja, the servants of V\'e4sudeva smiled and spoke the following words in voices as deep as the sound of rumbling clouds.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.38 - The blessed messengers of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, the Vi\'f1\'ebud\'fctas, said: If you are actually servants of Yamar\'e4ja, you must explain to us the meaning of religious principles and the symptoms of irreligion.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.39 - What is the process of punishing others? Who are the actual candidates for punishment? Are all karm\'e9s engaged in fruitive activities punishable, or only some of them?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.40 - The Yamad\'fctas replied: That which is prescribed in the Vedas constitutes dharma, the religious principles, and the opposite of that is irreligion. The Vedas are directly the Supreme Personality of Godhead, N\'e4r\'e4
ya\'eba, and are self-born. This we have heard from Yamar\'e4ja.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.41 - The supreme cause of all causes, N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba, is situated in His own abode in the spiritual world, but nevertheless He controls the entire cosmic manifestation according to the three mod
es of material nature-sattva-gu\'eba, rajo-gu\'eba and tamo-gu\'eba. In this way all living entities are awarded different qualities, different names [such as br\'e4hma\'eba, k\'f1atriya and vai\'e7ya], different duties according to the var\'eb\'e4\'e7
rama institution, and different forms. Thus N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba is the cause of the entire cosmic manifestation.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.42 - The sun, fire, sky, air, demigods, moon, evening, day, night, directions, water, land and Supersoul Himself all witness the activities of the living entity.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.43 - The c
andidates for punishment are those who are confirmed by these many witnesses to have deviated from their prescribed regulative duties. Everyone engaged in fruitive activities is suitable to be subjected to punishment according to his sinful acts.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.44 - O inhabitants of Vaiku\'eb\'f6ha, you are sinless, but those within this material world are all karm\'e9
s, whether acting piously or impiously. Both kinds of action are possible for them because they are contaminated by the three modes of nature and must act ac
cordingly. One who has accepted a material body cannot be inactive, and sinful action is inevitable for one acting under the modes of material nature. Therefore all the living entities within this material world are punishable.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.45 - In proportion to the extent of one's religious or irreligious actions in this life, one must enjoy or suffer the corresponding reactions of his karma in the next.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.46 - O best of the demigods, we can see three different varieties of life, which are due to the cont
amination of the three modes of nature. The living entities are thus known as peaceful, restless and foolish; as happy, unhappy or in-between; or as religious, irreligious and semireligious. We can deduce that in the next life these three kinds of materia
l nature will similarly act.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.47 - Just as springtime in the present indicates the nature of springtimes in the past and future, so this life of happiness, distress or a mixture of both gives evidence concerning the religious and irreligious activiti
es of one's past and future lives.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.48 - The omnipotent Yamar\'e4ja is as good as Lord Brahm\'e4, for while situated in his own abode or in everyone's heart like the Param\'e4tm\'e4, he mentally observes the past activities of a living entity and thus understan
ds how the living entity will act in future lives.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.49 - As a sleeping person acts according to the body manifested in his dreams and accepts it to be himself, so one identifies with his present body, which he acquired because of his past religious 
or irreligious actions, and is unable to know his past or future lives.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.50 - Above the five senses of perception, the five working senses and the five objects of the senses is the mind, which is the sixteenth element. Above the mind is the seventee
nth element, the soul, the living being himself, who, in cooperation with the other sixteen, enjoys the material world alone. The living being enjoys three kinds of situations, namely happy, distressful and mixed.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.51 - The subtle body is endowed wi
th sixteen parts-the five knowledge-acquiring senses, the five working senses, the five objects of sense gratification, and the mind. This subtle body is an effect of the three modes of material nature. It is composed of insurmountably strong desires, and
 
therefore it causes the living entity to transmigrate from one body to another in human life, animal life and life as a demigod. When the living entity gets the body of a demigod, he is certainly very jubilant, when he gets a human body he is always in la
mentation, and when he gets the body of an animal, he is always afraid. In all conditions, however, he is actually miserable. His miserable condition is called sa\'e0s\'e5ti, or transmigration in material life.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.52 - The foolish embodied living entity, ine
pt at controlling his senses and mind, is forced to act according to the influence of the modes of material nature, against his desires. He is like a silkworm that uses its own saliva to create a cocoon and then becomes trapped in it, with no possibility 
of getting out. The living entity traps himself in a network of his own fruitive activities and then can find no way to release himself. Thus he is always bewildered, and repeatedly he dies.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.53 - Not a single living entity can remain unengaged even 
for a moment. One must act by his natural tendency according to the three modes of material nature because this natural tendency forcibly makes him work in a particular way.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.54 - The fruitive activities a living being performs, whether pious or imp
ious, are the unseen cause for the fulfillment of his desires. This unseen cause is the root for the living entity's different bodies. Because of his intense desire, the living entity takes birth in a particular family and receives a body which is either 
like that of his mother or like that of his father. The gross and subtle bodies are created according to his desire.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.55 - Since the living entity is associated with material nature, he is in an awkward position, but if in the human form of life he i
s taught how to associate with the Supreme Personality of Godhead or His devotee, this position can be overcome.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.56-57 - In the beginning this br\'e4hma\'eba named Aj\'e4mila studied all the Vedic literatures. He was a reservoir of good character, good cond
uct and good qualities. Firmly established in executing all the Vedic injunctions, he was very mild and gentle, and he kept his mind and senses under control. Furthermore, he was always truthful, he knew how to chant the Vedic mantras, and he was also ver
y pure. Aj\'e4
mila was very respectful to his spiritual master, the fire-god, guests, and the elderly members of his household. Indeed, he was free from false prestige. He was upright, benevolent to all living entities, and well behaved. He would never speak n
onsense or envy anyone.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.58-60 - Once this br\'e4hma\'eba Aj\'e4mila, following the order of his father, went to the forest to collect fruit, flowers and two kinds of grass, called samit and ku\'e7a. On the way home, he came upon a \'e7\'fc
dra, a very lusty, fourth-class man, who was shamelessly embracing and kissing a prostitute. The \'e7\'fcdra was smiling, singing and enjoying as if this were proper behavior. Both the \'e7\'fc
dra and the prostitute were drunk. The prostitute's eyes were rolling in intoxication, and her dress had become loose. Such was the condition in which Aj\'e4mila saw them.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.61 - The \'e7\'fcdra, his arm decorated with turmeric powder, was embracing the prostitute. When Aj\'e4mila saw her, the dormant lusty desires in his heart awakened, and in illusion he fell under their control.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.62 - As far as possible he patiently tried to remember the instructions of the \'e7\'e4
stras not even to see a woman. With the help of this knowledge and his intellect, he tried to control his lusty desires, but because of the force of Cupid within his heart, he failed to control his mind.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.63 - In the same way that the sun and moon are eclipsed by a low planet, the br\'e4hma\'eba lost all his good sense. Taking advantage of this situation, he always thought of the prostitute, and wi
thin a short time he took her as a servant in his house and abandoned all the regulative principles of a br\'e4hma\'eba.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.64 - Thus Aj\'e4mila began spending whatever money he had inherited from his father to satisfy the prostitute with various material pres
entations so that she would remain pleased with him. He gave up all his brahminical activities to satisfy the prostitute.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.65 - Because his intelligence was pierced by the lustful glance of the prostitute, the victimized br\'e4hma\'eba Aj\'e4mila engaged in 
sinful acts in her association. He even gave up the company of his very beautiful young wife, who came from a very respectable br\'e4hma\'eba family.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.66 - Although born of a br\'e4hma\'eba family, this rascal, bereft of intelligence because of the prostitute's
 association, earned money somehow or other, regardless of whether properly or improperly, and used it to maintain the prostitute's sons and daughters.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.67 - This br\'e4hma\'eba irresponsibly spent his long lifetime transgressing all the rules and regulat
ions of the holy scripture, living extravagantly and eating food prepared by a prostitute. Therefore he is full of sins. He is unclean and is addicted to forbidden activities.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.1.68 - This man Aj\'e4mila did not undergo atonement. Therefore because of his sinful life, we must take him into the presence of Yamar\'e4
ja for punishment. There, according to the extent of his sinful acts, he will be punished and thus purified.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }}}{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 Translations 1-68
\par }\pard \s97\ql \fi-605\li720\ri720\nowidctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp20\brdrcf14 \brdrl\brdrs\brdrw45\brsp20\brdrcf13 \tx1080\faauto\outlinelevel0\rin720\lin720\rtlgutter\itap0\pararsid10252087 \shading10000\cfpat16 {
\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid10252087\charrsid10252087 \chftn {\footnote\ftnalt \pard\plain \s1217\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \shading10000\cfpat16 \b\f35\fs24\cf13\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {
\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid5731824\charrsid10252087 \chftn }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.1 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 said: My dear King, the servants of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu are always
 very expert in logic and arguments. After hearing the statements of the Yamad\'fctas, they replied as follows.
\par }\pard \s1217\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.2 - The Vi\'f1\'ebud\'fctas said: Alas, how painful it is that irreligion is being introduced into an assembly where religion should be maintained. 
Indeed, those in charge of maintaining the religious principles are needlessly punishing a sinless, unpunishable person.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.3 - A king or governmental official should be so well qualified that he acts as a father, maintainer and protector of the citiz
ens because of affection and love. He should give the citizens good advice and instructions according to the standard scriptures and should be equal to everyone. Yamar\'e4
ja does this, for he is the supreme master of justice, and so do those who follow in his
 footsteps. However, if such persons become polluted and exhibit partiality by punishing an innocent, blameless person, where will the citizens go to take shelter for their maintenance and security?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.4 - The mass of people follow the example of a leader in society and imitate his behavior. They accept as evidence whatever the leader accepts.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.5-6 - People in general are not very advanced in knowledge by which to discriminate between religion and irreligion. The innocent, unenlightened citizen 
is like an ignorant animal sleeping in peace with its head on the lap of its master, faithfully believing in the master's protection. If a leader is actually kindhearted and deserves to be the object of a living entity's faith, how can he punish or kill a
 foolish person who has fully surrendered in good faith and friendship?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.7 - Aj\'e4mila has already atoned for all his sinful actions. Indeed, he has atoned not only for sins performed in one life but for those performed in millions of lives, for in a h
elpless condition he chanted the holy name of N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba. Even though he did not chant purely, he chanted without offense, and therefore he is now pure and eligible for liberation.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.8 - The Vi\'f1\'ebud\'fctas continued: Even previously, while eating and at other times, this Aj\'e4mila would call his son, saying, "My dear N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eb
a, please come here." Although calling the name of his son, he nevertheless uttered the four syllables n\'e4-r\'e4-ya-\'eba. Simply by chanting the name of N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba in this way, he sufficiently ato
ned for the sinful reactions of millions of lives.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.9-10 - The chanting of the holy name of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu is the best process of atonement for a thief of gold or other valuables, for a drunkard, for one who betrays a friend or relative, for one who kills a br\'e4hma\'eb
a, or for one who indulges in sex with the wife of his guru or another superior. It is also the best method of atonement for one who murders women, the king or his father, for one who slaughters cows, and for all other sinful men. Simply by cha
nting the holy name of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, such sinful persons may attract the attention of the Supreme Lord, who therefore considers, "Because this man has chanted My holy name, My duty is to give him protection."
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.11 - By following the Vedic ritualistic c
eremonies or undergoing atonement, sinful men do not become as purified as by chanting once the holy name of Lord Hari. Although ritualistic atonement may free one from sinful reactions, it does not awaken devotional service, unlike the chanting of the Lo
rd's names, which reminds one of the Lord's fame, qualities, attributes, pastimes and paraphernalia.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.12 - The ritualistic ceremonies of atonement recommended in the religious scriptures are insufficient to cleanse the heart absolutely because after 
atonement one's mind again runs toward material activities. Consequently, for one who wants liberation from the fruitive reactions of material activities, the chanting of the Hare K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a mantra, or glorification of the name, fame and pastimes of the Lord, is recommended as the most perfect process of atonement because such chanting eradicates the dirt from one's heart completely.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.13 - At the time of death, this Aj\'e4mila helplessly and very loudly chanted the holy name of the Lord, N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba. That chant
ing alone has already freed him from the reactions of all sinful life. Therefore, O servants of Yamar\'e4ja, do not try to take him to your master for punishment in hellish conditions.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.14 - One who chants the holy name of the Lord is immediately freed
 from the reactions of unlimited sins, even if he chants indirectly [to indicate something else], jokingly, for musical entertainment, or even neglectfully. This is accepted by all the learned scholars of the scriptures.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.15 - If one chants the holy
 name of Hari and then dies because of an accidental misfortune, such as falling from the top of a house, slipping and suffering broken bones while traveling on the road, being bitten by a serpent, being afflicted with pain and high fever, or being injure
d by a weapon, one is immediately absolved from having to enter hellish life, even though he is sinful.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.16 - Authorities who are learned scholars and sages have carefully ascertained that one should atone for the heaviest sins by undergoing a heavy 
process of atonement and one should atone for lighter sins by undergoing lighter atonement. Chanting the Hare K\'e5\'f1\'eba mantra, however, vanquishes all the effects of sinful activities, regardless of whether heavy or light.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.17 - Although one may neutra
lize the reactions of sinful life through austerity, charity, vows and other such methods, these pious activities cannot uproot the material desires in one's heart. However, if one serves the lotus feet of the Personality of Godhead, he is immediately fre
ed from all such contaminations.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.18 - As a fire burns dry grass to ashes, so the holy name of the Lord, whether chanted knowingly or unknowingly, burns to ashes, without fail, all the reactions of one's sinful activities.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.19 - If a person un
aware of the effective potency of a certain medicine takes that medicine or is forced to take it, it will act even without his knowledge because its potency does not depend on the patient's understanding. Similarly, even though one does not know the value
 of chanting the holy name of the Lord, if one chants knowingly or unknowingly, the chanting will be very effective.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.20 - \'c7r\'e9 \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: My dear King, having thus perfectly judged the principles of devotional service with reasoning and arguments, the order carriers of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu released the br\'e4hma
\'eba Aj\'e4mila from the bondage of the Yamad\'fctas and saved him from imminent death.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.21 - My dear Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Par\'e9k\'f1it, O subduer of all enemies, after the servants of Yamar\'e4ja had been answered by the order carriers of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, they went to Yamar\'e4
ja and explained to him everything that had happened.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.22 - Having been released from the nooses of Yamar\'e4ja's servants, the br\'e4hma\'eba Aj\'e4mila, now free from fear, came to his senses and immediately offered obeisances to the Vi\'f1\'ebud\'fc
tas by bowing his head at their lotus feet. He was extremely pleased by their presence, for he had seen them save his life from the hands of the servants of Yamar\'e4ja.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.23 - O sinless Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Par\'e9k\'f1it, the order carriers of the Supreme Personality of Godhead, the Vi\'f1\'ebud\'fctas, saw that Aj\'e4
mila was attempting to say something, and thus they suddenly disappeared from his presence.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.24-25 - After hearing the discourses between the Yamad\'fctas and the Vi\'f1\'ebud\'fctas, Aj\'e4
mila could understand the religious principles that act under the three modes of material nature. These principles are mentioned in the three Vedas. He could also understand the transcendental religious principles, which are above the modes of mat
erial nature and which concern the relationship between the living being and the Supreme Personality of Godhead. Furthermore, Aj\'e4
mila heard glorification of the name, fame, qualities and pastimes of the Supreme Personality of Godhead. He thus became a perfectly pure devotee. He could then remember his past sinful activities, which he greatly regretted having performed.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.26 - Aj\'e4mila said: Alas, being a servant of my senses, how degraded I became! I fell down from my position as a duly qualified br\'e4hma\'eba and begot children in the womb of a prostitute.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.27 - Alas, all condemnation upon me! I acted so sinfully that I degraded my family tradition. Indeed, I gave up my chaste and beautiful young wife to have sexual intercourse with a fallen prostitu
te accustomed to drinking wine. All condemnation upon me!
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.28 - My father and mother were old and had no other son or friend to look after them. Because I did not take care of them, they lived with great difficulty. Alas, like an abominable lower-cl
ass man, I ungratefully left them in that condition.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.29 - It is now clear that as a consequence of such activities, a sinful person like me must be thrown into hellish conditions meant for those who have broken religious principles and must there suffer extreme miseries.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.30 - Was this a dream I saw, or was it reality? I saw fearsome men with ropes in their hands coming to arrest me and drag me away. Where have they gone?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.31 - And where have those four liberated and very beautiful persons gone who released me from arrest and saved me from being dragged down to the hellish regions?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.32 - I am certainly most abominable and unfortunate to have merged in an ocean of sinful activities, but nevertheless, because of my previous spiritu
al activities, I could see those four exalted personalities who came to rescue me. Now I feel exceedingly happy because of their visit.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.33 - Were it not for my past devotional service, how could I, a most unclean keeper of a prostitute, have gotten an opportunity to chant the holy name of Vaiku\'eb\'f6
hapati when I was just ready to die? Certainly it could not have been possible.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.34 - Aj\'e4mila continued: I am a shameless cheater who has killed his brahminical culture. Indeed, I am sin personified. Where am I in comparison to the all-auspicious chanting of the holy name of Lord N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba?

\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.35 - I am such a sinful person, but since I have now gotten this opportunity, I must completely control my mind, life and senses and always engage in devotio
nal service so that I may not fall again into the deep darkness and ignorance of material life.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.36-37 - Because of identifying oneself with the body, one is subjected to desires for sense gratification, and thus one engages in many different types 
of pious and impious action. This is what constitutes material bondage. Now I shall disentangle myself from my material bondage, which has been caused by the Supreme Personality of Godhead's illusory energy in the form of a woman. Being a most fallen soul
,
 I was victimized by the illusory energy and have become like a dancing dog led around by a woman's hand. Now I shall give up all lusty desires and free myself from this illusion. I shall become a merciful, well-wishing friend to all living entities and a
lways absorb myself in K\'e5\'f1\'eba consciousness.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.38 - Simply because I chanted the holy name of the Lord in the association of devotees, my heart is now becoming purified. Therefore I shall not fall victim again to the false lures of material sense grati
fication. Now that I have become fixed in the Absolute Truth, henceforward I shall not identify myself with the body. I shall give up false conceptions of "I" and "mine" and fix my mind on the lotus feet of K\'e5\'f1\'eba.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.39 - Because of a moment's association with devotees [the Vi\'f1\'ebud\'fctas], Aj\'e4
mila detached himself from the material conception of life with determination. Thus freed from all material attraction, he immediately started for Hardwar.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.40 - In Hardwar, Aj\'e4mila took shelter at a Vi\'f1\'ebu temple, where he executed the process of bhakti-yoga. He controlled his senses and fully applied his mind in the service of the Lord.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.41 - Aj\'e4mila fully engaged in devotional service. Thus he detached his mind from the process of sense gratification and became fully absorbed in thinking of the form of the Lord.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.42 - When his intelligence and mind were fixed upon the form of the Lord, the br\'e4hma\'eba Aj\'e4mila once again saw before him four celestial persons. He could understand that they were those 
he had seen previously, and thus he offered them his obeisances by bowing down before them.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.43 - Upon seeing the Vi\'f1\'ebud\'fctas, Aj\'e4mila gave up his material body at Hardwar on the bank of the Ganges. He regained his original spiritual body, which was a body appropriate for an associate of the Lord.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.44 - Accompanied by the order carriers of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, Aj\'e4mila boarded an airplane made of gold. Passing through the airways, he went directly to the abode of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, the husband of the goddess of fortune.

\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.45 - Aj\'e4mila was a br\'e4hma\'eb
a who because of bad association had given up all brahminical culture and religious principles. Becoming most fallen, he stole, drank and performed other abominable acts. He even kept a prostitute. Thus he was de
stined to be carried away to hell by the order carriers of Yamar\'e4ja, but he was immediately rescued simply by a glimpse of the chanting of the holy name N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.46 - Therefore one who desires freedom from material bondage should adopt the process
 of chanting and glorifying the name, fame, form and pastimes of the Supreme Personality of Godhead, at whose feet all the holy places stand. One cannot derive the proper benefit from other methods, such as pious atonement, speculative knowledge and medit
ation in mystic yoga, because even after following such methods one takes to fruitive activities again, unable to control his mind, which is contaminated by the base qualities of nature, namely passion and ignorance.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.47-48 - Because this very confid
ential historical narration has the potency to vanquish all sinful reactions, one who hears or describes it with faith and devotion is no longer doomed to hellish life, regardless of his having a material body and regardless of how sinful he may have been
. Indeed, the Yamad\'fctas, who carry out the orders of Yamar\'e4ja, do not approach him even to see him. After giving up his body, he returns home, back to Godhead, where he is very respectfully received and worshiped.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.2.49 - While suffering at the time of death, Aj\'e4
mila chanted the holy name of the Lord, and although the chanting was directed toward his son, he nevertheless returned home, back to Godhead. Therefore if one faithfully and inoffensively chants the holy name of the Lord, where is the doubt tha
t he will return to Godhead?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }}}{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  2. }{\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid10252087\charrsid10252087 \chftn {\footnote\ftnalt \pard\plain \s1217\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 
