\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.11 - The path of goodness traversed by your father, grandfather and great-grandfathers is that of maintaining the subjects [praj\'e4
s], including the men, animals and trees. That is the path you should follow. Unnecessary anger is contrary to your duty. Therefore I request you to control your anger.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.12 - As the father and mother are the friends and maintainers of their children, as the eyelid is the protector of the eye, as the husband is the maintainer and protector of a woman, as the ho
useholder is the maintainer and protector of beggars, and as the learned is the friend of the ignorant, so the king is the protector and giver of life to all his subjects. The trees are also subjects of the king. Therefore they should be given protection.

\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 
SB 6.4.13 - The Supreme Personality of Godhead is situated as the Supersoul within the cores of the hearts of all living entities, whether moving or nonmoving, including men, birds, animals, trees and, indeed, all living entities. Therefore you should con
sider every body a residence or temple of the Lord. By such vision you will satisfy the Lord. You should not angrily kill these living entities in the forms of trees.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.14 - One who inquires into self-realization and thus subdues his powerful anger-which awakens suddenly in the body as if falling from the sky-transcends the influence of the modes of material nature.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.15 - There is no need to burn these poor trees any longer. Let whatever trees still remain be happy. Indeed, you should also be happy. Now, here is a beautiful, well-qualified girl named M\'e4ri\'f1\'e4
, who was raised by the trees as their daughter. You may accept this beautiful girl as your wife.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.16 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: My dear King, after thus pacifying the Pracet\'e4s, Soma, the king of the moon, gave them the beautiful girl born of Pramloc\'e4 Apsar\'e4. The Pracet\'e4s all received Pramloc\'e4
's daughter, who had high, very beautiful hips, and married her according to the religious system.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.17 - In the womb of that girl the Pracet\'e4s all begot a son named Dak\'f1a, who filled the three worlds with living entities.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.18 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: Please hear from me with great attention how Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a, who was very affectionate to his daughters, created different types 
of living entities through his semen and through his mind.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.19 - With his mind, Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a first created all kinds of demigods, demons, human beings, birds, beasts, aquatics and so on.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.20 - But when Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a saw that he was not properly generating all kinds of living entities, he approached a mountain near the Vindhya mountain range, and there he executed very difficult austerities.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.21 - Near that mountain was a very holy place named Aghamar\'f1a\'eba. There Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a exec
uted ritualistic ceremonies and satisfied the Supreme Personality of Godhead, Hari, by engaging in great austerities to please Him.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.22 - My dear King, I shall fully explain to you the Ha\'e0sa-guhya prayers, which were offered to the Supreme Personality of Godhead by Dak\'f1a, and I shall explain how the Lord was pleased with him for those prayers.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.23 - Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a said: The Supreme Personality of Godhead is transcendental to the illusory energy and the physical categories it produces. He poss
esses the potency for unfailing knowledge and supreme willpower, and He is the controller of the living entities and the illusory energy. The conditioned souls who have accepted this material manifestation as everything cannot see Him, for He is above the
 evidence of experimental knowledge. Self-evident and self-sufficient, He is not caused by any superior cause. Let me offer my respectful obeisances unto Him.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.24 - As the sense objects [form, taste, touch, smell and sound] cannot understand how the 
senses perceive them, so the conditioned soul, although residing in his body along with the Supersoul, cannot understand how the supreme spiritual person, the master of the material creation, directs his senses. Let me offer my respectful obeisances unto 
that Supreme Person, who is the supreme controller.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.25 - Because they are only matter, the body, the life airs, the external and internal senses, the five gross elements and the subtle sense objects [form, taste, smell, sound and touch] cannot know 
their own nature, the nature of the other senses or the nature of their controllers. But the living being, because of his spiritual nature, can know his body, the life airs, the senses, the elements and the sense objects, and he can also know the three qu
alities that form their roots. Nevertheless, although the living being is completely aware of them, he is unable to see the Supreme Being, who is omniscient and unlimited. I therefore offer my respectful obeisances unto Him.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.26 - When one's consciousness is completely purified of the contamination of material existence, gross and subtle, without being agitated as in the working and dreaming states, and when the mind is not dissolved as in su\'f1
upti, deep sleep, one comes to the platform of trance. Then
 one's material vision and the memories of the mind, which manifests names and forms, are vanquished. Only in such a trance is the Supreme Personality of Godhead revealed. Thus let us offer our respectful obeisances unto the Supreme Personality of Godhead
, who is seen in that uncontaminated, transcendental state.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.27-28 - Just as great learned br\'e4hma\'ebas who are expert in performing ritualistic ceremonies and sacrifices can extract the fire dormant within wooden fuel by chanting the fifteen S\'e4midhen\'e9 
mantras, thus proving the efficacy of the Vedic mantras, so those who are actually advanced in consciousness-in other words, those who are K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a conscious-can find the Supersoul, who by His own spiritual potency is situated within the heart. The heart is c
overed by the three modes of material nature and the nine material elements [material nature, the total material energy, the ego, the mind and the five objects of sense gratification], and also by the five material elements and the ten senses. These twent
y-seven elements constitute the external energy of the Lord. Great yog\'e9s meditate upon the Lord, who is situated as the Supersoul, Param\'e4tm\'e4
, within the core of the heart. May that Supersoul be pleased with me. The Supersoul is realized when one is eager fo
r liberation from the unlimited varieties of material life. One actually attains such liberation when he engages in the transcendental loving service of the Lord and realizes the Lord because of his attitude of service. The Lord may be addressed by variou
s spiritual names, which are inconceivable to the material senses. When will that Supreme Personality of Godhead be pleased with me?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.29 - Anything expressed by material vibrations, anything ascertained by material intelligence and anything experienc
ed by the material senses or concocted within the material mind is but an effect of the modes of material nature and therefore has nothing to do with the real nature of the Supreme Personality of Godhead. The Supreme Lord is beyond the creation of this ma
terial world, for He is the source of the material qualities and creation. As the cause of all causes, He exists before the creation and after the creation. I wish to offer my respectful obeisances unto Him.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.30 - The Supreme Brahman, K\'e5\'f1\'eba, is the u
ltimate resting place and source of everything. Everything is done by Him, everything belongs to Him, and everything is offered to Him. He is the ultimate objective, and whether acting or causing others to act, He is the ultimate doer. There are many caus
es, high and low, but since He is the cause of all causes, He is well known as the Supreme Brahman who existed before all activities. He is one without a second and has no other cause. I therefore offer my respects unto Him.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.31 - Let me offer my res
pectful obeisances unto the all-pervading Supreme Personality of Godhead, who possesses unlimited transcendental qualities. Acting from within the cores of the hearts of all philosophers, who propagate various views, He causes them to forget their own sou
ls while sometimes agreeing and sometimes disagreeing among themselves. Thus He creates within this material world a situation in which they are unable to come to a conclusion. I offer my obeisances unto Him.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.32 - There are two parties-namely, the theists and the atheists. The theist, who accepts the Supersoul, finds the spiritual cause through mystic yoga. The S\'e4\'ec
khyite, however, who merely analyzes the material elements, comes to a conclusion of impersonalism and does not accept a supreme cause-whether Bhagav\'e4n, Param\'e4tm\'e4
 or even Brahman. Instead, he is preoccupied with the superfluous, external activities of material nature. Ultimately, however, both parties demonstrate the Absolute Truth because although they offer opposing statements, their objec
t is the same ultimate cause. They are both approaching the same Supreme Brahman, to whom I offer my respectful obeisances.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.33 - The Supreme Personality of Godhead, who is inconceivably opulent, who is devoid of all material names, forms and pastim
es, and who is all-pervading, is especially merciful to the devotees who worship His lotus feet. Thus He exhibits transcendental forms and names with His different pastimes. May that Supreme Personality of Godhead, whose form is eternal and full of knowle
dge and bliss, be merciful to me.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.34 - As the air carries various characteristics of the physical elements, like the aroma of a flower or colors resulting from a mixture of dust in the air, the Lord appears through lower systems of worship according
 to one's desires, although He appears as the demigods and not in His original form. What is the use of these other forms? May the original Supreme Personality of Godhead please fulfill my desires.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.35-39 - \'c7r\'e9 \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 said: The Supreme Personality of Godhead, Hari, who is extremely affectionate to His devotees, was very pleased by the prayers offered by Dak\'f1
a, and thus He appeared at that holy place known as Aghamar\'f1a\'eba. O Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Par\'e9k\'f1it, best of the Kuru dynasty, the Lord's lotus feet rested on the shoulders of His carrier, Garu\'f2
a, and He appeared with eight long, mighty, very beautiful arms. In His hands He held a disc, conchshell, sword, shield, arrow, bow, rope and club-in each hand a different weapon, all brilliantly shining. His gar
ments were yellow and His bodily hue deep bluish. His eyes and face were very cheerful, and from His neck to His feet hung a long garland of flowers. His chest was decorated with the Kaustubha jewel and the mark of \'c7r\'e9
vatsa. On His head was a gorgeous roun
d helmet, and His ears were decorated with earrings resembling sharks. All these ornaments were uncommonly beautiful. The Lord wore a golden belt on His waist, bracelets on His arms, rings on His fingers, and ankle bells on His feet. Thus decorated by var
ious ornaments, Lord Hari, who is attractive to all the living entities of the three worlds, is known as Puru\'f1ottama, the best personality. He was accompanied by great devotees like N\'e4
rada, Nanda and all the principal demigods, led by the heavenly king, Indra, and the residents of various upper planetary systems such as Siddhaloka, Gandharvaloka and C\'e4ra\'eb
aloka. Situated on both sides of the Lord and behind Him as well, these devotees offered Him prayers continuously.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.40 - Seeing that wonderful and effulgent form of the Supreme Personality of Godhead, Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a was first somewhat afraid, but then he was very pleased to see the Lord, and he fell to the ground like a stick [da
\'eb\'f2avat] to offer his respects to the Lord.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.41 - As rivers are filled by water flowing from a mountain, all of Dak\'f1a's senses were filled with pleasure. Because of his highly elevated happiness, Dak\'f1
a could not say anything, but simply remained flat on the ground.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.42 - Although Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a could not say anything, when the Lord, who knows everyone's heart, saw His devotee prostrate in that manner and desiring to increase the population, He addressed him as follows.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.43 - The Supreme Personality of Godhead said: O most fortunate Pr\'e4cetasa, because of your
 great faith in Me, you have attained the supreme devotional ecstasy. Indeed, because of your austerities, combined with exalted devotion, your life is now successful. You have achieved complete perfection.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.44 - My dear Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a, you have pe
rformed extreme austerities for the welfare and growth of the world. My desire also is that everyone within this world be happy. I am therefore very pleased with you because you are endeavoring to fulfill My desire for the welfare of the entire world.

\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.45 - Lord Brahm\'e4, Lord \'c7iva, the Manus, all the other demigods in the higher planetary systems, and you praj\'e4
patis, who are increasing the population, are working for the benefit of all living entities. Thus you expansions of My marginal energy are incarnations of My various qualities.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.46 - My dear br\'e4hma\'eba, austerity in the form of meditation is My heart, Vedic knowledge in the form of hymns and mantras constitutes My body, and spiritual activities and ecstatic emotions are My actual form. The 
ritualistic ceremonies and sacrifices, when properly conducted, are the various limbs of My body, the unseen good fortune proceeding from pious or spiritual activities constitutes My mind, and the demigods who execute My orders in various departments are 
My life and soul.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.47 - Before the creation of this cosmic manifestation, I alone existed with My specific spiritual potencies. Consciousness was then unmanifested, just as one's consciousness is unmanifested during the time of sleep.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.48 - I 
am the reservoir of unlimited potency, and therefore I am known as unlimited or all-pervading. From My material energy the cosmic manifestation appeared within Me, and in this universal manifestation appeared the chief being, Lord Brahm\'e4
, who is your source and is not born of a material mother.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.49-50 - When the chief lord of the universe, Lord Brahm\'e4 [Svayambh\'fc], having been inspired by My energy, was attempting to create, he thought himself incapable. Therefore I gave him advice, and in accordance 
with My instructions he underwent extremely difficult austerities. Because of these austerities, the great Lord Brahm\'e4 was able to create nine personalities, including you, to help him in the functions of creation.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.51 - O My dear son Dak\'f1a, Praj\'e4pati Pa\'efcajana has a daughter named Asikn\'e9, whom I offer to you so that you may accept her as your wife.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.52 - Now unite in sexual life as man and woman, and in this way, by sexual intercourse, you will be able to beget hundreds of children in the womb of this girl to increase the population.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.53 - After you give birth to many hundreds and thousands of children, they will also be captivated by My illusory energy and will engage, like You, in sexual intercourse. But because of My mercy to you and
 them, they will also be able to give Me presentations in devotion.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.54 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: After the creator of the entire universe, the Supreme Personality of Godhead, Hari, had spoken in this way in the presence of Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a, He i
mmediately disappeared as if He were an object experienced in a dream.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }}}{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  }{\f35\fs28\cf2\insrsid10252087 The Ha\'e0sa-guhya Prayers
\par }\pard \s97\ql \fi-605\li720\ri720\nowidctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp20\brdrcf14 \brdrl\brdrs\brdrw45\brsp20\brdrcf13 \tx1080\faauto\rin720\lin720\rtlgutter\itap0 \shading10000\cfpat8 {\fs28\cf2\insrsid10252087   \bullet   }{
\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid10252087\charrsid10252087 \chftn {\footnote\ftnalt \pard\plain \s1217\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\f35\fs24\cf13\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {
\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid5731824\charrsid10252087 \chftn }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.1-2 - The blessed King said to \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9: My dear lord, the demigods, demons, human beings, N\'e4
gas, beasts and birds were created during the reign of Sv\'e4yambhuva Manu. Y
ou have spoken about this creation briefly [in the Third Canto]. Now I wish to know about it elaborately. I also wish to know about the potency of the Supreme Personality of Godhead by which He brought about the secondary creation.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.3 - S\'fcta Gosv\'e4m\'e9 said: O great sages [assembled at Naimi\'f1\'e4ra\'ebya], after the great yogi \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 heard King Par\'e9k\'f1it's inquiry, he praised it and thus replied.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.4 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 said: When the ten sons of Pr\'e4c\'e9nabarhi emerged from the waters, in which they were performing austerities, they saw that the entire surface of the world was covered by trees.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.5 - Because of having undergone long austerities in the water, the Pracet\'e4s were very angry at the trees. Desiring to burn them to ashes, they generated wind and fire from their mouths.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.6 - My dear King Par\'e9k\'f1it, when Soma, the king of the trees and predominating deity of the moon, saw the fire and wind burning all the trees to ashes, he felt great sympathy because he is the maintainer of all 
herbs and trees. To appease the anger of the Pracet\'e4s, Soma spoke as follows.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.7 - O greatly fortunate ones, you should not kill these poor trees by burning them to ashes. Your duty is to wish the citizens [praj\'e4s] all prosperity and to act as their protectors.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.8 - The Supreme Personality of Godhead, \'c7r\'e9 Hari, is the master of all living entities, including all the praj\'e4patis, such as Lord Brahm\'e4
. Because He is the all-pervading and indestructible master, He has created all these trees and vegetables as eatables for other living entities.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.9 - By nature's arrangement, fruits and flowers are considered the food of insects and birds; grass and other legless living entities are meant to be the food of four-legged animals like cows and buff
alo; animals that cannot use their front legs as hands are meant to be the food of animals like tigers, which have claws; and four-legged animals like deer and goats, as well as food grains, are meant to be the food of human beings.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.10 - O pure-hearted ones, your father, Pr\'e4c\'e9
nabarhi, and the Supreme Personality of Godhead have ordered you to generate population. Therefore how can you burn to ashes these trees and herbs, which are needed for the maintenance of your subjects and descendants?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.11 - The path of goodness traversed by your father, grandfather and great-grandfathers is that of maintaining the subjects [praj\'e4
s], including the men, animals and trees. That is the path you should follow. Unnecessary anger is contrary to your duty. Therefore I request you to control your anger.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.12 - As the father and mother are the friends and maintainers of their children, as the eyelid is the protector of the eye, as the husband is the maintainer and protector of a woman, as the householder is t
he maintainer and protector of beggars, and as the learned is the friend of the ignorant, so the king is the protector and giver of life to all his subjects. The trees are also subjects of the king. Therefore they should be given protection.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.13 - T
he Supreme Personality of Godhead is situated as the Supersoul within the cores of the hearts of all living entities, whether moving or nonmoving, including men, birds, animals, trees and, indeed, all living entities. Therefore you should consider every b
ody a residence or temple of the Lord. By such vision you will satisfy the Lord. You should not angrily kill these living entities in the forms of trees.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.14 - One who inquires into self-realization and thus subdues his powerful anger-which awakens suddenly in the body as if falling from the sky-transcends the influence of the modes of material nature.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.15 - There is no need to burn these poor trees any longer. Let whatever trees still remain be happy. Indeed, you should also be happy. Now, here is a beautiful, well-qualified girl named M\'e4ri\'f1\'e4
, who was raised by the trees as their daughter. You may accept this beautiful girl as your wife.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.16 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: My dear King, after thus pacifying the Pracet\'e4s, Soma, the king of the moon, gave them the beautiful girl born of Pramloc\'e4 Apsar\'e4. The Pracet\'e4s all received Pramloc\'e4
's daughter, who had high, very beautiful hips, and married her according to the religious system.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.17 - In the womb of that girl the Pracet\'e4s all begot a son named Dak\'f1a, who filled the three worlds with living entities.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.18 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: Please hear from me with great attention how Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a, who was very affectionate to his daughters, created different types of living enti
ties through his semen and through his mind.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.19 - With his mind, Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a first created all kinds of demigods, demons, human beings, birds, beasts, aquatics and so on.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.20 - But when Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a saw that he was not properly generating all kinds of living entities, he approached a mountain near the Vindhya mountain range, and there he executed very difficult austerities.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.21 - Near that mountain was a very holy place named Aghamar\'f1a\'eba. There Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a executed ritualist
ic ceremonies and satisfied the Supreme Personality of Godhead, Hari, by engaging in great austerities to please Him.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.22 - My dear King, I shall fully explain to you the Ha\'e0sa-guhya prayers, which were offered to the Supreme Personality of Godhead by Dak\'f1a, and I shall explain how the Lord was pleased with him for those prayers.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.23 - Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a said: The Supreme Personality of Godhead is transcendental to the illusory energy and the physical categories it produces. He possesses the pote
ncy for unfailing knowledge and supreme willpower, and He is the controller of the living entities and the illusory energy. The conditioned souls who have accepted this material manifestation as everything cannot see Him, for He is above the evidence of e
xperimental knowledge. Self-evident and self-sufficient, He is not caused by any superior cause. Let me offer my respectful obeisances unto Him.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.24 - As the sense objects [form, taste, touch, smell and sound] cannot understand how the senses perceiv
e them, so the conditioned soul, although residing in his body along with the Supersoul, cannot understand how the supreme spiritual person, the master of the material creation, directs his senses. Let me offer my respectful obeisances unto that Supreme P
erson, who is the supreme controller.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.25 - Because they are only matter, the body, the life airs, the external and internal senses, the five gross elements and the subtle sense objects [form, taste, smell, sound and touch] cannot know their own natu
re, the nature of the other senses or the nature of their controllers. But the living being, because of his spiritual nature, can know his body, the life airs, the senses, the elements and the sense objects, and he can also know the three qualities that f
orm their roots. Nevertheless, although the living being is completely aware of them, he is unable to see the Supreme Being, who is omniscient and unlimited. I therefore offer my respectful obeisances unto Him.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.26 - When one's consciousness is completely purified of the contamination of material existence, gross and subtle, without being agitated as in the working and dreaming states, and when the mind is not dissolved as in su\'f1
upti, deep sleep, one comes to the platform of trance. Then one's materia
l vision and the memories of the mind, which manifests names and forms, are vanquished. Only in such a trance is the Supreme Personality of Godhead revealed. Thus let us offer our respectful obeisances unto the Supreme Personality of Godhead, who is seen 
in that uncontaminated, transcendental state.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.27-28 - Just as great learned br\'e4hma\'ebas who are expert in performing ritualistic ceremonies and sacrifices can extract the fire dormant within wooden fuel by chanting the fifteen S\'e4midhen\'e9 mantras, thus 
proving the efficacy of the Vedic mantras, so those who are actually advanced in consciousness-in other words, those who are K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a conscious-can find the Supersoul, who by His own spiritual potency is situated within the heart. The heart is covered by the 
three modes of material nature and the nine material elements [material nature, the total material energy, the ego, the mind and the five objects of sense gratification], and also by the five material elements and the ten senses. These twenty-seven elemen
ts constitute the external energy of the Lord. Great yog\'e9s meditate upon the Lord, who is situated as the Supersoul, Param\'e4tm\'e4
, within the core of the heart. May that Supersoul be pleased with me. The Supersoul is realized when one is eager for liberation f
rom the unlimited varieties of material life. One actually attains such liberation when he engages in the transcendental loving service of the Lord and realizes the Lord because of his attitude of service. The Lord may be addressed by various spiritual na
mes, which are inconceivable to the material senses. When will that Supreme Personality of Godhead be pleased with me?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.29 - Anything expressed by material vibrations, anything ascertained by material intelligence and anything experienced by the mate
rial senses or concocted within the material mind is but an effect of the modes of material nature and therefore has nothing to do with the real nature of the Supreme Personality of Godhead. The Supreme Lord is beyond the creation of this material world, 
for He is the source of the material qualities and creation. As the cause of all causes, He exists before the creation and after the creation. I wish to offer my respectful obeisances unto Him.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.30 - The Supreme Brahman, K\'e5\'f1\'eba, is the ultimate restin
g place and source of everything. Everything is done by Him, everything belongs to Him, and everything is offered to Him. He is the ultimate objective, and whether acting or causing others to act, He is the ultimate doer. There are many causes, high and l
ow, but since He is the cause of all causes, He is well known as the Supreme Brahman who existed before all activities. He is one without a second and has no other cause. I therefore offer my respects unto Him.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.31 - Let me offer my respectful obeisa
nces unto the all-pervading Supreme Personality of Godhead, who possesses unlimited transcendental qualities. Acting from within the cores of the hearts of all philosophers, who propagate various views, He causes them to forget their own souls while somet
imes agreeing and sometimes disagreeing among themselves. Thus He creates within this material world a situation in which they are unable to come to a conclusion. I offer my obeisances unto Him.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.32 - There are two parties-namely, the theists and the atheists. The theist, who accepts the Supersoul, finds the spiritual cause through mystic yoga. The S\'e4\'ec
khyite, however, who merely analyzes the material elements, comes to a conclusion of impersonalism and does not accept a supreme cause-whether Bhagav\'e4n, Param\'e4tm\'e4
 or even Brahman. Instead, he is preoccupied with the superfluous, external activities of material nature. Ultimately, however, both parties demonstrate the Absolute Truth because although they offer opposing statements, their object is the same 
ultimate cause. They are both approaching the same Supreme Brahman, to whom I offer my respectful obeisances.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.33 - The Supreme Personality of Godhead, who is inconceivably opulent, who is devoid of all material names, forms and pastimes, and who is
 all-pervading, is especially merciful to the devotees who worship His lotus feet. Thus He exhibits transcendental forms and names with His different pastimes. May that Supreme Personality of Godhead, whose form is eternal and full of knowledge and bliss,
 be merciful to me.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 
SB 6.4.34 - As the air carries various characteristics of the physical elements, like the aroma of a flower or colors resulting from a mixture of dust in the air, the Lord appears through lower systems of worship according to one's desi
res, although He appears as the demigods and not in His original form. What is the use of these other forms? May the original Supreme Personality of Godhead please fulfill my desires.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.35-39 - \'c7r\'e9 \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 said: The Supreme Personality of Godhead, Hari, who is extremely affectionate to His devotees, was very pleased by the prayers offered by Dak\'f1
a, and thus He appeared at that holy place known as Aghamar\'f1a\'eba. O Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Par\'e9k\'f1it, best of the Kuru dynasty, the Lord's lotus feet rested on the shoulders of His carrier, Garu\'f2
a, and He appeared with eight long, mighty, very beautiful arms. In His hands He held a disc, conchshell, sword, shield, arrow, bow, rope and club-in each hand a different weapon, all brilliantly shining. His garments were yel
low and His bodily hue deep bluish. His eyes and face were very cheerful, and from His neck to His feet hung a long garland of flowers. His chest was decorated with the Kaustubha jewel and the mark of \'c7r\'e9
vatsa. On His head was a gorgeous round helmet, and 
His ears were decorated with earrings resembling sharks. All these ornaments were uncommonly beautiful. The Lord wore a golden belt on His waist, bracelets on His arms, rings on His fingers, and ankle bells on His feet. Thus decorated by various ornaments
, Lord Hari, who is attractive to all the living entities of the three worlds, is known as Puru\'f1ottama, the best personality. He was accompanied by great devotees like N\'e4
rada, Nanda and all the principal demigods, led by the heavenly king, Indra, and the residents of various upper planetary systems such as Siddhaloka, Gandharvaloka and C\'e4ra\'eb
aloka. Situated on both sides of the Lord and behind Him as well, these devotees offered Him prayers continuously.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.40 - Seeing that wonderful and effulgent form of the Supreme Personality of Godhead, Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a was first somewhat afraid, but then he was very pleased to see the Lord, and he fell to the ground like a stick [da
\'eb\'f2avat] to offer his respects to the Lord.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.41 - As rivers are filled by water flowing from a mountain, all of Dak\'f1a's senses were filled with pleasure. Because of his highly elevated happiness, Dak\'f1
a could not say anything, but simply remained flat on the ground.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.42 - Although Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a could not say anything, when the Lord, who knows everyone's heart, saw His devotee prostrate in that manner and desiring to increase the population, He addressed him as follows.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.43 - The Supreme Personality of Godhead said: O most fortunate Pr\'e4cetasa, because of your great faith i
n Me, you have attained the supreme devotional ecstasy. Indeed, because of your austerities, combined with exalted devotion, your life is now successful. You have achieved complete perfection.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.44 - My dear Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a, you have performed extrem
e austerities for the welfare and growth of the world. My desire also is that everyone within this world be happy. I am therefore very pleased with you because you are endeavoring to fulfill My desire for the welfare of the entire world.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.45 - Lord Brahm\'e4, Lord \'c7iva, the Manus, all the other demigods in the higher planetary systems, and you praj\'e4
patis, who are increasing the population, are working for the benefit of all living entities. Thus you expansions of My marginal energy are incarnations of My various qualities.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.46 - My dear br\'e4hma\'eb
a, austerity in the form of meditation is My heart, Vedic knowledge in the form of hymns and mantras constitutes My body, and spiritual activities and ecstatic emotions are My actual form. The ritualistic ce
remonies and sacrifices, when properly conducted, are the various limbs of My body, the unseen good fortune proceeding from pious or spiritual activities constitutes My mind, and the demigods who execute My orders in various departments are My life and so
ul.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.47 - Before the creation of this cosmic manifestation, I alone existed with My specific spiritual potencies. Consciousness was then unmanifested, just as one's consciousness is unmanifested during the time of sleep.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.48 - I am the reservo
ir of unlimited potency, and therefore I am known as unlimited or all-pervading. From My material energy the cosmic manifestation appeared within Me, and in this universal manifestation appeared the chief being, Lord Brahm\'e4
, who is your source and is not born of a material mother.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.49-50 - When the chief lord of the universe, Lord Brahm\'e4 [Svayambh\'fc
], having been inspired by My energy, was attempting to create, he thought himself incapable. Therefore I gave him advice, and in accordance with My instru
ctions he underwent extremely difficult austerities. Because of these austerities, the great Lord Brahm\'e4 was able to create nine personalities, including you, to help him in the functions of creation.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.51 - O My dear son Dak\'f1a, Praj\'e4pati Pa\'efcajana has a daughter named Asikn\'e9, whom I offer to you so that you may accept her as your wife.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.52 - Now unite in sexual life as man and woman, and in this way, by sexual intercourse, you will be able to beget hundreds of children in the womb of this girl to increase the population.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.53 - After you give birth to many hundreds and thousands of children, they will also be captivated by My illusory energy and will engage, like You, in sexual intercourse. But because of My mercy to you and them, they wi
ll also be able to give Me presentations in devotion.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.4.54 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: After the creator of the entire universe, the Supreme Personality of Godhead, Hari, had spoken in this way in the presence of Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a, He immediately dis
appeared as if He were an object experienced in a dream.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }}}{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 Translations 1-54
\par }\pard \s97\ql \fi-605\li720\ri720\nowidctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp20\brdrcf14 \brdrl\brdrs\brdrw45\brsp20\brdrcf13 \tx1080\faauto\rin720\lin720\rtlgutter\itap0 \shading10000\cfpat16 {\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid10252087\charrsid10252087 \chftn 
{\footnote\ftnalt \pard\plain \s1217\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \shading10000\cfpat16 \b\f35\fs24\cf13\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid5731824\charrsid10252087 \chftn }{
\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.1 - \'c7r\'e9la \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: Impelled by the illusory energy of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a begot ten thousand sons in the womb of P\'e4\'efcajan\'e9 [Asikn\'e9
]. My dear King, these sons were called the Harya\'e7vas.
\par }\pard \s1217\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.2 - My dear King, all the sons of Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a were alike in being very gentle and obedient to the orders of their father. When their father ordered them to beget children, they all went in the western direction.

\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.3 - In the west, where the River Sindhu meets the sea, there is a great place of pilgrimage known as N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba-saras. Many sages and others advanced in spiritual consciousness live there.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.4-5 - In that holy place, the Harya\'e7vas began regularly touching the lake's waters and bathing in them. Gradually becoming very much purified, they became inclined toward the activities of paramaha\'e0
sas. Nevertheless, because their father had ordered them to increase the population, they performed severe austerities to fulfill his desires. One day, when the great sage N\'e4
rada saw those boys performing such fine austerities to increase the population, N\'e4rada approached them.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.6-8 - The great sage N\'e4rada said: My dear Harya\'e7vas, you have not seen the extremities of
 the earth. There is a kingdom where only one man lives and where there is a hole from which, having entered, no one emerges. A woman there who is extremely unchaste adorns herself with various attractive dresses, and the man who lives there is her husban
d
. In that kingdom, there is a river flowing in both directions, a wonderful home made of twenty-five materials, a swan that vibrates various sounds, and an automatically revolving object made of sharp razors and thunderbolts. You have not seen all this, a
nd therefore you are inexperienced boys without advanced knowledge. How, then, will you create progeny?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.9 - Alas, your father is omniscient, but you do not know his actual order. Without knowing the actual purpose of your father, how will you create progeny?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.10 - \'c7r\'e9 \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 said: Hearing these enigmatic words of N\'e4rada Muni, the Harya\'e7vas considered them with their natural intelligence, without help from others.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.11 - [The Harya\'e7vas understood the meaning of N\'e4rada's words as follows.] The word "bh\'fc\'f9
" ["the earth"] refers to the field of activities. The material body, which is a result of the living being's actions, is his field of activities, and it gives him false designations. Since time immemorial, he has received various 
types of material bodies, which are the roots of bondage to the material world. If one foolishly engages in temporary fruitive activities and does not look toward the cessation of this bondage, what will be the benefit of his actions?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.12 - [N\'e4rada Muni had said that there is a kingdom where there is only one male. The Harya\'e7
vas realized the purport of this statement.] The only enjoyer is the Supreme Personality of Godhead, who observes everything, everywhere. He is full of six opulences and fully in
dependent of everyone else. He is never subject to the three modes of material nature, for He is always transcendental to this material creation. If the members of human society do not understand Him, the Supreme, through their advancement in knowledge an
d activities, but simply work very hard like cats and dogs all day and night for temporary happiness, what will be the benefit of their activities?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.13 - [N\'e4rada Muni had described that there is a bila, or hole, from which, having entered, one does not return. The Harya\'e7
vas understood the meaning of this allegory.] Hardly once has a person who has entered the lower planetary system called P\'e4t\'e4la been seen to return. Similarly, if one enters the Vaiku\'eb\'f6ha-dh\'e4ma [pratyag-dh\'e4ma], he does not return to th
is material world. If there is such a place, from which, having gone, one does not return to the miserable material condition of life, what is the use of jumping like monkeys in the temporary material world and not seeing or understanding that place? What
 will be the profit?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.14 - [N\'e4rada Muni had described a woman who is a professional prostitute. The Harya\'e7
vas understood the identity of this woman.] Mixed with the mode of passion, the unsteady intelligence of every living entity is like a prostitut
e who changes dresses just to attract one's attention. If one fully engages in temporary fruitive activities, not understanding how this is taking place, what does he actually gain?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.15 - [N\'e4rada Muni had also spoken of a man who is the husband of the prostitute. The Harya\'e7
vas understood this as follows.] If one becomes the husband of a prostitute, he loses all independence. Similarly, if a living entity has polluted intelligence, he prolongs his materialistic life. Frustrated by material nature, he 
must follow the movements of the intelligence, which brings various conditions of happiness and distress. If one performs fruitive activities under such conditions, what will be the benefit?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.16 - [N\'e4rada Muni had said that there is a river flowing in both directions. The Harya\'e7
vas understood the purport of this statement.] Material nature functions in two ways-by creation and dissolution. Thus the river of material nature flows both ways. A living entity who unknowingly falls in this river is submer
ged in its waves, and since the current is swifter near the banks of the river, he is unable to get out. What will be the benefit of performing fruitive activities in that river of m\'e4y\'e4?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.17 - [N\'e4rada Muni had said that there is a house made of twenty-five elements. The Harya\'e7
vas understood this analogy.] The Supreme Lord is the reservoir of the twenty-five elements, and as the Supreme Being, the conductor of cause and effect, He causes their manifestation. If one engages in temporary fruitive activi
ties, not knowing that Supreme Person, what benefit will he derive?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.18 - [N\'e4rada Muni had spoken of a swan. That swan is explained in this verse.] The Vedic literatures [\'e7\'e4stras] vividly describe how to understand the Supreme Lord, the source of al
l material and spiritual energy. Indeed, they elaborately explain these two energies. The swan [ha\'e0
sa] is one who discriminates between matter and spirit, who accepts the essence of everything, and who explains the means of bondage and the means of liberat
ion. The words of scriptures consist of variegated vibrations. If a foolish rascal leaves aside the study of these \'e7\'e4stras to engage in temporary activities, what will be the result?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.19 - [N\'e4rada Muni had spoken of a physical object made of sharp blades and thunderbolts. The Harya\'e7
vas understood this allegory as follows.] Eternal time moves very sharply, as if made of razors and thunderbolts. Uninterrupted and fully independent, it drives the activities of the entire world. If one does not try to st
udy the eternal element of time, what benefit can he derive from performing temporary material activities?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.20 - [N\'e4rada Muni had asked how one could ignorantly defy one's own father. The Harya\'e7vas understood the meaning of this question.] One must accept the original instructions of the \'e7\'e4
stra. According to Vedic civilization, one is offered a sacred thread as a sign of second birth. One takes his second birth by dint of having received instructions in the \'e7\'e4stra from a bona fide spiritual master. Therefore, \'e7\'e4
stra, scripture, is the real father. All the \'e7\'e4stras instruct that one should end his material way of life. If one does not know the purpose of the father's orders, the \'e7\'e4stras, he is ignorant. The words of a material father who endeavors to e
ngage his son in material activities are not the real instructions of the father.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.21 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: My dear King, after hearing the instructions of N\'e4rada, the Harya\'e7vas, the sons of Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a, were firmly convinced. They all be
lieved in his instructions and reached the same conclusion. Having accepted him as their spiritual master, they circumambulated that great sage and followed the path by which one never returns to this world.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.22 - The seven musical notes-\'f1a, \'e5, g\'e4, ma, pa, dha and ni-are used in musical instruments, but originally they come from the S\'e4ma Veda. The great sage N\'e4
rada vibrates sounds describing the pastimes of the Supreme Lord. By such transcendental vibrations, such as Hare K\'e5\'f1\'eba, Hare K\'e5\'f1\'eba, K\'e5\'f1\'eba K\'e5\'f1\'eba, Hare Hare/ Hare R\'e4ma, Hare R\'e4ma, R\'e4ma R\'e4
ma, Hare Hare, he fixes his mind at the lotus feet of the Lord. Thus he directly perceives H\'e5\'f1\'e9ke\'e7a, the master of the senses. After delivering the Harya\'e7vas, N\'e4rada Muni continued traveling throughout the planet
ary systems, his mind always fixed at the lotus feet of the Lord.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.23 - The Harya\'e7vas, the sons of Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a, were very well behaved, cultured sons, but unfortunately, because of the instructions of N\'e4rada Muni, they deviated from the order of their father. When Dak\'f1
a heard this news, which was brought to him by N\'e4rada Muni, he began to lament. Although he was the father of such good sons, he had lost them all. Certainly this was lamentable.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.24 - When Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a was lamenting for his lost children, Lord Brahm\'e4 pacified him with instructions, and thereafter Dak\'f1a begot one thousand more children in the womb of his wife, P\'e4\'efcajan\'e9
. This time his sons were known as the Saval\'e4\'e7vas.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.25 - In accordance with their father's order to beget children, the second group of sons also went to N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eb
a-saras, the same place where their brothers had previously attained perfection by following the instructions of N\'e4rada. Undertaking great vows of austerity, the Saval\'e4\'e7vas remained at that holy place.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.26 - At N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eb
a-saras, the second group of sons performed penances in the same way as the first. They bathed in the holy water, and by its touch all the dirty material desires in their hearts were cleansed away. They murmured mantras beginning with o\'e0k\'e4
ra and underwent a severe course of austerities.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.27-28 - For a few months the sons of Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a drank only water and ate only air. Thus undergoing great austerities, they recited this mantra: "Let us offer our respectful obeisances unto N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eb
a, the Supreme Personality of Godhead, who is always situated in His transcendental abode. Since He is the Supreme Person [paramaha\'e0sa], let us offer our respectful obeisances unto Him."
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.29 - O King Par\'e9k\'f1it, N\'e4rada Muni approached these sons of Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a, who were engaged in tapasya to beget children, and spoke enigmatic words to them just as he had spoken to their elder brothers.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.30 - O sons of Dak\'f1a, please hear my words of instruction attentively. You are all very affectionate to your elder brothers, the Harya\'e7vas. Therefore you should follow their path.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.31 - A brother aware of the principles of religion follows in the footsteps of his elder brothers. Because of being highly elevated, such a pious brother get
s the opportunity to associate and enjoy with demigods like the Maruts, who are all affectionate to their brothers.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.32 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: O best of the advanced \'c4ryans, after saying this much to the sons of Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a, N\'e4rada Muni, 
whose merciful glance never goes in vain, left as he had planned. The sons of Dak\'f1a followed their elder brothers. Not attempting to produce children, they engaged themselves in K\'e5\'f1\'eba consciousness.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.33 - The Saval\'e4\'e7vas took to the correct path, whi
ch is obtainable by a mode of life meant to achieve devotional service, or the mercy of the Supreme Personality of Godhead. Like nights that have gone to the west, they have not returned even until now.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.34 - At this time, Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a observed many inauspicious signs, and he heard from various sources that his second group of sons, the Saval\'e4\'e7
vas, had followed the path of their elder brothers in accordance with the instructions of N\'e4rada.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.35 - When he heard that the Saval\'e4\'e7vas had also left this world to engage in devotional service, Dak\'f1a was angry at N\'e4rada, and he almost fainted due to lamentation. When Dak\'f1a met N\'e4rada, Dak\'f1
a's lips began trembling in anger, and he spoke as follows.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.36 - Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a said: Alas, N\'e4rada Muni, you wear the dress of a saintly person, but you are not actually a saint. Indeed, although I am now in g\'e5
hastha life, I am a saintly person. By showing my sons the path of renunciation, you have done me an abominable injustice.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.37 - Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a said: My sons were not at all freed from their three debts. Indeed, they did not properly consider their obligations. O N\'e4
rada Muni, O personality of sinful action, you have obstructed their progress toward good fortune in this world and the next because they are still indebted to the saintly persons, the demigods and their father.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.38 - Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a continued: Thus committing violence against other living entities and yet claiming to be an associate of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, you are defaming the Supreme P
ersonality of Godhead. You needlessly created a mentality of renunciation in innocent boys, and therefore you are shameless and devoid of compassion. How could you travel with the personal associates of the Supreme Lord ?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.39 - All the devotees of t
he Lord but you are very kind to the conditioned souls and are eager to benefit others. Although you wear the dress of a devotee, you create enmity with people who are not your enemies, or you break friendship and create enmity between friends. Are you no
t ashamed of posing as a devotee while performing these abominable actions?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.40 - Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a continued: If you think that simply awakening the sense of renunciation will detach one from the material world, I must say that unless full knowledge i
s awakened, simply changing dresses as you have done cannot possibly bring detachment.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.41 - Material enjoyment is indeed the cause of all unhappiness, but one cannot give it up unless one has personally experienced how much suffering it is. Therefo
re one should be allowed to remain in so-called material enjoyment while simultaneously advancing in knowledge to experience the misery of this false material happiness. Then, without help from others, one will find material enjoyment detestful. Those who
se minds are changed by others do not become as renounced as those who have personal experience.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.42 - Although I live in household life with my wife and children, I honestly follow the Vedic injunctions by engaging in fruitive activities to enjoy life without sinful reactions. I have performed all kinds of yaj\'ef
as, including the deva-yaj\'efa, \'e5\'f1i-yaj\'efa, pit\'e5-yaj\'efa and n\'e5-yaj\'efa. Because these yaj\'efas are called vratas [vows], I am known as a g\'e5havrata. Unfortunately, you have given me great displeasure by
 misguiding my sons, for no reason, to the path of renunciation. This can be tolerated once.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.43 - You have made me lose my sons once, and now you have again done the same inauspicious thing. Therefore you are a rascal who does not know how to behav
e toward others. You may travel all over the universe, but I curse you to have no residence anywhere.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.44 - \'c7r\'e9 \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: My dear King, since N\'e4rada Muni is an approved saintly person, when cursed by Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a he replied, "tad b\'e4\'f2
ham: Yes, what you have said is good. I accept this curse." He could have cursed Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a in return, but because he is a tolerant and merciful s\'e4dhu, he took no action.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }}}{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  5. }{\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid10252087\charrsid10252087 \chftn {\footnote\ftnalt \pard\plain \s1217\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 
\b\f35\fs24\cf13\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid5731824\charrsid10252087 \chftn }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.1 - \'c7r\'e9la \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: Impelled by the illusory energy of Lord Vi\'f1
\'ebu, Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a begot ten thousand sons in the womb of P\'e4\'efcajan\'e9 [Asikn\'e9]. My dear King, these sons were called the Harya\'e7vas.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.2 - My dear King, all the sons of Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a were alike in being very gentle and obedient to the orders of their father. When their father ordered them to beget children, they all went in the western direction.

\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.3 - In the west, where the River Sindhu meets the sea, there is a great place of pilgrimage known as N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba-saras. Many sages and others advanced in spiritual consciousness live there.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.4-5 - In that holy place, the Harya\'e7vas began regularly touching the lake's waters and bathing in them. Gradually becoming very much purified, they became inclined toward the activities of paramaha\'e0
sas. Nevertheless, because their father had ordered them to increase the population, they performed severe austerities to fulfill his desires. One day, when the great sage N\'e4rada saw those boys performing such fine austerities to increase the populatio
n, N\'e4rada approached them.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.6-8 - The great sage N\'e4rada said: My dear Harya\'e7
vas, you have not seen the extremities of the earth. There is a kingdom where only one man lives and where there is a hole from which, having entered, no one emerges. A woma
n there who is extremely unchaste adorns herself with various attractive dresses, and the man who lives there is her husband. In that kingdom, there is a river flowing in both directions, a wonderful home made of twenty-five materials, a swan that vibrate
s various sounds, and an automatically revolving object made of sharp razors and thunderbolts. You have not seen all this, and therefore you are inexperienced boys without advanced knowledge. How, then, will you create progeny?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.9 - Alas, your father is omniscient, but you do not know his actual order. Without knowing the actual purpose of your father, how will you create progeny?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.10 - \'c7r\'e9 \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 said: Hearing these enigmatic words of N\'e4rada Muni, the Harya\'e7vas considered them with their natural intelligence, without help from others.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.11 - [The Harya\'e7vas understood the meaning of N\'e4rada's words as follows.] The word "bh\'fc\'f9" ["the earth"] refers to the field of activities. The material body, which is a result of the living being
's actions, is his field of activities, and it gives him false designations. Since time immemorial, he has received various types of material bodies, which are the roots of bondage to the material world. If one foolishly engages in temporary fruitive acti
vities and does not look toward the cessation of this bondage, what will be the benefit of his actions?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.12 - [N\'e4rada Muni had said that there is a kingdom where there is only one male. The Harya\'e7vas realized the purport of this statement.] The only 
enjoyer is the Supreme Personality of Godhead, who observes everything, everywhere. He is full of six opulences and fully independent of everyone else. He is never subject to the three modes of material nature, for He is always transcendental to this mate
r
ial creation. If the members of human society do not understand Him, the Supreme, through their advancement in knowledge and activities, but simply work very hard like cats and dogs all day and night for temporary happiness, what will be the benefit of th
eir activities?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.13 - [N\'e4rada Muni had described that there is a bila, or hole, from which, having entered, one does not return. The Harya\'e7
vas understood the meaning of this allegory.] Hardly once has a person who has entered the lower planetary system called P\'e4t\'e4la been seen to return. Similarly, if one enters the Vaiku\'eb\'f6ha-dh\'e4ma [pratyag-dh\'e4
ma], he does not return to this material world. If there is such a place, from which, having gone, one does not return to the miserable material condition of lif
e, what is the use of jumping like monkeys in the temporary material world and not seeing or understanding that place? What will be the profit?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.14 - [N\'e4rada Muni had described a woman who is a professional prostitute. The Harya\'e7vas understood the i
dentity of this woman.] Mixed with the mode of passion, the unsteady intelligence of every living entity is like a prostitute who changes dresses just to attract one's attention. If one fully engages in temporary fruitive activities, not understanding how
 this is taking place, what does he actually gain?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.15 - [N\'e4rada Muni had also spoken of a man who is the husband of the prostitute. The Harya\'e7vas understood this as follows.] If one becomes the husband of a prostitute, he loses all independence. Sim
ilarly, if a living entity has polluted intelligence, he prolongs his materialistic life. Frustrated by material nature, he must follow the movements of the intelligence, which brings various conditions of happiness and distress. If one performs fruitive 
activities under such conditions, what will be the benefit?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.16 - [N\'e4rada Muni had said that there is a river flowing in both directions. The Harya\'e7vas understood the purport of this statement.] Material nature functions in two ways-by creation and d
issolution. Thus the river of material nature flows both ways. A living entity who unknowingly falls in this river is submerged in its waves, and since the current is swifter near the banks of the river, he is unable to get out. What will be the benefit o
f performing fruitive activities in that river of m\'e4y\'e4?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.17 - [N\'e4rada Muni had said that there is a house made of twenty-five elements. The Harya\'e7vas understood this analogy.] The Supreme Lord is the reservoir of the twenty-five elements, and as the 
Supreme Being, the conductor of cause and effect, He causes their manifestation. If one engages in temporary fruitive activities, not knowing that Supreme Person, what benefit will he derive?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.18 - [N\'e4rada Muni had spoken of a swan. That swan is explained in this verse.] The Vedic literatures [\'e7\'e4
stras] vividly describe how to understand the Supreme Lord, the source of all material and spiritual energy. Indeed, they elaborately explain these two energies. The swan [ha\'e0sa] is one who discriminates betw
een matter and spirit, who accepts the essence of everything, and who explains the means of bondage and the means of liberation. The words of scriptures consist of variegated vibrations. If a foolish rascal leaves aside the study of these \'e7\'e4
stras to engage in temporary activities, what will be the result?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.19 - [N\'e4rada Muni had spoken of a physical object made of sharp blades and thunderbolts. The Harya\'e7vas understood this allegory as follows.] Eternal time moves very sharply, as if made of razors an
d thunderbolts. Uninterrupted and fully independent, it drives the activities of the entire world. If one does not try to study the eternal element of time, what benefit can he derive from performing temporary material activities?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.20 - [N\'e4rada Muni had asked how one could ignorantly defy one's own father. The Harya\'e7vas understood the meaning of this question.] One must accept the original instructions of the \'e7\'e4
stra. According to Vedic civilization, one is offered a sacred thread as a sign of second birth. One takes his second birth by dint of having received instructions in the \'e7\'e4stra from a bona fide spiritual master. Therefore, \'e7\'e4
stra, scripture, is the real father. All the \'e7\'e4stras instruct that one should end his material way of life. If one does not know the purpose of the father's orders, the \'e7\'e4
stras, he is ignorant. The words of a material father who endeavors to engage his son in material activities are not the real instructions of the father.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.21 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: My dear King, after hearing the instructions of N\'e4rada, the Harya\'e7vas, the sons of Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1
a, were firmly convinced. They all believed in his instructions and reached the same conclusion. Having accepted him as their spiritual master, they circumambulated that great sage and followed the path by which one never returns to this world.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.22 - The seven musical notes-\'f1a, \'e5, g\'e4, ma, pa, dha and ni-are used in musical instruments, but originally they come from the S\'e4ma Veda. The great sage N\'e4rada vibrates soun
ds describing the pastimes of the Supreme Lord. By such transcendental vibrations, such as Hare K\'e5\'f1\'eba, Hare K\'e5\'f1\'eba, K\'e5\'f1\'eba K\'e5\'f1\'eba, Hare Hare/ Hare R\'e4ma, Hare R\'e4ma, R\'e4ma R\'e4
ma, Hare Hare, he fixes his mind at the lotus feet of the Lord. Thus he directly perceives H\'e5\'f1\'e9ke\'e7a, the master of the senses. After delivering the Harya\'e7vas, N\'e4
rada Muni continued traveling throughout the planetary systems, his mind always fixed at the lotus feet of the Lord.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.23 - The Harya\'e7vas, the sons of Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a, were very well behaved, cultured sons, but unfortunately, because of the instructions of N\'e4rada Muni, they deviated from the order of their father. When Dak\'f1
a heard this news, which was brought to him by N\'e4rada Muni, he began to lament. Although he was the father of such good sons, he had lost them all. Certainly this was lamentable.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.24 - When Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a was lamenting for his lost children, Lord Brahm\'e4 pacified him with instructions, and thereafter Dak\'f1a begot one thousand more children in the womb of his wife, P\'e4\'efcajan\'e9
. This time his sons were known as the Saval\'e4\'e7vas.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.25 - In accordance with their father's order to beget children, the second group of sons also went to N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba-saras, the same place where their brothers had previously attained p
erfection by following the instructions of N\'e4rada. Undertaking great vows of austerity, the Saval\'e4\'e7vas remained at that holy place.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.26 - At N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba-saras, the second group of sons performed penances in the same way as the first. They bathed in the
 holy water, and by its touch all the dirty material desires in their hearts were cleansed away. They murmured mantras beginning with o\'e0k\'e4ra and underwent a severe course of austerities.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.27-28 - For a few months the sons of Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a drank only water and ate only air. Thus undergoing great austerities, they recited this mantra: "Let us offer our respectful obeisances unto N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eb
a, the Supreme Personality of Godhead, who is always situated in His transcendental abode. Since He is the Supreme Person [paramaha\'e0sa], let us offer our respectful obeisances unto Him."
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.29 - O King Par\'e9k\'f1it, N\'e4rada Muni approached these sons of Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a, who were engaged in tapasya to beget children, and spoke enigmatic words to them just as he had spoken to their elder brothers.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.30 - O sons of Dak\'f1a, please hear my words of instruction attentively. You are all very affectionate to your elder brothers, the Harya\'e7vas. Therefore you should follow their path.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.31 - A brother aware of the princ
iples of religion follows in the footsteps of his elder brothers. Because of being highly elevated, such a pious brother gets the opportunity to associate and enjoy with demigods like the Maruts, who are all affectionate to their brothers.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.32 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: O best of the advanced \'c4ryans, after saying this much to the sons of Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a, N\'e4
rada Muni, whose merciful glance never goes in vain, left as he had planned. The sons of Dak\'f1a followed their elder brothers. Not attempting to produce children, they engaged themselves in K\'e5\'f1\'eba consciousness.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.33 - The Saval\'e4\'e7vas took to the correct path, which is obtainable by a mode of life meant to achieve devotional service, or the mercy of the Supreme Personality of Godhead. Like nig
hts that have gone to the west, they have not returned even until now.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.34 - At this time, Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a observed many inauspicious signs, and he heard from various sources that his second group of sons, the Saval\'e4\'e7vas, had followed the path of th
eir elder brothers in accordance with the instructions of N\'e4rada.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.35 - When he heard that the Saval\'e4\'e7vas had also left this world to engage in devotional service, Dak\'f1a was angry at N\'e4rada, and he almost fainted due to lamentation. When Dak\'f1a met N\'e4rada, Dak\'f1
a's lips began trembling in anger, and he spoke as follows.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.36 - Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a said: Alas, N\'e4rada Muni, you wear the dress of a saintly person, but you are not actually a saint. Indeed, although I am now in g\'e5hastha life, I am a saintl
y person. By showing my sons the path of renunciation, you have done me an abominable injustice.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.37 - Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a said: My sons were not at all freed from their three debts. Indeed, they did not properly consider their obligations. O N\'e4rada Mun
i, O personality of sinful action, you have obstructed their progress toward good fortune in this world and the next because they are still indebted to the saintly persons, the demigods and their father.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.38 - Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a continued: Thus committing violence against other living entities and yet claiming to be an associate of Lord Vi\'f1\'eb
u, you are defaming the Supreme Personality of Godhead. You needlessly created a mentality of renunciation in innocent boys, and therefore you are shameless and devoid of compassion. How could you travel with the personal associates of the Supreme Lord ?

\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.39 - All the devotees of the Lord but you are very kind to the conditioned souls and are eager to benefit others. Although you wear the dress of a devotee, yo
u create enmity with people who are not your enemies, or you break friendship and create enmity between friends. Are you not ashamed of posing as a devotee while performing these abominable actions?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.40 - Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a continued: If you think that
 simply awakening the sense of renunciation will detach one from the material world, I must say that unless full knowledge is awakened, simply changing dresses as you have done cannot possibly bring detachment.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.41 - Material enjoyment is indeed the
 cause of all unhappiness, but one cannot give it up unless one has personally experienced how much suffering it is. Therefore one should be allowed to remain in so-called material enjoyment while simultaneously advancing in knowledge to experience the mi
sery of this false material happiness. Then, without help from others, one will find material enjoyment detestful. Those whose minds are changed by others do not become as renounced as those who have personal experience.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.42 - Although I live in household life with my wife and children, I honestly follow the Vedic injunctions by engaging in fruitive activities to enjoy life without sinful reactions. I have performed all kinds of yaj\'ef
as, including the deva-yaj\'efa, \'e5\'f1i-yaj\'efa, pit\'e5-yaj\'efa and n\'e5-yaj\'efa. Because these yaj\'efas are called vratas [vows], I am known as a g\'e5
havrata. Unfortunately, you have given me great displeasure by misguiding my sons, for no reason, to the path of renunciation. This can be tolerated once.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.43 - You have made me lose my s
ons once, and now you have again done the same inauspicious thing. Therefore you are a rascal who does not know how to behave toward others. You may travel all over the universe, but I curse you to have no residence anywhere.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.44 - \'c7r\'e9 \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: My dear King, since N\'e4rada Muni is an approved saintly person, when cursed by Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a he replied, "tad b\'e4\'f2
ham: Yes, what you have said is good. I accept this curse." He could have cursed Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a in return, but because he is a tolerant and merciful s\'e4dhu, he took no action.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }}}{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  N\'e4rada Muni Cursed by Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a
\par }\pard \s97\ql \fi-605\li720\ri720\nowidctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp20\brdrcf14 \brdrl\brdrs\brdrw45\brsp20\brdrcf13 \tx1080\faauto\outlinelevel0\rin720\lin720\rtlgutter\itap0\pararsid10252087 \shading10000\cfpat8 {\fs28\cf2\insrsid10252087   \bullet 
  }{\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid10252087\charrsid10252087 \chftn {\footnote\ftnalt \pard\plain \s1217\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\f35\fs24\cf13\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {
\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid5731824\charrsid10252087 \chftn }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.1 - \'c7r\'e9la \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: Impelled by the illusory energy of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a begot ten thousand sons in the womb of P
\'e4\'efcajan\'e9 [Asikn\'e9]. My dear King, these sons were called the Harya\'e7vas.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.2 - My dear King, all the sons of Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a were alike in being very gentle and obedient to the orders of their father. When their father ordered them to beget children, they all went in the western direction.

\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.3 - In the west, where the River Sindhu meets the sea, there is a great place of pilgrimage known as N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba-saras. Many sages and others advanced in spiritual consciousness live there.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.4-5 - In that holy place, the Harya\'e7vas began regularly touching the lake's waters and bathing in them. Gradually becoming very much purified, they became inclined toward the activities of paramaha\'e0
sas. Nevertheless, because their father had ordered them to increase the population, they performed severe austerities to fulfill his desires. One day, when the great sage N\'e4
rada saw those boys performing such fine austerities to increase the population, N\'e4rada approached them.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.6-8 - The great sage N\'e4rada said: My dear Harya\'e7vas, you have not seen the extremities of the ear
th. There is a kingdom where only one man lives and where there is a hole from which, having entered, no one emerges. A woman there who is extremely unchaste adorns herself with various attractive dresses, and the man who lives there is her husband. In th
a
t kingdom, there is a river flowing in both directions, a wonderful home made of twenty-five materials, a swan that vibrates various sounds, and an automatically revolving object made of sharp razors and thunderbolts. You have not seen all this, and there
fore you are inexperienced boys without advanced knowledge. How, then, will you create progeny?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.9 - Alas, your father is omniscient, but you do not know his actual order. Without knowing the actual purpose of your father, how will you create progeny?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.10 - \'c7r\'e9 \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 said: Hearing these enigmatic words of N\'e4rada Muni, the Harya\'e7vas considered them with their natural intelligence, without help from others.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.11 - [The Harya\'e7vas understood the meaning of N\'e4rada's words as follows.] The word "bh\'fc\'f9
" ["the earth"] refers to the field of activities. The material body, which is a result of the living being's actions, is his field of activities, and it gives him false designations. Since time immemorial, he has received various types of
 material bodies, which are the roots of bondage to the material world. If one foolishly engages in temporary fruitive activities and does not look toward the cessation of this bondage, what will be the benefit of his actions?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.12 - [N\'e4rada Muni had said that there is a kingdom where there is only one male. The Harya\'e7
vas realized the purport of this statement.] The only enjoyer is the Supreme Personality of Godhead, who observes everything, everywhere. He is full of six opulences and fully independen
t of everyone else. He is never subject to the three modes of material nature, for He is always transcendental to this material creation. If the members of human society do not understand Him, the Supreme, through their advancement in knowledge and activi
ties, but simply work very hard like cats and dogs all day and night for temporary happiness, what will be the benefit of their activities?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.13 - [N\'e4rada Muni had described that there is a bila, or hole, from which, having entered, one does not return. The Harya\'e7
vas understood the meaning of this allegory.] Hardly once has a person who has entered the lower planetary system called P\'e4t\'e4la been seen to return. Similarly, if one enters the Vaiku\'eb\'f6ha-dh\'e4ma [pratyag-dh\'e4
ma], he does not return to this mater
ial world. If there is such a place, from which, having gone, one does not return to the miserable material condition of life, what is the use of jumping like monkeys in the temporary material world and not seeing or understanding that place? What will be
 the profit?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.14 - [N\'e4rada Muni had described a woman who is a professional prostitute. The Harya\'e7
vas understood the identity of this woman.] Mixed with the mode of passion, the unsteady intelligence of every living entity is like a prostitute who ch
anges dresses just to attract one's attention. If one fully engages in temporary fruitive activities, not understanding how this is taking place, what does he actually gain?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.15 - [N\'e4rada Muni had also spoken of a man who is the husband of the prostitute. The Harya\'e7
vas understood this as follows.] If one becomes the husband of a prostitute, he loses all independence. Similarly, if a living entity has polluted intelligence, he prolongs his materialistic life. Frustrated by material nature, he must fol
low the movements of the intelligence, which brings various conditions of happiness and distress. If one performs fruitive activities under such conditions, what will be the benefit?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.16 - [N\'e4rada Muni had said that there is a river flowing in both directions. The Harya\'e7
vas understood the purport of this statement.] Material nature functions in two ways-by creation and dissolution. Thus the river of material nature flows both ways. A living entity who unknowingly falls in this river is submerged in i
ts waves, and since the current is swifter near the banks of the river, he is unable to get out. What will be the benefit of performing fruitive activities in that river of m\'e4y\'e4?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.17 - [N\'e4rada Muni had said that there is a house made of twenty-five elements. The Harya\'e7
vas understood this analogy.] The Supreme Lord is the reservoir of the twenty-five elements, and as the Supreme Being, the conductor of cause and effect, He causes their manifestation. If one engages in temporary fruitive activities, no
t knowing that Supreme Person, what benefit will he derive?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.18 - [N\'e4rada Muni had spoken of a swan. That swan is explained in this verse.] The Vedic literatures [\'e7\'e4stras] vividly describe how to understand the Supreme Lord, the source of all materi
al and spiritual energy. Indeed, they elaborately explain these two energies. The swan [ha\'e0
sa] is one who discriminates between matter and spirit, who accepts the essence of everything, and who explains the means of bondage and the means of liberation. The
 words of scriptures consist of variegated vibrations. If a foolish rascal leaves aside the study of these \'e7\'e4stras to engage in temporary activities, what will be the result?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.19 - [N\'e4rada Muni had spoken of a physical object made of sharp blades and thunderbolts. The Harya\'e7
vas understood this allegory as follows.] Eternal time moves very sharply, as if made of razors and thunderbolts. Uninterrupted and fully independent, it drives the activities of the entire world. If one does not try to study the 
eternal element of time, what benefit can he derive from performing temporary material activities?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.20 - [N\'e4rada Muni had asked how one could ignorantly defy one's own father. The Harya\'e7vas understood the meaning of this question.] One must accept the original instructions of the \'e7\'e4
stra. According to Vedic civilization, one is offered a sacred thread as a sign of second birth. One takes his second birth by dint of having received instructions in the \'e7\'e4stra from a bona fide spiritual master. Therefore, \'e7\'e4
stra, scripture, is the real father. All the \'e7\'e4stras instruct that one should end his material way of life. If one does not know the purpose of the father's orders, the \'e7\'e4
stras, he is ignorant. The words of a material father who endeavors to engage his son in material activities are not the real instructions of the father.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.21 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: My dear King, after hearing the instructions of N\'e4rada, the Harya\'e7vas, the sons of Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a, were firmly convinced. They all believed i
n his instructions and reached the same conclusion. Having accepted him as their spiritual master, they circumambulated that great sage and followed the path by which one never returns to this world.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.22 - The seven musical notes-\'f1a, \'e5, g\'e4, ma, pa, dha and ni-are used in musical instruments, but originally they come from the S\'e4ma Veda. The great sage N\'e4
rada vibrates sounds describing the pastimes of the Supreme Lord. By such transcendental vibrations, such as Hare K\'e5\'f1\'eba, Hare K\'e5\'f1\'eba, K\'e5\'f1\'eba K\'e5\'f1\'eba, Hare Hare/ Hare R\'e4ma, Hare R\'e4ma, R\'e4ma R\'e4
ma, Hare Hare, he fixes his mind at the lotus feet of the Lord. Thus he directly perceives H\'e5\'f1\'e9ke\'e7a, the master of the senses. After delivering the Harya\'e7vas, N\'e4rada Muni continued traveling throughout the planetary syst
ems, his mind always fixed at the lotus feet of the Lord.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.23 - The Harya\'e7vas, the sons of Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a, were very well behaved, cultured sons, but unfortunately, because of the instructions of N\'e4rada Muni, they deviated from the order of their father. When Dak\'f1
a heard this news, which was brought to him by N\'e4rada Muni, he began to lament. Although he was the father of such good sons, he had lost them all. Certainly this was lamentable.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.24 - When Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a was lamenting for his lost children, Lord Brahm\'e4 pacified him with instructions, and thereafter Dak\'f1a begot one thousand more children in the womb of his wife, P\'e4\'efcajan\'e9
. This time his sons were known as the Saval\'e4\'e7vas.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.25 - In accordance with their father's order to beget children, the second group of sons also went to N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eb
a-saras, the same place where their brothers had previously attained perfection by following the instructions of N\'e4rada. Undertaking great vows of austerity, the Saval\'e4\'e7vas remained at that holy place.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.26 - At N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eb
a-saras, the second group of sons performed penances in the same way as the first. They bathed in the holy water, and by its touch all the dirty material desires in their hearts were cleansed away. They murmured mantras beginning with o\'e0k\'e4
ra and underwent a severe course of austerities.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.27-28 - For a few months the sons of Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a drank only water and ate only air. Thus undergoing great austerities, they recited this mantra: "Let us offer our respectful obeisances unto N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eb
a, the Supreme Personality of Godhead, who is always situated in His transcendental abode. Since He is the Supreme Person [paramaha\'e0sa], let us offer our respectful obeisances unto Him."
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.29 - O King Par\'e9k\'f1it, N\'e4rada Muni approached these sons of Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a, who were engaged in tapasya to beget children, and spoke enigmatic words to them just as he had spoken to their elder brothers.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.30 - O sons of Dak\'f1a, please hear my words of instruction attentively. You are all very affectionate to your elder brothers, the Harya\'e7vas. Therefore you should follow their path.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.31 - A brother aware of the principles of religion follows in the footsteps of his elder brothers. Because of being highly elevated, such a pious brother gets the op
portunity to associate and enjoy with demigods like the Maruts, who are all affectionate to their brothers.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.32 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: O best of the advanced \'c4ryans, after saying this much to the sons of Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a, N\'e4rada Muni, whose me
rciful glance never goes in vain, left as he had planned. The sons of Dak\'f1a followed their elder brothers. Not attempting to produce children, they engaged themselves in K\'e5\'f1\'eba consciousness.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.33 - The Saval\'e4\'e7vas took to the correct path, which is ob
tainable by a mode of life meant to achieve devotional service, or the mercy of the Supreme Personality of Godhead. Like nights that have gone to the west, they have not returned even until now.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.34 - At this time, Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a observed many inauspicious signs, and he heard from various sources that his second group of sons, the Saval\'e4\'e7
vas, had followed the path of their elder brothers in accordance with the instructions of N\'e4rada.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.35 - When he heard that the Saval\'e4\'e7vas had also left this world to engage in devotional service, Dak\'f1a was angry at N\'e4rada, and he almost fainted due to lamentation. When Dak\'f1a met N\'e4rada, Dak\'f1
a's lips began trembling in anger, and he spoke as follows.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.36 - Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a said: Alas, N\'e4rada Muni, you wear the dress of a saintly person, but you are not actually a saint. Indeed, although I am now in g\'e5
hastha life, I am a saintly person. By showing my sons the path of renunciation, you have done me an abominable injustice.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.37 - Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a said: My sons were not at all freed from their three debts. Indeed, they did not properly consider their obligations. O N\'e4
rada Muni, O personality of sinful action, you have obstructed their progress toward good fortune in this world and the next because they are still indebted to the saintly persons, the demigods and their father.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.38 - Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a continued: Thus committing violence against other living entities and yet claiming to be an associate of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, you are defaming the Supreme Personali
ty of Godhead. You needlessly created a mentality of renunciation in innocent boys, and therefore you are shameless and devoid of compassion. How could you travel with the personal associates of the Supreme Lord ?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.39 - All the devotees of the Lord 
but you are very kind to the conditioned souls and are eager to benefit others. Although you wear the dress of a devotee, you create enmity with people who are not your enemies, or you break friendship and create enmity between friends. Are you not ashame
d of posing as a devotee while performing these abominable actions?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.40 - Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a continued: If you think that simply awakening the sense of renunciation will detach one from the material world, I must say that unless full knowledge is awaken
ed, simply changing dresses as you have done cannot possibly bring detachment.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.41 - Material enjoyment is indeed the cause of all unhappiness, but one cannot give it up unless one has personally experienced how much suffering it is. Therefore one s
hould be allowed to remain in so-called material enjoyment while simultaneously advancing in knowledge to experience the misery of this false material happiness. Then, without help from others, one will find material enjoyment detestful. Those whose minds
 are changed by others do not become as renounced as those who have personal experience.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.42 - Although I live in household life with my wife and children, I honestly follow the Vedic injunctions by engaging in fruitive activities to enjoy life without sinful reactions. I have performed all kinds of yaj\'ef
as, including the deva-yaj\'efa, \'e5\'f1i-yaj\'efa, pit\'e5-yaj\'efa and n\'e5-yaj\'efa. Because these yaj\'efas are called vratas [vows], I am known as a g\'e5havrata. Unfortunately, you have given me great displeasure by misguid
ing my sons, for no reason, to the path of renunciation. This can be tolerated once.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.43 - You have made me lose my sons once, and now you have again done the same inauspicious thing. Therefore you are a rascal who does not know how to behave toward
 others. You may travel all over the universe, but I curse you to have no residence anywhere.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.5.44 - \'c7r\'e9 \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: My dear King, since N\'e4rada Muni is an approved saintly person, when cursed by Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a he replied, "tad b\'e4\'f2ham
: Yes, what you have said is good. I accept this curse." He could have cursed Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a in return, but because he is a tolerant and merciful s\'e4dhu, he took no action.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }}}{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 Translations 1-44
\par }\pard \s97\ql \fi-605\li720\ri720\nowidctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp20\brdrcf14 \brdrl\brdrs\brdrw45\brsp20\brdrcf13 \tx1080\faauto\rin720\lin720\rtlgutter\itap0 \shading10000\cfpat16 {\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid10252087\charrsid10252087 \chftn 
{\footnote\ftnalt \pard\plain \s1217\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \shading10000\cfpat16 \b\f35\fs24\cf13\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid5731824\charrsid10252087 \chftn }{
\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.1 - \'c7r\'e9 \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 said: My dear King, thereafter, at the request of Lord Brahm\'e4, Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a, who is known as Pr\'e4cetasa, begot sixty daughters in the womb of his wife Asikn\'e9
. All the daughters were very affectionate toward their father.
\par }\pard \s1217\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.2 - He gave ten daughters in charity to Dharmar\'e4ja [Yamar\'e4ja], thirteen to Ka\'e7yapa [first twelve and then one more], twenty-seven to the moon-god, and two each to A\'ecgir\'e4, K\'e5\'e7\'e4\'e7va and Bh\'fc
ta. The other four daughters were given to Ka\'e7yapa. [Thus Ka\'e7yapa received seventeen daughters in all.]
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.3 - Now please hear from me the names of all these daughters and their descendants, who filled all the three worlds.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.4 - The ten daughters given to Yamar\'e4ja were named Bh\'e4nu, Lamb\'e4, Kakud, Y\'e4mi, Vi\'e7v\'e4, S\'e4dhy\'e4, Marutvat\'e9, Vasu, Muh\'fcrt\'e4 and Sa\'eckalp\'e4. Now hear the names of their sons.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.5 - O King, a son named Deva-\'e5\'f1abha was born from the womb of Bh\'e4nu, and from him came a son named Indrasena. From the womb of Lamb\'e4 came a son named Vidyota, who generated all the clouds.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.6 - From the womb of Kakud came the son named Sa\'ecka\'f6a, whose son was named K\'e9ka\'f6a. From K\'e9ka\'f6a came the demigods named Durg\'e4. From Y\'e4mi came the son named Svarga, whose son was named Nandi.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.7 - The sons of Vi\'e7v\'e4 were the Vi\'e7vadevas, who had no progeny. From the womb of S\'e4dhy\'e4 came the S\'e4dhyas, who had a son named Arthasiddhi.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.8 - The two sons who took birth from the womb of Marutvat\'e9 were Marutv\'e4n and Jayanta. Jayanta, who is an expansion of Lord V\'e4sudeva, is known as Upendra.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.9 - The demigods named the Mauh\'fcrtikas took birth from the womb of Muh\'fcrt\'e4. These demigods deliver the results of actions to the living entities of their respective times.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.10-11 - The son of Sa\'eckalp\'e4 was known as Sa\'eckalpa, and from him lust was born. The sons of Vasu were known as the eight Vasus. Just hear their names from me: Dro\'eba, Pr\'e4\'eba, Dhruva, Arka, Agni, Do\'f1a, V\'e4
stu and Vibh\'e4vasu. From Abhimati, the wife of the Vasu named Dro\'eba, were generated the sons named Har\'f1a, \'c7oka, Bhaya and so on.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.12 - \'dcrjasvat\'e9, the wife of Pr\'e4\'eba, gave birth to three sons, named Saha, \'c4yus and Purojava. The wife of Dhruva was known as Dhara\'ebi, and from her womb various cities took birth.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.13 - From the womb of V\'e4san\'e4, the wife of Arka, came many sons, headed by Tar\'f1a. Dh\'e4r\'e4, the wife of the Vasu named Agni, gave birth to many sons, headed by Dravi\'ebaka.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.14 - From K\'e5ttik\'e4, another wife of Agni, came the son named Skanda, K\'e4rttikeya, whose sons were headed by Vi\'e7\'e4kha. From the womb of \'c7arvar\'e9, the wife of the Vasu named Do\'f1a, came the son named \'c7i
\'e7um\'e4ra, who was an expansion of the Supreme Personality of Godhead.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.15 - From \'c4\'ecgiras\'e9, the wife of the Vasu named V\'e4stu, was born the great architect Vi\'e7vakarm\'e4. Vi\'e7vakarm\'e4 became the husband of \'c4k\'e5t\'e9, from whom the Manu named C\'e4k\'f1u\'f1
a was born. The sons of Manu were known as the Vi\'e7vadevas and S\'e4dhyas.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.16 - \'dc\'f1\'e4, the wife of Vibh\'e4vasu, gave birth to three sons-Vyu\'f1\'f6a, Roci\'f1a and \'c4tapa. From \'c4tapa came Pa\'efcay\'e4ma, the span of day, who awakens all living entities to material activities.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.17-18 - Sar\'fcp\'e4, the wife of Bh\'fcta, gave birth to the ten million Rudras, of whom the eleven principle Rudras were Raivata, Aja, Bhava, Bh\'e9ma, V\'e4ma, Ugra, V\'e5\'f1\'e4kapi, Ajaikap\'e4t, Ahirbradhna, Bahur\'fc
pa and Mah\'e4n. Their associates, the ghosts and goblins, who are very fearful, were born of the other wife of Bh\'fcta.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.19 - The praj\'e4pati A\'ecgir\'e4 had two wives, named Svadh\'e4 and Sat\'e9. The wife named Svadh\'e4 accepted all the Pit\'e4s as her sons, and Sat\'e9 accepted the Atharv\'e4\'ecgirasa Veda as her son.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.20 - K\'e5\'e7\'e4\'e7va had two wives, named Arcis and Dhi\'f1a\'eb\'e4. In the wife named Arcis he begot Dh\'fcmaketu and in Dhi\'f1a\'eb\'e4 he begot four sons, named Veda\'e7ir\'e4, Devala, Vayuna and Manu.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.21-22 - Ka\'e7yapa, who is also named T\'e4rk\'f1ya, had four wives-Vinat\'e4 [Supar\'eb\'e4], Kadr\'fc, Pata\'ecg\'e9 and Y\'e4min\'e9. Pata\'ecg\'e9 gave birth to many kinds of birds, and Y\'e4min\'e9
 gave birth to locusts. Vinat\'e4 [Supar\'eb\'e4] gave birth to Garu\'f2a, the carrier of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, and to An\'fcru, or Aru\'eba, the chariot driver of the sun-god. Kadr\'fc gave birth to different varieties of serpents.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.23 - O Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Par\'e9k\'f1it, best of the Bh\'e4ratas, the constellations named K\'e5ttik\'e4 were all wives of the moon-god. However, because Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1
a had cursed him to suffer from a disease causing gradual destruction, the moon-god could not beget children in any of his wives.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.24-26 - Thereafter the King of the moon pacified Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a with courteous words and thus regained the portions of light he had lost during his disease. Nevertheless he could not beget children. The moon 
loses his shining power during the dark fortnight, and in the bright fortnight it is manifest again. O King Par\'e9k\'f1it, now please hear from me the names of Ka\'e7
yapa's wives, from whose wombs the population of the entire universe has come. They are the mothers of almost all the population of the entire universe, and their names are very auspicious to hear. They are Aditi, Diti, Danu, K\'e4\'f1\'f6h\'e4, Ari\'f1
\'f6\'e4, Suras\'e4, Il\'e4, Muni, Krodhava\'e7\'e4, T\'e4mr\'e4, Surabhi, Saram\'e4 and Timi. From the womb of Timi all the aquatics took birth, and from the womb of Saram\'e4 the ferocious animals like the tigers and lions took birth.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.27 - My dear King Par\'e9k\'f1it, from the womb of Surabhi the buffalo, cow and other animals with cloven hooves took birth, from the womb of T\'e4mr\'e4 the eagles, vul
tures and other large birds of prey took birth, and from the womb of Muni the angels took birth.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.28 - The sons born of Krodhava\'e7\'e4 were the serpents known as danda\'e7\'fcka, as well as other serpents and the mosquitoes. All the various creepers and trees were born from the womb of Il\'e4. The R\'e4k\'f1
asas, bad spirits, were born from the womb of Suras\'e4.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.29-31 - The Gandharvas were born from the womb of Ari\'f1\'f6\'e4, and animals whose hooves are not split, such as the horse, were born from the womb of K\'e4\'f1\'f6h\'e4. O
 King, from the womb of Danu came sixty-one sons, of whom these eighteen were very important: Dvim\'fcrdh\'e4, \'c7ambara, Ari\'f1\'f6a, Hayagr\'e9va, Vibh\'e4vasu, Ayomukha, \'c7a\'ecku\'e7ir\'e4, Svarbh\'e4nu, Kapila, Aru\'eba, Pulom\'e4, V\'e5\'f1aparv
\'e4, Ekacakra, Anut\'e4pana, Dh\'fcmrake\'e7a, Vir\'fcp\'e4k\'f1a, Vipracitti and Durjaya.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.32 - The daughter of Svarbh\'e4nu named Suprabh\'e4 was married by Namuci. The daughter of V\'e5\'f1aparv\'e4 named \'c7armi\'f1\'f6h\'e4 was given to the powerful King Yay\'e4ti, the son of Nahu\'f1a.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.33-36 - Vai\'e7v\'e4nara, the son of Danu, had four beautiful daughters, named Upad\'e4nav\'e9, Haya\'e7ir\'e4, Pulom\'e4 and K\'e4lak\'e4. Hira\'eby\'e4k\'f1a married Upad\'e4nav\'e9, and Kratu married Haya\'e7ir\'e4
. Thereafter, at the request of Lord Brahm\'e4, Praj\'e4pati Ka\'e7yapa married Pulom\'e4 and K\'e4lak\'e4, the other two daughters of Vai\'e7v\'e4nara. From the wombs of these two wives of Ka\'e7yapa came sixty thousand sons, headed by Niv\'e4
takavaca, who are known as the Paulomas and the K\'e4lakeyas. They were physically very strong and expert in fighting, and their aim was to disturb the sacrifices performed by the g
reat sages. My dear King, when your grandfather Arjuna went to the heavenly planets, he alone killed all these demons, and thus King Indra became extremely affectionate toward him.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.37 - In his wife Si\'e0hik\'e4, Vipracitti begot one hundred and one sons, of whom the eldest is R\'e4hu and the others are the one hundred Ketus. All of them attained positions in the influential planets.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.38-39 - Now please hear me as I describe the descendants of Aditi in chronological order. In this dynasty the Supreme Personality of Godhead N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eb
a descended by His plenary expansion. The names of the sons of Aditi are as follows: Vivasv\'e4n, Aryam\'e4, P\'fc\'f1\'e4, Tva\'f1\'f6\'e4, Savit\'e4, Bhaga, Dh\'e4t\'e4, Vidh\'e4t\'e4, Varu\'eba, Mitra, \'c7atru and Urukrama.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.40 - Sa\'e0j\'ef\'e4, the wife of Vivasv\'e4n, the sun-god, gave birth to the Manu named \'c7r\'e4ddhadeva, and the same fortunate wife also gave birth to the twins Yamar\'e4ja and the River Yamun\'e4. Then Yam\'e9
, while wandering on the earth in the form of a mare, gave birth to the A\'e7vin\'e9-kum\'e4ras.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.41 - Ch\'e4y\'e4, another wife of the sun-god, begot two sons named \'c7anai\'e7cara and S\'e4var\'ebi Manu, and one daughter, Tapat\'e9, who married Sa\'e0vara\'eba.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.42 - From the womb of M\'e4t\'e5k\'e4, the wife of Aryam\'e4, were born many learned scholars. Among them Lord Brahm\'e4 created the human species, which are endowed with an aptitude for self-examination.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.43 - P\'fc\'f1\'e4 had no sons. When Lord \'c7iva was angry at Dak\'f1a, P\'fc\'f1\'e4 had laughed at Lord \'c7iva and shown his teeth. Therefore he lost his teeth and had to live by eating only ground flour.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.44 - Racan\'e4, the daughter of the Daityas, became the wife of Praj\'e4pati Tva\'f1\'f6\'e4. By his semina he begot in her womb two very powerful sons named Sannive\'e7a and Vi\'e7var\'fcpa.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.45 - Although Vi\'e7var\'fcpa was the son of the daughter of their eternal enemies the demons, the demigods accepted him as their priest in accordance with the order of Brahm\'e4
 when they were abandoned by their spiritual master, B\'e5haspati, whom they had disrespected.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }}}{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  6. }{\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid10252087\charrsid10252087 \chftn {\footnote\ftnalt \pard\plain \s1217\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 
\b\f35\fs24\cf13\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid5731824\charrsid10252087 \chftn }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.1 - \'c7r\'e9 \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 said: My dear King, thereafter, at the request of Lord Brahm
\'e4, Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a, who is known as Pr\'e4cetasa, begot sixty daughters in the womb of his wife Asikn\'e9. All the daughters were very affectionate toward their father.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.2 - He gave ten daughters in charity to Dharmar\'e4ja [Yamar\'e4ja], thirteen to Ka\'e7yapa [first twelve and then one more], twenty-seven to the moon-god, and two each to A\'ecgir\'e4, K\'e5\'e7\'e4\'e7va and Bh\'fc
ta. The other four daughters were given to Ka\'e7yapa. [Thus Ka\'e7yapa received seventeen daughters in all.]
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.3 - Now please hear from me the names of all these daughters and their descendants, who filled all the three worlds.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.4 - The ten daughters given to Yamar\'e4ja were named Bh\'e4nu, Lamb\'e4, Kakud, Y\'e4mi, Vi\'e7v\'e4, S\'e4dhy\'e4, Marutvat\'e9, Vasu, Muh\'fcrt\'e4 and Sa\'eckalp\'e4. Now hear the names of their sons.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.5 - O King, a son named Deva-\'e5\'f1abha was born from the womb of Bh\'e4nu, and from him came a son named Indrasena. From the womb of Lamb\'e4 came a son named Vidyota, who generated all the clouds.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.6 - From the womb of Kakud came the son named Sa\'ecka\'f6a, whose son was named K\'e9ka\'f6a. From K\'e9ka\'f6a came the demigods named Durg\'e4. From Y\'e4mi came the son named Svarga, whose son was named Nandi.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.7 - The sons of Vi\'e7v\'e4 were the Vi\'e7vadevas, who had no progeny. From the womb of S\'e4dhy\'e4 came the S\'e4dhyas, who had a son named Arthasiddhi.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.8 - The two sons who took birth from the womb of Marutvat\'e9 were Marutv\'e4n and Jayanta. Jayanta, who is an expansion of Lord V\'e4sudeva, is known as Upendra.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.9 - The demigods named the Mauh\'fcrtikas took birth from the womb of Muh\'fcrt\'e4. These demigods deliver the results of actions to the living entities of their respective times.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.10-11 - The son of Sa\'eckalp\'e4 was known as Sa\'eckalpa, and from him lust was born. The sons of Vasu were known as the eight Vasus. Just hear their names from me: Dro\'eba, Pr\'e4\'eba, Dhruva, Arka, Agni, Do\'f1a, V\'e4
stu and Vibh\'e4vasu. From Abhimati, the wife of the Vasu named Dro\'eba, were generated the sons named Har\'f1a, \'c7oka, Bhaya and so on.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.12 - \'dcrjasvat\'e9, the wife of Pr\'e4\'eba, gave birth to three sons, named Saha, \'c4yus and Purojava. The wife of Dhruva was known as Dhara\'ebi, and from her womb various cities took birth.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.13 - From the womb of V\'e4san\'e4, the wife of Arka, came many sons, headed by Tar\'f1a. Dh\'e4r\'e4, the wife of the Vasu named Agni, gave birth to many sons, headed by Dravi\'ebaka.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.14 - From K\'e5ttik\'e4, another wife of Agni, came the son named Skanda, K\'e4rttikeya, whose sons were headed by Vi\'e7\'e4kha. From the womb of \'c7arvar\'e9, the wife of the Vasu named Do\'f1a, came the son named \'c7i
\'e7um\'e4ra, who was an expansion of the Supreme Personality of Godhead.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.15 - From \'c4\'ecgiras\'e9, the wife of the Vasu named V\'e4stu, was born the great architect Vi\'e7vakarm\'e4. Vi\'e7vakarm\'e4 became the husband of \'c4k\'e5t\'e9, from whom the Manu named C\'e4k\'f1u\'f1
a was born. The sons of Manu were known as the Vi\'e7vadevas and S\'e4dhyas.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.16 - \'dc\'f1\'e4, the wife of Vibh\'e4vasu, gave birth to three sons-Vyu\'f1\'f6a, Roci\'f1a and \'c4tapa. From \'c4tapa came Pa\'efcay\'e4ma, the span of day, who awakens all living entities to material activities.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.17-18 - Sar\'fcp\'e4, the wife of Bh\'fcta, gave birth to the ten million Rudras, of whom the eleven principle Rudras were Raivata, Aja, Bhava, Bh\'e9ma, V\'e4ma, Ugra, V\'e5\'f1\'e4kapi, Ajaikap\'e4t, Ahirbradhna, Bahur\'fc
pa and Mah\'e4n. Their associates, the ghosts and goblins, who are very fearful, were born of the other wife of Bh\'fcta.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.19 - The praj\'e4pati A\'ecgir\'e4 had two wives, named Svadh\'e4 and Sat\'e9. The wife named Svadh\'e4 accepted all the Pit\'e4s as her sons, and Sat\'e9 accepted the Atharv\'e4\'ecgirasa Veda as her son.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.20 - K\'e5\'e7\'e4\'e7va had two wives, named Arcis and Dhi\'f1a\'eb\'e4. In the wife named Arcis he begot Dh\'fcmaketu and in Dhi\'f1a\'eb\'e4 he begot four sons, named Veda\'e7ir\'e4, Devala, Vayuna and Manu.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.21-22 - Ka\'e7yapa, who is also named T\'e4rk\'f1ya, had four wives-Vinat\'e4 [Supar\'eb\'e4], Kadr\'fc, Pata\'ecg\'e9 and Y\'e4min\'e9. Pata\'ecg\'e9 gave birth to many kinds of birds, and Y\'e4min\'e9
 gave birth to locusts. Vinat\'e4 [Supar\'eb\'e4] gave birth to Garu\'f2a, the carrier of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, and to An\'fcru, or Aru\'eba, the chariot driver of the sun-god. Kadr\'fc gave birth to different varieties of serpents.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.23 - O Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Par\'e9k\'f1it, best of the Bh\'e4ratas, the constellations named K\'e5ttik\'e4 were all wives of the moon-god. However, because Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1
a had cursed him to suffer from a disease causing gradual destruction, the moon-god could not beget children in any of his wives.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.24-26 - Thereafter the King of the moon pacified Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a with courteous words and thus regained the portions of light he had lost during his disease. Nevertheless he could not b
eget children. The moon loses his shining power during the dark fortnight, and in the bright fortnight it is manifest again. O King Par\'e9k\'f1it, now please hear from me the names of Ka\'e7
yapa's wives, from whose wombs the population of the entire universe has come. They are the mothers of almost all the population of the entire universe, and their names are very auspicious to hear. They are Aditi, Diti, Danu, K\'e4\'f1\'f6h\'e4, Ari\'f1
\'f6\'e4, Suras\'e4, Il\'e4, Muni, Krodhava\'e7\'e4, T\'e4mr\'e4, Surabhi, Saram\'e4 and Timi. From the womb of Timi all the aquatics took birth, and from the womb of Saram\'e4 the ferocious animals like the tigers and lions took birth.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.27 - My dear King Par\'e9k\'f1it, from the womb of Surabhi the buffalo, cow and other animals with cloven hooves took birth, from the womb of T\'e4mr\'e4
 the eagles, vultures and other large birds of prey took birth, and from the womb of Muni the angels took birth.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.28 - The sons born of Krodhava\'e7\'e4 were the serpents known as danda\'e7\'fcka, as well as other serpents and the mosquitoes. All the various creepers and trees were born from the womb of Il\'e4. The R\'e4k\'f1
asas, bad spirits, were born from the womb of Suras\'e4.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.29-31 - The Gandharvas were born from the womb of Ari\'f1\'f6\'e4, and animals whose hooves are not split, such as the horse, were born from the womb of K\'e4\'f1\'f6h\'e4
. O King, from the womb of Danu came sixty-one sons, of whom these eighteen were very important: Dvim\'fcrdh\'e4, \'c7ambara, Ari\'f1\'f6a, Hayagr\'e9va, Vibh\'e4vasu, Ayomukha, \'c7a\'ecku\'e7ir\'e4, Svarbh\'e4nu, Kapila, Aru\'eba, Pulom\'e4, V\'e5\'f1
aparv\'e4, Ekacakra, Anut\'e4pana, Dh\'fcmrake\'e7a, Vir\'fcp\'e4k\'f1a, Vipracitti and Durjaya.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.32 - The daughter of Svarbh\'e4nu named Suprabh\'e4 was married by Namuci. The daughter of V\'e5\'f1aparv\'e4 named \'c7armi\'f1\'f6h\'e4 was given to the powerful King Yay\'e4ti, the son of Nahu\'f1a.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.33-36 - Vai\'e7v\'e4nara, the son of Danu, had four beautiful daughters, named Upad\'e4nav\'e9, Haya\'e7ir\'e4, Pulom\'e4 and K\'e4lak\'e4. Hira\'eby\'e4k\'f1a married Upad\'e4nav\'e9, and Kratu married Haya\'e7ir\'e4
. Thereafter, at the request of Lord Brahm\'e4, Praj\'e4pati Ka\'e7yapa married Pulom\'e4 and K\'e4lak\'e4, the other two daughters of Vai\'e7v\'e4nara. From the wombs of these two wives of Ka\'e7yapa came sixty thousand sons, headed by Niv\'e4
takavaca, who are known as the Paulomas and the K\'e4lakeyas. They were physically very strong and expert in fighting, and their aim was to disturb the sacri
fices performed by the great sages. My dear King, when your grandfather Arjuna went to the heavenly planets, he alone killed all these demons, and thus King Indra became extremely affectionate toward him.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.37 - In his wife Si\'e0hik\'e4, Vipracitti begot one hundred and one sons, of whom the eldest is R\'e4hu and the others are the one hundred Ketus. All of them attained positions in the influential planets.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.38-39 - Now please hear me as I describe the descendants of Aditi in chronological order. In this dynasty the Supreme Personality of Godhead N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eb
a descended by His plenary expansion. The names of the sons of Aditi are as follows: Vivasv\'e4n, Aryam\'e4, P\'fc\'f1\'e4, Tva\'f1\'f6\'e4, Savit\'e4, Bhaga, Dh\'e4t\'e4, Vidh\'e4t\'e4, Varu\'eba, Mitra, \'c7atru and Urukrama.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.40 - Sa\'e0j\'ef\'e4, the wife of Vivasv\'e4n, the sun-god, gave birth to the Manu named \'c7r\'e4ddhadeva, and the same fortunate wife also gave birth to the twins Yamar\'e4ja and the River Yamun\'e4. Then Yam\'e9
, while wandering on the earth in the form of a mare, gave birth to the A\'e7vin\'e9-kum\'e4ras.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.41 - Ch\'e4y\'e4, another wife of the sun-god, begot two sons named \'c7anai\'e7cara and S\'e4var\'ebi Manu, and one daughter, Tapat\'e9, who married Sa\'e0vara\'eba.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.42 - From the womb of M\'e4t\'e5k\'e4, the wife of Aryam\'e4, were born many learned scholars. Among them Lord Brahm\'e4 created the human species, which are endowed with an aptitude for self-examination.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.43 - P\'fc\'f1\'e4 had no sons. When Lord \'c7iva was angry at Dak\'f1a, P\'fc\'f1\'e4 had laughed at Lord \'c7iva and shown his teeth. Therefore he lost his teeth and had to live by eating only ground flour.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.44 - Racan\'e4, the daughter of the Daityas, became the wife of Praj\'e4pati Tva\'f1\'f6\'e4. By his semina he begot in her womb two very powerful sons named Sannive\'e7a and Vi\'e7var\'fcpa.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.45 - Although Vi\'e7var\'fcpa was the son of the daughter of their eternal enemies the demons, the demigods accepted him as their priest in accordance with the order of Brahm\'e4
 when they were abandoned by their spiritual master, B\'e5haspati, whom they had disrespected.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }}}{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  }{\f35\fs28\cf2\insrsid10252087 The Progeny of the Daughters of Dak\'f1a
\par }\pard \s97\ql \fi-605\li720\ri720\nowidctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp20\brdrcf14 \brdrl\brdrs\brdrw45\brsp20\brdrcf13 \tx1080\faauto\outlinelevel0\rin720\lin720\rtlgutter\itap0\pararsid10252087 \shading10000\cfpat8 {\fs28\cf2\insrsid10252087   \bullet 
  }{\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid10252087\charrsid10252087 \chftn {\footnote\ftnalt \pard\plain \s1217\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\f35\fs24\cf13\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {
\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid5731824\charrsid10252087 \chftn }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.1 - \'c7r\'e9 \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 said: My dear King, thereafter, at the request of Lord Brahm\'e4, Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a, who is known as Pr\'e4
cetasa, begot sixty daughters in the womb of his wife Asikn\'e9. All the daughters were very affectionate toward their father.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.2 - He gave ten daughters in charity to Dharmar\'e4ja [Yamar\'e4ja], thirteen to Ka\'e7yapa [first twelve and then one more], twenty-seven to the moon-god, and two each to A\'ecgir\'e4, K\'e5\'e7\'e4\'e7va and Bh\'fc
ta. The other four daughters were given to Ka\'e7yapa. [Thus Ka\'e7yapa received seventeen daughters in all.]
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.3 - Now please hear from me the names of all these daughters and their descendants, who filled all the three worlds.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.4 - The ten daughters given to Yamar\'e4ja were named Bh\'e4nu, Lamb\'e4, Kakud, Y\'e4mi, Vi\'e7v\'e4, S\'e4dhy\'e4, Marutvat\'e9, Vasu, Muh\'fcrt\'e4 and Sa\'eckalp\'e4. Now hear the names of their sons.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.5 - O King, a son named Deva-\'e5\'f1abha was born from the womb of Bh\'e4nu, and from him came a son named Indrasena. From the womb of Lamb\'e4 came a son named Vidyota, who generated all the clouds.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.6 - From the womb of Kakud came the son named Sa\'ecka\'f6a, whose son was named K\'e9ka\'f6a. From K\'e9ka\'f6a came the demigods named Durg\'e4. From Y\'e4mi came the son named Svarga, whose son was named Nandi.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.7 - The sons of Vi\'e7v\'e4 were the Vi\'e7vadevas, who had no progeny. From the womb of S\'e4dhy\'e4 came the S\'e4dhyas, who had a son named Arthasiddhi.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.8 - The two sons who took birth from the womb of Marutvat\'e9 were Marutv\'e4n and Jayanta. Jayanta, who is an expansion of Lord V\'e4sudeva, is known as Upendra.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.9 - The demigods named the Mauh\'fcrtikas took birth from the womb of Muh\'fcrt\'e4. These demigods deliver the results of actions to the living entities of their respective times.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.10-11 - The son of Sa\'eckalp\'e4 was known as Sa\'eckalpa, and from him lust was born. The sons of Vasu were known as the eight Vasus. Just hear their names from me: Dro\'eba, Pr\'e4\'eba, Dhruva, Arka, Agni, Do\'f1a, V\'e4
stu and Vibh\'e4vasu. From Abhimati, the wife of the Vasu named Dro\'eba, were generated the sons named Har\'f1a, \'c7oka, Bhaya and so on.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.12 - \'dcrjasvat\'e9, the wife of Pr\'e4\'eba, gave birth to three sons, named Saha, \'c4yus and Purojava. The wife of Dhruva was known as Dhara\'ebi, and from her womb various cities took birth.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.13 - From the womb of V\'e4san\'e4, the wife of Arka, came many sons, headed by Tar\'f1a. Dh\'e4r\'e4, the wife of the Vasu named Agni, gave birth to many sons, headed by Dravi\'ebaka.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.14 - From K\'e5ttik\'e4, another wife of Agni, came the son named Skanda, K\'e4rttikeya, whose sons were headed by Vi\'e7\'e4kha. From the womb of \'c7arvar\'e9, the wife of the Vasu named Do\'f1a, came the son named \'c7i
\'e7um\'e4ra, who was an expansion of the Supreme Personality of Godhead.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.15 - From \'c4\'ecgiras\'e9, the wife of the Vasu named V\'e4stu, was born the great architect Vi\'e7vakarm\'e4. Vi\'e7vakarm\'e4 became the husband of \'c4k\'e5t\'e9, from whom the Manu named C\'e4k\'f1u\'f1
a was born. The sons of Manu were known as the Vi\'e7vadevas and S\'e4dhyas.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.16 - \'dc\'f1\'e4, the wife of Vibh\'e4vasu, gave birth to three sons-Vyu\'f1\'f6a, Roci\'f1a and \'c4tapa. From \'c4tapa came Pa\'efcay\'e4ma, the span of day, who awakens all living entities to material activities.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.17-18 - Sar\'fcp\'e4, the wife of Bh\'fcta, gave birth to the ten million Rudras, of whom the eleven principle Rudras were Raivata, Aja, Bhava, Bh\'e9ma, V\'e4ma, Ugra, V\'e5\'f1\'e4kapi, Ajaikap\'e4t, Ahirbradhna, Bahur\'fc
pa and Mah\'e4n. Their associates, the ghosts and goblins, who are very fearful, were born of the other wife of Bh\'fcta.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.19 - The praj\'e4pati A\'ecgir\'e4 had two wives, named Svadh\'e4 and Sat\'e9. The wife named Svadh\'e4 accepted all the Pit\'e4s as her sons, and Sat\'e9 accepted the Atharv\'e4\'ecgirasa Veda as her son.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.20 - K\'e5\'e7\'e4\'e7va had two wives, named Arcis and Dhi\'f1a\'eb\'e4. In the wife named Arcis he begot Dh\'fcmaketu and in Dhi\'f1a\'eb\'e4 he begot four sons, named Veda\'e7ir\'e4, Devala, Vayuna and Manu.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.21-22 - Ka\'e7yapa, who is also named T\'e4rk\'f1ya, had four wives-Vinat\'e4 [Supar\'eb\'e4], Kadr\'fc, Pata\'ecg\'e9 and Y\'e4min\'e9. Pata\'ecg\'e9 gave birth to many kinds of birds, and Y\'e4min\'e9
 gave birth to locusts. Vinat\'e4 [Supar\'eb\'e4] gave birth to Garu\'f2a, the carrier of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, and to An\'fcru, or Aru\'eba, the chariot driver of the sun-god. Kadr\'fc gave birth to different varieties of serpents.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.23 - O Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Par\'e9k\'f1it, best of the Bh\'e4ratas, the constellations named K\'e5ttik\'e4 were all wives of the moon-god. However, because Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a had cursed him to suffer from a disease c
ausing gradual destruction, the moon-god could not beget children in any of his wives.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.24-26 - Thereafter the King of the moon pacified Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a with courteous words and thus regained the portions of light he had lost during his disease. Nev
ertheless he could not beget children. The moon loses his shining power during the dark fortnight, and in the bright fortnight it is manifest again. O King Par\'e9k\'f1it, now please hear from me the names of Ka\'e7
yapa's wives, from whose wombs the population of the entire universe has come. They are the mothers of almost all the population of the entire universe, and their names are very auspicious to hear. They are Aditi, Diti, Danu, K\'e4\'f1\'f6h\'e4, Ari\'f1
\'f6\'e4, Suras\'e4, Il\'e4, Muni, Krodhava\'e7\'e4, T\'e4mr\'e4, Surabhi, Saram\'e4 and Timi. From the womb of Timi all the aquatics took birth, and from the womb of Saram\'e4 the ferocious animals like the tigers and lions took birth.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.27 - My dear King Par\'e9k\'f1it, from the womb of Surabhi the buffalo, cow and other animals with cloven hooves took birth, from the womb of T\'e4mr\'e4
 the eagles, vultures and other large birds of prey took birth, and from the womb of Muni the angels took birth.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.28 - The sons born of Krodhava\'e7\'e4 were the serpents known as danda\'e7\'fcka, as well as other serpents and the mosquitoes. All the various creepers and trees were born from the womb of Il\'e4. The R\'e4k\'f1
asas, bad spirits, were born from the womb of Suras\'e4.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.29-31 - The Gandharvas were born from the womb of Ari\'f1\'f6\'e4, and animals whose hooves are not split, such as the horse, were born from the womb of K\'e4\'f1\'f6h\'e4
. O King, from the womb of Danu came sixty-one sons, of whom these eighteen were very important: Dvim\'fcrdh\'e4, \'c7ambara, Ari\'f1\'f6a, Hayagr\'e9va, Vibh\'e4vasu, Ayomukha, \'c7a\'ecku\'e7ir\'e4, Svarbh\'e4nu, Kapila, Aru\'eba, Pulom\'e4, V\'e5\'f1
aparv\'e4, Ekacakra, Anut\'e4pana, Dh\'fcmrake\'e7a, Vir\'fcp\'e4k\'f1a, Vipracitti and Durjaya.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.32 - The daughter of Svarbh\'e4nu named Suprabh\'e4 was married by Namuci. The daughter of V\'e5\'f1aparv\'e4 named \'c7armi\'f1\'f6h\'e4 was given to the powerful King Yay\'e4ti, the son of Nahu\'f1a.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.33-36 - Vai\'e7v\'e4nara, the son of Danu, had four beautiful daughters, named Upad\'e4nav\'e9, Haya\'e7ir\'e4, Pulom\'e4 and K\'e4lak\'e4. Hira\'eby\'e4k\'f1a married Upad\'e4nav\'e9, and Kratu married Haya\'e7ir\'e4
. Thereafter, at the request of Lord Brahm\'e4, Praj\'e4pati Ka\'e7yapa married Pulom\'e4 and K\'e4lak\'e4, the other two daughters of Vai\'e7v\'e4nara. From the wombs of these two wives of Ka\'e7yapa came sixty thousand sons, headed by Niv\'e4
takavaca, who are known as the Paulomas and the K\'e4lakeyas. They were physically very strong and expert in fighting, and their aim 
was to disturb the sacrifices performed by the great sages. My dear King, when your grandfather Arjuna went to the heavenly planets, he alone killed all these demons, and thus King Indra became extremely affectionate toward him.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.37 - In his wife Si\'e0hik\'e4, Vipracitti begot one hundred and one sons, of whom the eldest is R\'e4hu and the others are the one hundred Ketus. All of them attained positions in the influential planets.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.38-39 - Now please hear me as I describe the descendants of Aditi in chronological order. In this dynasty the Supreme Personality of Godhead N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eb
a descended by His plenary expansion. The names of the sons of Aditi are as follows: Vivasv\'e4n, Aryam\'e4, P\'fc\'f1\'e4, Tva\'f1\'f6\'e4, Savit\'e4, Bhaga, Dh\'e4t\'e4, Vidh\'e4t\'e4, Varu\'eba, Mitra, \'c7atru and Urukrama.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.40 - Sa\'e0j\'ef\'e4, the wife of Vivasv\'e4n, the sun-god, gave birth to the Manu named \'c7r\'e4ddhadeva, and the same fortunate wife also gave birth to the twins Yamar\'e4ja and the River Yamun\'e4. Then Yam\'e9
, while wandering on the earth in the form of a mare, gave birth to the A\'e7vin\'e9-kum\'e4ras.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.41 - Ch\'e4y\'e4, another wife of the sun-god, begot two sons named \'c7anai\'e7cara and S\'e4var\'ebi Manu, and one daughter, Tapat\'e9, who married Sa\'e0vara\'eba.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.42 - From the womb of M\'e4t\'e5k\'e4, the wife of Aryam\'e4, were born many learned scholars. Among them Lord Brahm\'e4 created the human species, which are endowed with an aptitude for self-examination.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.43 - P\'fc\'f1\'e4 had no sons. When Lord \'c7iva was angry at Dak\'f1a, P\'fc\'f1\'e4 had laughed at Lord \'c7iva and shown his teeth. Therefore he lost his teeth and had to live by eating only ground flour.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.44 - Racan\'e4, the daughter of the Daityas, became the wife of Praj\'e4pati Tva\'f1\'f6\'e4. By his semina he begot in her womb two very powerful sons named Sannive\'e7a and Vi\'e7var\'fcpa.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.6.45 - Although Vi\'e7var\'fcpa was the son of the daughter of their eternal enemies the demons, the demigods accepted him as their priest in accordance with the order of Brahm\'e4
 when they were abandoned by their spiritual master, B\'e5haspati, whom they had disrespected.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }}}{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 Translations 1-45
\par }\pard \s97\ql \fi-605\li720\ri720\nowidctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp20\brdrcf14 \brdrl\brdrs\brdrw45\brsp20\brdrcf13 \tx1080\faauto\rin720\lin720\rtlgutter\itap0 \shading10000\cfpat16 {\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid10252087\charrsid10252087 \chftn 
{\footnote\ftnalt \pard\plain \s1217\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \shading10000\cfpat16 \b\f35\fs24\cf13\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid5731824\charrsid10252087 \chftn }{
\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.1 - Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Par\'e9k\'f1it inquired from \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9: O great sage, why did the spiritual master of the demigods, B\'e5
haspati, reject the demigods, who were his own disciples? What offense did the demigods commit against their spiritual master? Please describe to me this incident.
\par }\pard \s1217\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.2-8 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9
 said: O King, once upon a time, the King of heaven, Indra, being extremely proud because of his great opulence of the three worlds, transgressed the law of Vedic etiquette. Seated on his throne, he was surrounded by the Maruts, Vasus, Rudras, \'c4
dityas, \'c5bhus, Vi\'e7vadevas, S\'e4dhyas, A\'e7vin\'e9-kum\'e4ras, Siddhas, C\'e4ra\'ebas and Gandharvas and by great saintly persons. Also surrounding him were the Vidy\'e4dharas, Apsar\'e4s, Kinnaras, Patagas [birds] and Uragas [snakes]. A
ll of them were offering Indra their respects and services, and the Apsar\'e4
s and Gandharvas were dancing and singing with very sweet musical instruments. Over Indra's head was a white umbrella as effulgent as the full moon. Fanned by yak-tail whisks and ser
ved with all the paraphernalia of a great king, Indra was sitting with his wife, \'c7ac\'e9dev\'e9, who occupied half the throne, when the great sage B\'e5haspati appeared in that assembly. B\'e5
haspati, the best of the sages, was the spiritual master of Indra and the de
migods and was respected by the demigods and demons alike. Nevertheless, although Indra saw his spiritual master before him, he did not rise from his own seat or offer a seat to his spiritual master, nor did Indra offer him a respectful welcome. Indra did
 nothing to show him respect.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.9 - B\'e5haspati knew everything that would happen in the future. Seeing Indra's transgression of etiquette, he completely understood that Indra was puffed up by his material opulence. Although able to curse Indra, he did 
not do so. Instead, he left the assembly and in silence returned to his home.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.10 - Indra, the King of heaven, could immediately understand his mistake. Realizing he had disrespected his spiritual master, he condemned himself in the presence of all the members of the assembly.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.11 - Alas, what a regrettable deed I have committed because of my lack of intelligence and my pride in my material opulences. I failed to show respect to my spiritual master when he entered this assembly, and thus I hav
e insulted him.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 
SB 6.7.12 - Although I am King of the demigods, who are situated in the mode of goodness, I was proud of a little opulence and polluted by false ego. Under the circumstances, who in this world would accept such riches at the risk of fallin
g down ? Alas! I condemn my wealth and opulence.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.13 - If a person says, "One who is situated on the exalted throne of a king should not stand up to show respect to another king or a br\'e4hma\'eba," it is to be understood that he does not know the superi
or religious principles.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.14 - Leaders who have fallen into ignorance and who mislead people by directing them to the path of destruction [as described in the previous verse] are, in effect, boarding a stone boat, and so too are those who blindly fo
llow them. A stone boat would be unable to float and would sink in the water with its passengers. Similarly, those who mislead people go to hell, and their followers go with them.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.15 - King Indra said: Therefore with great frankness and without duplicity I shall now bow my head at the lotus feet of B\'e5
haspati, the spiritual master of the demigods. Because he is in the mode of goodness, he is fully aware of all knowledge and is the best of the br\'e4hma\'ebas. Now I shall touch his lotus feet and offer my obeisances unto him to try to satisfy him.

\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.16 - While Indra, the King of the demigods, thought in this way and repented in his own assembly, B\'e5haspati, the most powerful spiritual master, understood his mind. Thus he became invisible to Indra and left home, for B
\'e5haspati was spiritually more powerful than King Indra.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.17 - Although Indra searched vigorously with the assistance of the other demigods, he could not find B\'e5haspati. Then Indra thought, "Alas, my spiritual master has become dissatis
fied with me, and now I have no means of achieving good fortune." Although Indra was surrounded by demigods, he could not find peace of mind.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.18 - Hearing of the pitiable condition of King Indra, the demons, following the instructions of their guru, \'c7ukr\'e4c\'e4rya, equipped themselves with weapons and declared war against the demigods.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.19 - The demigods' heads, thighs and arms and the other parts of their bodies were injured by the sharp arrows of the demons. The demigods, headed by Indra, saw no other course than to immediately approach Lord Brahm\'e4
 with bowed heads for shelter and proper instruction.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.20 - When the most powerful Lord Brahm\'e4 saw the demigods coming toward him, their bodies gravely injured by the arrows of the demons, he pacified them by his great causeless mercy and spoke as follows.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.21 - Lord Brahm\'e4 said: O best of the demigods, unfortunately, because of madness resulting from your material opulence, you failed to receive B\'e5haspati properly when he came to your ass
embly. Because he is aware of the Supreme Brahman and fully in control of his senses, he is the best of the br\'e4hma\'ebas. Therefore it is very astonishing that you have acted impudently toward him.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.22 - Because of your misbehavior toward B\'e5haspati, yo
u have been defeated by the demons. My dear demigods, since the demons were weak, having been defeated by you several times, how else could you, who were so advanced in opulence, be defeated by them?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.23 - O Indra, your enemies, the demons, were extremely weak because of their disrespect toward \'c7ukr\'e4c\'e4rya, but since they have now worshiped \'c7ukr\'e4c\'e4
rya with great devotion, they have again become powerful. By their devotion to \'c7ukr\'e4c\'e4rya, they have increased their strength so much that now they are even able to easily seize my abode from me.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.24 - Because of their firm determination to follow the instructions of \'c7ukr\'e4c\'e4rya, his disciples, the demons, are now unconcerned about the demigods. In fact, kings or others who have determined faith in the 
mercy of br\'e4hma\'ebas, cows and the Supreme Personality of Godhead, K\'e5\'f1\'eba, and who always worship these three are always strong in their position.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.25 - O demigods, I instruct you to approach Vi\'e7var\'fcpa, the son of Tva\'f1\'f6\'e4, and accept him as your guru. He is a pure and very powerful br\'e4hma\'eb
a undergoing austerity and penances. Pleased by your worship, he will fulfill your desires, provided that you tolerate his being inclined to side with the demons.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.26 - \'c7r\'e9la \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: Thus advised by Lord Brahm\'e4 and relieved of their anxiety, all the demigods went to the sage Vi\'e7var\'fcpa, the son of Tva\'f1\'f6\'e4
. My dear King, they embraced him and spoke as follows.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.27 - The demigods said: Beloved Vi\'e7var\'fcpa, may there be all good fortune for you. We, the demigods, have come to your \'e4\'e7
rama as your guests. Please try to fulfill our desires according to the time, since we are on the level of your parents.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.28 - O br\'e4hma\'eba, the highest duty of a son, even though he has sons of his own, is to serve his parents, and what to speak of a son who is a brahmac\'e4r\'e9?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.29-30 - The \'e4c\'e4rya, the spiritual master who teaches all the Vedic knowledge and gives initiation by offering the sacred thread, is the personification of all the Vedas. Similarly, a father personifies Lord Brahm\'e4
; a brother, King Indra; a mother, the planet earth; and a sister, mercy. A guest personifies religious principles, an invited guest personifies the demigod Agni, and all living entities personify Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, the Supreme Personality of Godhead.

\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 
SB 6.7.31 - Dear son, we have been defeated by our enemies, and therefore we are very much aggrieved. Please mercifully fulfill our desires by relieving our distress through the strength of your austerities. Please fulfill our prayers.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.32 - Since you are completely aware of the Supreme Brahman, you are a perfect br\'e4hma\'eb
a, and therefore you are the spiritual master of all orders of life. We accept you as our spiritual master and director so that by the power of your austerity we may easily defeat the enemies who have conquered us.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.33 - The demigods continued: Do not fear criticism for being younger than us. Such etiquette does not apply in regard to Vedic mantras. Except in relationship to Vedic mantras, seniority is deter
mined by age, but one may offer respectful obeisances even to a younger person who is advanced in chanting Vedic mantras. Therefore although you are junior in relationship to us, you may become our priest without hesitation.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.34 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: When all the demigods requested the great Vi\'e7var\'fcpa to be their priest, Vi\'e7var\'fcpa, who was advanced in austerities, was very pleased. He replied to them as follows.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.35 - \'c7r\'e9 Vi\'e7var\'fcpa said: O demigods, although the acceptance of priesthoo
d is decried as causing the loss of previously acquired brahminical power, how can someone like me refuse to accept your personal request? You are all exalted commanders of the entire universe. I am your disciple and must take many lessons from you. There
fore I cannot refuse you. I must agree for my own benefit.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.36 - O exalted governors of various planets, the true br\'e4hma\'eba, who has no material possessions, maintains himself by the profession of accepting \'e7ilo\'efchana. In other words, he picks up grai
ns left in the field and on the ground in the wholesale marketplace. By this means, householder br\'e4hma\'eb
as who actually abide by the principles of austerity and penance maintain themselves and their families and perform all necessary pious activities. A br\'e4hma\'eb
a who desires to achieve happiness by gaining wealth through professional priesthood must certainly have a very low mind. How shall I accept such priesthood?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.37 - All of you are my superiors. Therefore although accepting priesthood is sometimes
 reproachable, I cannot refuse even a small request from you. I agree to be your priest. I shall fulfill your request by dedicating my life and possessions.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.38 - \'c7r\'e9 \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: O King, after making this promise to the demigods, the exalted Vi\'e7var\'fc
pa, surrounded by the demigods, performed the necessary priestly activities with great enthusiasm and attention.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.39 - The opulence of the demons, who are generally known as the enemies of the demigods, was protected by the talents and tactics of \'c7ukr\'e4c\'e4rya, but Vi\'e7var\'fc
pa, who was most powerful, composed a protective prayer known as the N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba-kavaca. By this intelligent mantra, he took away the opulence of the demons and gave it to Mahendra, the King of heaven.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.40 - Vi\'e7var\'fcpa, who was most liberal, spoke to King Indra [Sahasr\'e4k\'f1a] the secret hymn that protected Indra and conquered the military power of the demons.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }}}{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  7. }{\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid10252087\charrsid10252087 \chftn {\footnote\ftnalt \pard\plain \s1217\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 
\b\f35\fs24\cf13\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid5731824\charrsid10252087 \chftn }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.1 - Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Par\'e9k\'f1it inquired from \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9
: O great sage, why did the spiritual master of the demigods, B\'e5haspati, reject the demigods, who were his own disciples? What offense did the demigods commit against their spiritual master? Please describe to me this incident.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.2-8 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 said: O King, once upon a time, the King of hea
ven, Indra, being extremely proud because of his great opulence of the three worlds, transgressed the law of Vedic etiquette. Seated on his throne, he was surrounded by the Maruts, Vasus, Rudras, \'c4dityas, \'c5bhus, Vi\'e7vadevas, S\'e4dhyas, A\'e7vin
\'e9-kum\'e4ras, Siddhas, C\'e4ra\'ebas and Gandharvas and by great saintly persons. Also surrounding him were the Vidy\'e4dharas, Apsar\'e4
s, Kinnaras, Patagas [birds] and Uragas [snakes]. All of them were offering Indra their respects and services, and the Apsar\'e4s and Gandharvas were danci
ng and singing with very sweet musical instruments. Over Indra's head was a white umbrella as effulgent as the full moon. Fanned by yak-tail whisks and served with all the paraphernalia of a great king, Indra was sitting with his wife, \'c7ac\'e9dev\'e9
, who occupied half the throne, when the great sage B\'e5haspati appeared in that assembly. B\'e5
haspati, the best of the sages, was the spiritual master of Indra and the demigods and was respected by the demigods and demons alike. Nevertheless, although Indra saw his spiri
tual master before him, he did not rise from his own seat or offer a seat to his spiritual master, nor did Indra offer him a respectful welcome. Indra did nothing to show him respect.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.9 - B\'e5haspati knew everything that would happen in the future. S
eeing Indra's transgression of etiquette, he completely understood that Indra was puffed up by his material opulence. Although able to curse Indra, he did not do so. Instead, he left the assembly and in silence returned to his home.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.10 - Indra, the King of heaven, could immediately understand his mistake. Realizing he had disrespected his spiritual master, he condemned himself in the presence of all the members of the assembly.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.11 - Alas, what a regrettable deed I have committed because of m
y lack of intelligence and my pride in my material opulences. I failed to show respect to my spiritual master when he entered this assembly, and thus I have insulted him.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.12 - Although I am King of the demigods, who are situated in the mode of good
ness, I was proud of a little opulence and polluted by false ego. Under the circumstances, who in this world would accept such riches at the risk of falling down ? Alas! I condemn my wealth and opulence.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.13 - If a person says, "One who is situated on the exalted throne of a king should not stand up to show respect to another king or a br\'e4hma\'eb
a," it is to be understood that he does not know the superior religious principles.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.14 - Leaders who have fallen into ignorance and who mislead people b
y directing them to the path of destruction [as described in the previous verse] are, in effect, boarding a stone boat, and so too are those who blindly follow them. A stone boat would be unable to float and would sink in the water with its passengers. Si
milarly, those who mislead people go to hell, and their followers go with them.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.15 - King Indra said: Therefore with great frankness and without duplicity I shall now bow my head at the lotus feet of B\'e5haspati, the spiritual master of the demigods. 
Because he is in the mode of goodness, he is fully aware of all knowledge and is the best of the br\'e4hma\'ebas. Now I shall touch his lotus feet and offer my obeisances unto him to try to satisfy him.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.16 - While Indra, the King of the demigods, thought in this way and repented in his own assembly, B\'e5haspati, the most powerful spiritual master, understood his mind. Thus he became invisible to Indra and left home, for B
\'e5haspati was spiritually more powerful than King Indra.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.17 - Although Indra searched vigorously with the assistance of the other demigods, he could not find B\'e5
haspati. Then Indra thought, "Alas, my spiritual master has become dissatisfied with me, and now I have no means of achieving good fortune." Although Indra was surrounded by demigods, he could not find peace of mind.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.18 - Hearing of the pitiable condition of King Indra, the demons, following the instructions of their guru, \'c7ukr\'e4c\'e4rya, equipped themselves with weapons and declared war against the demigods.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.19 - The demigods' heads, thighs and arms and the other parts of their bodies were injured by the sharp arrows of the demons. The demigods, headed by Indra, saw no other course than to immediately approach Lord Brahm\'e4
 with bowed heads for shelter and proper instruction.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.20 - When the most powerful Lord Brahm\'e4 saw the demigods coming toward him, their bodies gravely injured by the arrows of the demons, he pacified them by his great causeless mercy and spoke as follows.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.21 - Lord Brahm\'e4 said: O best of the demigods, unfortunately, because of madness resulting from your material opulence, you failed to receive B\'e5
haspati properly when he came to your assembly. Because he is aware of the Supreme Brahman and fully in control of his senses, he is the best of the br\'e4hma\'ebas. Therefore it is very astonishing that you have acted impudently toward him.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.22 - Because of your misbehavior toward B\'e5haspati, you have been defeated by the demons. My dear demigods, since the demons were weak, having been defeate
d by you several times, how else could you, who were so advanced in opulence, be defeated by them?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.23 - O Indra, your enemies, the demons, were extremely weak because of their disrespect toward \'c7ukr\'e4c\'e4rya, but since they have now worshiped \'c7ukr\'e4c\'e4r
ya with great devotion, they have again become powerful. By their devotion to \'c7ukr\'e4c\'e4rya, they have increased their strength so much that now they are even able to easily seize my abode from me.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.24 - Because of their firm determination to follow the instructions of \'c7ukr\'e4c\'e4
rya, his disciples, the demons, are now unconcerned about the demigods. In fact, kings or others who have determined faith in the mercy of br\'e4hma\'ebas, cows and the Supreme Personality of Godhead, K\'e5\'f1\'eba, and who always worship these 
three are always strong in their position.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.25 - O demigods, I instruct you to approach Vi\'e7var\'fcpa, the son of Tva\'f1\'f6\'e4, and accept him as your guru. He is a pure and very powerful br\'e4hma\'eb
a undergoing austerity and penances. Pleased by your worship, he will fulfill your desires, provided that you tolerate his being inclined to side with the demons.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.26 - \'c7r\'e9la \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: Thus advised by Lord Brahm\'e4 and relieved of their anxiety, all the demigods went to the sage Vi\'e7var\'fcpa, the son of Tva\'f1\'f6\'e4
. My dear King, they embraced him and spoke as follows.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.27 - The demigods said: Beloved Vi\'e7var\'fcpa, may there be all good fortune for you. We, the demigods, have come to your \'e4\'e7rama as your guests. Please try to fulfill our desires accord
ing to the time, since we are on the level of your parents.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.28 - O br\'e4hma\'eba, the highest duty of a son, even though he has sons of his own, is to serve his parents, and what to speak of a son who is a brahmac\'e4r\'e9?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.29-30 - The \'e4c\'e4rya, the spiritual master who teaches all the Vedic knowledge and gives initiation by offering the sacred thread, is the personification of all the Vedas. Similarly, a father personifies Lord Brahm\'e4
; a brother, King Indra; a mother, the planet earth; and a sister, mercy. A guest personifies religious principles, an invited guest personifies the demigod Agni, and all living entities personify Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, the Supreme Personality of Godhead.

\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.31 - Dear son, we have been defeated by our enemies, and therefore we are
 very much aggrieved. Please mercifully fulfill our desires by relieving our distress through the strength of your austerities. Please fulfill our prayers.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.32 - Since you are completely aware of the Supreme Brahman, you are a perfect br\'e4hma\'eba, and 
therefore you are the spiritual master of all orders of life. We accept you as our spiritual master and director so that by the power of your austerity we may easily defeat the enemies who have conquered us.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.33 - The demigods continued: Do not fear
 criticism for being younger than us. Such etiquette does not apply in regard to Vedic mantras. Except in relationship to Vedic mantras, seniority is determined by age, but one may offer respectful obeisances even to a younger person who is advanced in ch
anting Vedic mantras. Therefore although you are junior in relationship to us, you may become our priest without hesitation.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.34 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: When all the demigods requested the great Vi\'e7var\'fcpa to be their priest, Vi\'e7var\'fcpa, who was advanced in austerities, was very pleased. He replied to them as follows.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.35 - \'c7r\'e9 Vi\'e7var\'fcpa said: O demigods, although the acceptance of priesthood is decried as causing the loss of previously acquired brahminical power, how can someone like me re
fuse to accept your personal request? You are all exalted commanders of the entire universe. I am your disciple and must take many lessons from you. Therefore I cannot refuse you. I must agree for my own benefit.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.36 - O exalted governors of various planets, the true br\'e4hma\'eba, who has no material possessions, maintains himself by the profession of accepting \'e7ilo\'ef
chana. In other words, he picks up grains left in the field and on the ground in the wholesale marketplace. By this means, householder br\'e4hma\'eb
as who actually abide by the principles of austerity and penance maintain themselves and their families and perform all necessary pious activities. A br\'e4hma\'eb
a who desires to achieve happiness by gaining wealth through professional priesthood must certainly have a very low mind. How shall I accept such priesthood?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.37 - All of you are my superiors. Therefore although accepting priesthood is sometimes reproachable, I cannot refuse even a small request from you. I agree to be your priest. I shall fulf
ill your request by dedicating my life and possessions.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.38 - \'c7r\'e9 \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: O King, after making this promise to the demigods, the exalted Vi\'e7var\'fcpa, surrounded by the demigods, performed the necessary priestly activities with grea
t enthusiasm and attention.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.39 - The opulence of the demons, who are generally known as the enemies of the demigods, was protected by the talents and tactics of \'c7ukr\'e4c\'e4rya, but Vi\'e7var\'fc
pa, who was most powerful, composed a protective prayer known as the N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba-kavaca. By this intelligent mantra, he took away the opulence of the demons and gave it to Mahendra, the King of heaven.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid5731824 SB 6.7.40 - Vi\'e7var\'fcpa, who was most liberal, spoke to King Indra [Sahasr\'e4k\'f1a] the secret hymn that protected Indra and conquered the military power of the demons.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid5731824 
\par }}}{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  Indra Offends His Spiritual Master, B\'e5haspati.
