\par }\pard\plain \s23\qc \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \i\f35\fs28\cf1\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid10252087 kasya v\'e4 kuta \'e4y\'e4t\'e4\'f9
\par kasm\'e4d asya ni\'f1edhatha
\par ki\'e0 dev\'e4 upadev\'e4 y\'e4
\par y\'fcya\'e0 ki\'e0 siddha-sattam\'e4\'f9
\par }\pard\plain \s1146\qc \li0\ri0\sb360\sa180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\f35\fs24\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\v\fs28\insrsid3943952 SYNONYMS}{\v\fs28\insrsid10252087 
\par }\pard\plain \s3019\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sb240\sa180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid1521817 \f35\fs28\cf9\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 kasya}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash whose servants; }{
\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 v\'e4}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash or; }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 kuta\'f9}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash from where; }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 \'e4y\'e4t\'e4\'f9}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash have you come; }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 
kasm\'e4t}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash what is the reason; }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 asya}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash (the taking away) of this Aj\'e4mila; }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 ni\'f1edhatha}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash are you forbidding; }{
\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 kim}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash whether; }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 dev\'e4\'f9}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash demigods; }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 upadev\'e4\'f9}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash sub-demigods; }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 y
\'e4\'f9}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash who; }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 y\'fcyam}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash all of you; }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 kim}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash whether; }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 siddha-sat-tam\'e4\'f9}{\insrsid10252087 
\emdash the best of the perfect beings, the pure devotees.
\par }\pard\plain \s1187\qc \li0\ri0\sb240\sa120\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\v\f35\fs24\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\v0\fs28\insrsid10252087 TRANSLATION
\par }\pard\plain \s3000\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid741136 \b\f35\fs28\cf13\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid10252087 
Dear sirs, whose servants are you, where have you come from, and why are you forbidding us to touch the body of Aj\'e4mila? Are you demigods from the heavenly planets, are you sub-demigods, or are you the best of devotees?
\par }\pard\plain \s1187\qc \li0\ri0\sb240\sa120\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\v\f35\fs24\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\v0\fs28\insrsid10252087 PURPORT
\par }\pard\plain \s537\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sa86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid3348640 The most significant word used in this verse is }{
\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 siddha-sattam\'e4\'f9,}{\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid3348640  which means "the best of the perfect." In }{\cs2542\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4}{
\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid3348640  (7.3) it is said, }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 manu\'f1y\'e4\'eb\'e4\'e0 sahasre\'f1u ka\'e7cid yatati siddhaye:}{\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid3348640 
 out of millions of persons, one may try to become }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 siddha,}{\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid3348640  perfect\emdash 
or, in other words, self-realized. A self-realized person knows that he is not the body but a spiritual soul (}{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 aha\'e0 brahm\'e4smi}{\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid3348640 
). At the present moment practically everyone is unaware of this fact, but one who understands this has attained perfection and is therefore called }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 siddha.}{\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid3348640 
 When one understands that the soul is part and parcel of the supreme soul and one thus engages in the devotional service of the supreme soul, one becomes }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 siddha-sat-tama.}{
\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid3348640  One is then eligible to live in the Vaiku\'eb\'f6ha planets or K\'e5\'f1\'ebaloka. The word }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 siddha-sattama,}{\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid3348640 
 therefore, refers to a liberated, pure devotee.
\par }\pard\plain \s3005\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sa86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid3824524 \f35\fs28\cf1\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524 Since the Yamad\'fctas are servants of Yamar\'e4
ja, who is also one of the }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 siddha-sattamas,}{\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  they knew that a }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 siddha-sattama}{\insrsid10252087  is above the demigods and sub-d
emigods and, indeed, above all the living entities within this material world. The Yamad\'fctas therefore inquired why the Vi\'f1\'ebud\'fctas were present where a sinful man was going to die.
\par It should also be noted that Aj\'e4mila was not yet dead, for the Yamad\'fctas were trying to snatch the soul from his heart. They could not take the soul, however, and therefore Aj\'e4mila was not yet dead. This will be revealed in later verses. Aj\'e4
mila was simply in an unconscious state when the argument was in progress between the Yamad\'fctas and the Vi\'f1\'ebud\'fctas. The conclusion of the argument was to be a decision regarding who would claim the soul of Aj\'e4mila.
