One who has the power to punish others should not punish everyone. There are innumerable living entities, the majority of whom are in the spiritual world and are }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 nitya-mukta,}{
\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid3348640  everlastingly liberated. There is no question of judging these liberated living beings. Only a small fraction of the living entities, perhaps one fourth, are in the material world. And the major p
ortion of the living entities in the material world\emdash 8,000,000 of the 8,400,000 forms of life\emdash 
are lower than human beings. They are not punishable, for under the laws of material nature they are automatically evolving. Human beings, who are advanced in cons
ciousness, are responsible, but not all of them are punishable. Those engaged in advanced pious activities are beyond punishment. Only those who engage in sinful activities are punishable. Therefore the Vi\'f1\'ebud\'fc
tas particularly inquired about who is punishable and why Yamar\'e4ja has been designated to discriminate between who is punishable and who is not. How is one to be judged? What is the basic principle of authority? These are the questions raised by the Vi
\'f1\'ebud\'fctas.
\par }\pard\plain \s1467\qr \li0\ri0\sb180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid10252087 SB 6.1.40
