\par }\pard\plain \s1146\qc \li0\ri0\sb360\sa180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\f35\fs24\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\v\fs28\insrsid3943952 SYNONYMS}{\v\fs28\insrsid10252087 
\par }\pard\plain \s3019\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sb240\sa180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid1521817 \f35\fs28\cf9\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 pa\'efcabhi\'f9}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash 
with the five working senses (voice, arms, legs, anus and genitals); }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 kurute}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash performs; }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 sva-arth\'e4n}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash his desired interests; }{
\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 pa\'efca}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash the five objects of the senses (sound, form, touch, aroma and taste); }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 veda}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash knows; }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 atha}{\insrsid10252087 
\emdash thus; }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 pa\'efcabhi\'f9}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash by the five senses of perception (hearing, seeing, smelling, tasting and feeling); }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 eka\'f9}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash the one; }{
\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 tu}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash but; }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 \'f1o\'f2a\'e7ena}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash by these fifteen items and the mind; }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 tr\'e9n}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash 
the three categories of experience (happiness, distress and a mixture of both); }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 svayam}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash he, the living entity himself; }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 saptada\'e7a\'f9}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash 
the seventeenth item; }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 a\'e7nute}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash enjoys.
\par }\pard\plain \s1187\qc \li0\ri0\sb240\sa120\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\v\f35\fs24\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\v0\fs28\insrsid10252087 TRANSLATION
\par }\pard\plain \s3000\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid741136 \b\f35\fs28\cf13\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid10252087 Above the five senses of perception, the five working senses and the
 five objects of the senses is the mind, which is the sixteenth element. Above the mind is the seventeenth element, the soul, the living being himself, who, in cooperation with the other sixteen, enjoys the material world alone. The living being enjoys th
ree kinds of situations, namely happy, distressful and mixed.
\par }\pard\plain \s1187\qc \li0\ri0\sb240\sa120\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\v\f35\fs24\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\v0\fs28\insrsid10252087 PURPORT
\par }\pard\plain \s537\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sa86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid3348640 
Everyone engages in work with his hands, legs and other senses just to achieve a certain goal according to his concocted ideas. One tries to enjoy the five sense objects, namely form, sou
nd, taste, aroma and touch, not knowing the actual goal of life, which is to satisfy the Supreme Lord. Because of disobeying the Supreme Lord, one is put into material conditions, and he then tries to improve his situation in a concocted way, not desiring
 
to follow the instructions of the Supreme Personality of Godhead. Nevertheless, the Supreme Lord is so kind that He comes Himself to instruct the bewildered living entity how to act obediently and then gradually return home, back to Godhead, where he can 
attain an eternal, peaceful life of bliss and knowledge. The living entity has a body, which is a very complicated combination of the material elements, and with this body he struggles alone, as indicated in this verse by the words }{
\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 ekas tu.}{\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid3348640  For example, if 
one is struggling in the ocean, he must swim through it alone. Although many other men and aquatics are swimming in the ocean, he must take care of himself because no one else will help him. Therefore this verse indicates that the seventeenth item, the so
ul, must work alone. Although he tries to create society, friendship and love, no one will be able to help him but K\'e5\'f1\'eba, the Supreme Lord. Therefore his only concern should be how to satisfy K\'e5\'f1\'eba. That is also what K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a wants (}{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 sarva-dharm\'e4n parityajya m\'e4m eka\'e0 \'e7ara\'eba\'e0 vraja}{\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid3348640 
). People bewildered by material conditions try to be united, but although they strive for unity among men and nations, all their attempts are futile. Everyone must struggle alone for existence with the many elements of nature. Therefor
e one's only hope, as K\'e5\'f1\'eba advises, is to surrender to Him, for He can help one become free from the ocean of nescience. \'c7r\'e9 Caitanya Mah\'e4prabhu therefore prayed:
\par }\pard\plain \s1462\qc \li0\ri0\sb180\sa180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \i\f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 ayi nanda-tanuja ki\'eckara\'e0\line patita\'e0 m\'e4\'e0 vi\'f1ame bhav\'e4mbudhau
