\par }\pard\plain \s3005\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sa86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid3824524 \f35\fs28\cf1\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid10252087 \'c7r\'e9la Vi\'e7van\'e4tha Cakravart\'e9 \'d6h\'e4
kura's commentary to texts nine and ten of this chapter form a dialogue concerning how one can become free from all sinful reactions simply by chanting the holy name of the Lord.
\par Someone may say, "It may be accepted that by chanting the holy name of the Lord one becomes freed from all the reactions of sinful life. H
owever, if one commits sinful acts in full consciousness, not only once but many, many times, he is unable to free himself from the reactions of such sins even after atoning for them for twelve years or more. How is it possible, then, that simply by once 
chanting the holy name of the Lord one immediately becomes freed from the reactions of such sins?"
\par \'c7r\'e9la Vi\'e7van\'e4tha Cakravart\'e9 \'d6h\'e4kura replies by quoting verses nine and ten of this chapter: "The chanting of the holy name of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu is the best process o
f atonement for a thief of gold or other valuables, for a drunkard, for one who betrays a friend or relative, for one who kills a }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 br\'e4hma\'eba,}{\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524 
 or for one who indulges in sex with the wife of his }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 guru}{\insrsid10252087  or another superior. It is also the best method of atonemen
t for one who murders women, the king or his father, for one who slaughters cows, and for all other sinful men. Simply by chanting the holy name of Lord Vi\'f1\'eb
u, such sinful persons may attract the attention of the Supreme Lord, who therefore considers "Because this man has chanted My holy name, My duty is to give him protection.' "
\par One may atone for sinful life and vanquish all sinful reactions by chanting the holy name, although this is not called atonement. Ordinary atonement may temporarily protect a sinf
ul person, but it does not completely cleanse his heart of the deep-rooted desire to commit sinful acts. Therefore atonement is not as powerful as the chanting of the holy name of the Lord. In the }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 \'e7\'e4stras}{
\insrsid10252087  it is said that if a person only once chants the holy name and completely surrenders unto the lotus feet of the Lord, the Lord immediately considers him His ward and is always inclined to give him protection. This is confirmed by \'c7r
\'e9dhara Sv\'e4m\'e9. Thus when Aj\'e4mila was in great danger of being carried off by the order carriers of Yamar\'e4ja, the Lord immediately sent His personal order carriers to protect him, and because Aj\'e4
mila was freed from all sinful reactions, the Vi\'f1\'ebud\'fctas spoke on his behalf.
\par Aj\'e4mila had named his son N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba, and because he loved the boy very much, he would call him again and again. Although he was calling for his son, the name itself was powerful because the name N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eb
a is not different from the Supreme Lord N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba. When Aj\'e4mila named his son N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba, all the reactions of his sinful life were neutralized, and as he continued calling his son and thus chanting the holy name of N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eb
a thousands of times, he was actually unconsciously advancing in K\'e5\'f1\'eba consciousness.
\par One may argue, "Since he was constantly chanting the name of N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba, how was it possible for him to be associating with a p}{\insrsid8917035 rostitute and thinking of wine?}{\insrsid10252087 
 By his sinful actions he was bringing suffering upon himself again and again, and therefore one may say that his ultimate chanting of N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba was the cause of his being freed. However, his chanting would then have been a }{
\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 n\'e4ma-apar\'e4dha.}{\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 N\'e4mno bal\'e4d yasya hi p\'e4pa-buddhi\'f9:}{\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524 
 one who continues to act sinfully and tries to neutralize his sins by chanting the holy name of the Lord is a }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 n\'e4ma-apar\'e4dh\'e9,}{\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  an offender to the hol
y name. In response it may be said that Aj\'e4mila's chanting was inoffensive because he did not chant the name of N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eb
a with the purpose of counteracting his sins. He did not know that he was addicted to sinful actions, nor did he know that his chanting of the name of N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba was neutralizing them. Thus he did not commit a }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 n\'e4
ma-apar\'e4dha,}{\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  and his repeated chanting of the holy name of N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba while calling his son may be called pure chanting. Because of this pure chanting, Aj\'e4mila unconsciously accumulated the results of }{
\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 bhakti.}{\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524 
 Indeed, even his first utterance of the holy name was sufficient to nullify all the sinful reactions of his life. To cite a logical example, a fig tree does not immediately yield fruits, but in time the fruits are available. Similarly, Aj\'e4
mila's devotional service grew little by little, and therefore although he committed very sinful acts, the reactions did not affect him. In the }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 \'e7\'e4stras}{\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524 
 it is said that if one chants the holy name of the Lord even once, the reactions of past, present
 or future sinful life do not affect him. To give another example, if one extracts the poison fangs of a serpent, this saves the serpent's future victims from poisonous effects, even if the serpent bites repeatedly. Similarly, if a devotee chants the holy
 name even once inoffensively, this protects him eternally. He need only wait for the results of the chanting to mature in due course of time.
\par }\pard\plain \s1178\ql \li0\ri0\sb360\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \i\f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs1998\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 Thus end the Bhaktivedanta purports of the Sixth Canto, Second Chapter, of the}{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  }{\i0\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid6975074 \'c7r\'e9mad-Bh\'e4gavatam}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 , }{\cs1998\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 entitled "Aj\'e4mila Delivered by the Vi\'f1\'ebud\'fctas."
\par }\pard\plain \s20\qr \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf18\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid10252087 SB 6.3: Yamar\'e4ja Instructs His Messengers
\par }\pard\plain \s330\qc \li0\ri0\sb540\sa240\nowidctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw105\brsp140\brdrcf6 \brdrb\brdrs\brdrw105\brsp140\brdrcf6 \faauto\outlinelevel0\rin0\lin0\rtlgutter\itap0\pararsid10252087 \shading10000\cfpat10 
\b\f35\fs36\cf8\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid741136 CHAPTER THREE\line }{\insrsid10252087 Yamar\'e4ja Instructs His Messengers
\par }\pard\plain \s1467\qr \li0\ri0\sb180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid10252087 SB 6.3 Summary
\par }\pard\plain \s3022\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14314150 \f35\fs28\cf1\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid10252087 As related in this chapter, the Yamad\'fctas approached Yamar\'e4
ja, who very exhaustively explained }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 bh\'e4gavata-dharma,}{\insrsid10252087  the religious principle of devotional service. Yamar\'e4ja thus satisfied the Yamad\'fctas, who had been very disappointed. Yamar\'e4
ja said, "Although Aj\'e4mila was calling for his son, he chanted the holy name of the Lord, N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba, and simply by a glimpse of the chanting of the holy name, he immediately achieved the association of Lord Vi\'f1\'eb
u's order carriers, who saved him from your attempt to arrest him. This is quite all right. It is a fact that even a chronically sinful person who chants the holy name of the Lord, alt
hough not completely without offenses, does not take another material birth."
\par By chanting the holy name of the Lord, Aj\'e4mila had met four order carriers of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu. They were very beautiful and had quickly come to rescue him. Yamar\'e4ja now described them. "The Vi\'f1\'ebud\'fc
tas are all pure devotees of the Lord, the Supreme Person in regard to the creation, maintenance and annihilation of this cosmic manifestation. Neither King Indra, Varu\'eba, \'c7iva, Brahm\'e4, the seven }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 \'e5\'f1is}{
\insrsid10252087  nor I myself can understand the transcend
ental activities of the Supreme Lord, who is self-sufficient and beyond the reach of the material senses. With material senses, no one can attain enlightenment about Him. The Lord, the master of the illusory energy, possesses transcendental qualities for 
t
he good fortune of everyone, and His devotees are also qualified in that way. The devotees, concerned only with rescuing the fallen souls from this material world, apparently take birth in different places in the material world just to save the conditione
d souls. If one is somewhat interested in spiritual life, the devotees of the Lord protect him in many ways."
\par Yamar\'e4ja continued, "The essence of }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 san\'e4tana-dharma,}{\insrsid10252087  or eternal religion, is extremely confidential. No one but the Lord Himself can deliver that c
onfidential religious system to human society. It is by the mercy of the Lord that the transcendental system of religion can be understood by His pure devotees, and specifically by the twelve }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 mah\'e4janas}{\insrsid5247587 
\emdash }{\insrsid10252087 Lord Brahm\'e4, N\'e4rada Muni, Lord \'c7iva, the Kum\'e4ras, Kapila, Manu, Prahl\'e4da, Janaka, Bh\'e9\'f1ma, Bali, \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9
 and me. Other learned scholars, headed by Jaimini, are almost always covered by the illusory energy, and therefore they are more or less attracted by the flowery language of the three }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 Vedas,}{\insrsid10252087  namely }{
\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 \'c5g, Yajur}{\insrsid10252087  and }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 S\'e4ma,}{\insrsid10252087  which are called }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 tray\'e9.}{\insrsid10252087 
 Instead of becoming pure devotees, people captivated by the flowery words of these three }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 Vedas}{\insrsid10252087 
 are interested in the Vedic ritualistic ceremonies. They cannot understand the glories of chanting the holy name of 
the Lord. Intelligent persons, however, take to the devotional service of the Lord. When they chant the holy name of the Lord without offenses, they are no longer subject to my rulings. If by chance they commit some sinful act, they are protected by the h
oly name of the Lord because that is where their interest lies. The four weapons of the Lord, especially the club and the Sudar\'e7ana }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 cakra,}{\insrsid10252087 
 always protect the devotees. One who chants, hears or remembers the holy name of the Lord without duplicity, or who pr
ays or offers obeisances to the Lord, becomes perfect, whereas even a learned person may be called to hell if he is bereft of devotional service."
\par After Yamar\'e4ja thus described the glories of the Lord and His devotees, \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 further explained the potency of chanting the holy name and the futility of performing Vedic ritualistic ceremonies and pious activities for atonement.

