\par }\pard\plain \s23\qc \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \i\f35\fs28\cf1\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid10252087 ast\'e9ti n\'e4st\'e9ti ca vastu-ni\'f1\'f6hayor
\par eka-sthayor bhinna-viruddha-dharma\'ebo\'f9
\par avek\'f1ita\'e0 ki\'efcana yoga-s\'e4\'eckhyayo\'f9
\par sama\'e0 para\'e0 hy anuk\'fcla\'e0 b\'e5hat tat
\par }\pard\plain \s1146\qc \li0\ri0\sb360\sa180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\f35\fs24\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\v\fs28\insrsid3943952 SYNONYMS}{\v\fs28\insrsid10252087 
\par }\pard\plain \s3019\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sb240\sa180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid1521817 \f35\fs28\cf9\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 asti}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash there is; }{
\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 iti}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash thus; }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 na}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash not; }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 asti}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash there is; }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 iti}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash 
thus; }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 ca}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash and; }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 vastu-ni\'f1\'f6hayo\'f9}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash professing knowledge of the ultimate cause; }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 eka-sthayo\'f9}{\insrsid10252087 
\emdash with one and the same subject matter, establishing Brahman; }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 bhinna}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash demonstrating different; }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 viruddha-dharma\'ebo\'f9}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash 
and opposing characteristics; }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 avek\'f1itam}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash perceived; }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 ki\'efcana}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash that something which; }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 yoga-s\'e4\'eckhyayo\'f9}{
\insrsid10252087 \emdash of mystic }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 yoga}{\insrsid10252087  and the S\'e4\'eckhya philosophy (analysis of the ways of nature); }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 samam}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash the same; }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 
param}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash transcendental; }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 hi}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash indeed; }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 anuk\'fclam}{\insrsid10252087 \emdash dwelling place; }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 b\'e5hat tat}{\insrsid10252087 
\emdash that ultimate cause.
\par }\pard\plain \s1187\qc \li0\ri0\sb240\sa120\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\v\f35\fs24\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\v0\fs28\insrsid10252087 TRANSLATION
\par }\pard\plain \s3000\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid741136 \b\f35\fs28\cf13\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid10252087 There are two parties\emdash 
namely, the theists and the atheists. The theist, who accepts the Supersoul, finds the spiritual cause through mystic yoga. The S\'e4\'ec
khyite, however, who merely analyzes the material elements, comes to a conclusion of impersonalism and does not accept a supreme cause\emdash whether Bhagav\'e4n, Param\'e4tm\'e4 or eve
n Brahman. Instead, he is preoccupied with the superfluous, external activities of material nature. Ultimately, however, both parties demonstrate the Absolute Truth because although they offer opposing statements, their object is the same ultimate cause. 
They are both approaching the same Supreme Brahman, to whom I offer my respectful obeisances.
\par }\pard\plain \s1187\qc \li0\ri0\sb240\sa120\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\v\f35\fs24\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\v0\fs28\insrsid10252087 PURPORT
\par }\pard\plain \s537\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sa86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 Actually there are two sides to this argument. Some say that the Absolute has no form (}{
\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 nir\'e4k\'e4ra}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 ), and others say that the Absolute has a form (}{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 s\'e4k\'e4ra}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 ). Therefore the word }{\fs28\cf1\insrsid8277221 "}{
\cs1998\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid8277221 form}{\cs1998\fs28\cf1\insrsid8277221 "}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  is the common factor, although some accept it (}{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 asti}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  or }{
\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 astika}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 ) whereas others try to negate it (}{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 n\'e4sti}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  or }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 n\'e4stika}{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 ). Since the devotee considers the word "form" (}{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 \'e4k\'e4ra}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 ) the common factor for both, he offers his respectful obeisances to
 the form, although others may go on arguing about whether the Absolute has a form or not.
\par }\pard\plain \s3005\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sa86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid3824524 \f35\fs28\cf1\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524 In this verse the word }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 
yoga-s\'e4\'eckhyayo\'f9}{\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  is very important. }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 Yoga}{\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  means }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 bhakti-yoga}{\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524 
 because }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 yog\'e9s}{\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  also accept the existence of the all-pervading Supreme Soul and try to see that Supreme Soul within their hearts. As stated in }{\cs2542\i\insrsid10252087 \'c7r\'e9
mad-Bh\'e4gavatam}{\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  (12.13.1), }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 dhy\'e4n\'e4vasthita-tad-gatena manas\'e4 pa\'e7yanti ya\'e0 yogina\'f9.}{\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524 
 The devotee tries to come directly in touch with the Supreme Personality of Godhead, whereas the }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 yog\'e9}{\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  tries to find
 the Supersoul within the heart by meditation. Thus, both directly and indirectly, }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 yoga}{\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  means }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 bhakti-yoga.}{\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  S\'e4
\'eckhya, however, means physical study of the cosmic situation through speculative knowledge. This is generally known as }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 j\'ef\'e4na-\'e7\'e4stra.}{\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  The S\'e4\'eckhyites
 are attached to the impersonal Brahman, but the Absolute Truth is known in three ways. }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 Brahmeti param\'e4tmeti bhagav\'e4n iti \'e7abdyate:}{\cs1998\insrsid10252087  }{\insrsid10252087 
the Absolute Truth is one, but some accept Him as impersonal Brahman, some as the Supersoul existing everywhere, and some as Bhagav\'e4n, the Supreme Personality of Godhead. The central point is the Absolute Truth.
\par Although the impersonalists and personalists fight with one another}{\insrsid13462472 , they focus upon the same Para}{\insrsid10252087 brahman, the same Absolute Truth. In the }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 yoga-\'e7\'e4stras,}{
\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  K\'e5\'f1\'eba is described as follows: }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 k\'e5\'f1\'eba\'e0 pi\'e7a\'ecg\'e4mbaram ambujek\'f1a\'eba\'e0 catur-bhuja\'e0 \'e7a\'eckha-gad\'e4dy-ud\'e4yudham.}{\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524 
 Thus the pleasing appearance of the Supreme Personality of Godhead's bodily features, His limbs and His dress are described. The }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 s\'e4\'eckhya-\'e7\'e4stra,}{\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  however, denies the exi
stence of the Lord's transcendental form. The }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 s\'e4\'eckhya-\'e7\'e4stra}{\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  says that the Supreme Absolute Truth has no hands, no legs and no name: }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 hy an\'e4ma-r\'fc
pa-gu\'eba-p\'e4\'ebi-p\'e4dam acak\'f1ur a\'e7rotram ekam advit\'e9yam api n\'e4ma-r\'fcp\'e4dika\'e0 n\'e4sti.}{\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  The Vedic }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 mantras}{\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  say, }{
\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 ap\'e4\'ebi-p\'e4do javano grah\'e9t\'e4:}{\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524 
 the Supreme Lord has no legs and hands, but He can accept whatever is offered to Him. Actually such statements accept that the Supreme has hands and legs, but deny that He has material hands and legs. This is why the Absolute is called }{
\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 apr\'e4k\'e5ta.}{\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  K\'e5\'f1\'eba, the Supreme Personality of Godhead, has a }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 sac-cid-\'e4nanda-vigraha,}{\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524 
 a form of eternity, knowledge and bliss, not a material form. The S\'e4\'eckhyites, or }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 j\'ef\'e4n\'e9s,}{\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524 
 deny the material form, and the devotees also know very well that the Absolute Truth, Bhagav\'e4n, has no material form.
\par }\pard\plain \s1462\qc \li0\ri0\sb180\sa180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \i\f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 \'e9\'e7vara\'f9 parama\'f9 k\'e5\'f1\'eba\'f9\line sac-cid-\'e4nanda-vigraha\'f9\line 
an\'e4dir \'e4dir govinda\'f9\line sarva-k\'e4ra\'eba-k\'e4ra\'ebam}{\i0\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 
\par }\pard\plain \s135\qj \li0\ri0\sb89\sa89\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs3025\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid9723571 "K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a, who is known as Govinda, is the supreme controller. He has an eternal, blissful, spiritual body. He is the origin of all. He has n
o other origin, for He is the prime cause of all causes." The conception of the Absolute without hands and legs and the conception of the Absolute with hands and legs are apparently contradictory, but they both coincide with the same truth about the Supre
me Absolute Person. Therefore the word }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 vastu-ni\'f1\'f6hayo\'f9,}{\cs3025\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid9723571  which is used herein, indicates that both the }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 yog\'e9s}{
\cs3025\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid9723571  and S\'e4\'eckhyites have faith in the reality, but are arguing about it from the different viewpoints of material and spiritual identities. }{\cs3025\fs28\cf1\insrsid10775694 Parabrahman}{
\cs3025\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid9723571 , or }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 b\'e5hat,}{\cs3025\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid9723571  is the common point. The S\'e4\'eckhyites and }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 yog\'e9s}{
\cs3025\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid9723571  are both situated in that same Brahman, but they differ because of different angles of vision.
\par }\pard\plain \s908\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sa86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs3006\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524 The directions given by the }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 
bhakti-\'e7\'e4stra}{\cs3006\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  point one in the perfect direction because the Supreme Personality of Godhead says in }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4,}{
\cs3006\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 bhakty\'e4 m\'e4m abhij\'e4n\'e4ti:}{\cs1998\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  [}{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 Bg}{\cs1998\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 . }{
\cs3006\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524 18.55] "Only by devotional service am I to be known." The }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 bhaktas}{\cs3006\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524 
 know that the Supreme Person has no material form, whereas the }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 j\'ef\'e4n\'e9s}{\cs3006\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  simply deny the material form. One should therefore take shelter of the }{
\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 bhakti-m\'e4rga,}{\cs3006\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  the path of devotion; then everything will be clear. }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 J\'ef\'e4n\'e9s}{
\cs3006\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  concentrate on the }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid7961013\charrsid7961013 vir\'e4\'f6a}{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 -r\'fcpa,}{\cs3006\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524 
 the gigantic universal form of the Lord. This is a good system in the beginning for those who are extremely materialistic, but there is no need to think continuously of the }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid7961013\charrsid7961013 vir\'e4\'f6a}{
\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 -r\'fcpa.}{\cs3006\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  When Arjuna was shown the }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid7961013\charrsid7961013 vir\'e4\'f6a}{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 -r\'fcpa}{
\cs3006\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  of K\'e5\'f1\'eba, he saw it, but he did not want to see it perpetually. He therefore requested the Lord to return to His original form as two-armed K\'e5\'f1\'eba. In conclusion, le
arned scholars find no contradictions in the devotees' concentration upon the spiritual form of the Lord (}{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 \'e9\'e7vara\'f9 parama\'f9 k\'e5\'f1\'eba\'f9 sac-cid-\'e4nanda-vigraha\'f9}{
\cs3006\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524 ). In this regard, \'c7r\'e9la Madhv\'e4c\'e4rya says that less intelligent nondevotees think that their conclusion 
is the ultimate, but because devotees are completely learned, they can understand that the Supreme Personality of Godhead is the ultimate goal.
\par }\pard\plain \s1467\qr \li0\ri0\sb180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid10252087 SB 6.4.33
\par }\pard\plain \s2995\qc \li0\ri0\sb240\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid10252087 \b\f35\fs28\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid10252087 TEXT 33
