ra alpa chidra sarva-loke g\'e4ya}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  (}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid550855 Cc.}{\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 Madhya }{\cs1998\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid550855 12.51}{
\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 ).}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  In society one will find many }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 sanny\'e4s\'e9s, v\'e4naprasthas, g\'e5hasthas}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  and }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 
brahmac\'e4r\'e9s,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  but if all of them properly live in accordance with their duties, they are understood to be }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 s\'e4dhus.}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a was certainly a }{
\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 s\'e4dhu}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  because he had executed such great austerities that the Supreme Personality of Godhead, Lord Vi\'f1\'eb
u, had appeared before him. Nevertheless, he had a fault-finding mentality. He improperly thought N\'e4rada Muni to be }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 as\'e4dhu,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  or nonsaintly, because N\'e4
rada had foiled his intentions. Desiring to train his sons to become }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 g\'e5hasthas}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  fully equipped with knowledge, Dak\'f1a had sent them to execute austerities by N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba-saras. N
\'e4rada Muni, however, taking advantage of their highly elevated position in austerity, instructed them to become Vai\'f1\'ebavas in the renounced order. This is the duty of N\'e4
rada Muni and his followers. They must show everyone the path of renouncing this material world and returning home, back to Godhead. Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a, however, could not see the exaltedness of the duties N\'e4
rada Muni performed in relation to his sons. Unable to appreciate N\'e4rada Muni's behavior, Dak\'f1a accused N\'e4rada of being }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 as\'e4dhu.
\par }\pard\plain \s3005\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sa86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid3824524 \f35\fs28\cf1\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524 The words }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 bhik\'f1or m\'e4
rga,}{\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  "the path of the renounced order," are very significant in this regard. A }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 sanny\'e4s\'e9}{\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  is called }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 trida\'eb
\'f2i-bhik\'f1u}{\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  because his duty is to beg alms from the homes of }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 g\'e5hasthas}{\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  and to give the }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 g\'e5hasthas}{
\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  spiritual instructions. A }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 sanny\'e4s\'e9}{\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  is allowed to beg from door to door, but a }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 g\'e5hastha}{
\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  cannot do so. }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 G\'e5hasthas}{\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  may earn their living according to the four divisions of spiritual life. A }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 br\'e4hma\'eb
a g\'e5hastha}{\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  may earn his livelihood by becoming a learned scholar and teaching people in general how to worship the Supreme Personality of Godhead. He may also assume the 
duty of worship himself. Therefore it is said that only }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 br\'e4hma\'ebas}{\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  may engage in Deity worship, and they may accept as }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 pras\'e4da}{
\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  whatever people offer the Deity. Although a }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 br\'e4hma\'eba}{\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  may sometimes accept charity, it is not for his personal maintenance but for 
the worship of the Deity. Thus a }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 br\'e4hma\'eba}{\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  does not stock anything for his future use. Similarly, }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 k\'f1atriyas}{\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524 
 may collect taxes from the citizens, and they must also protect the citizens, enforce rules and regulations, and maintain law and order. }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 Vai\'e7yas}{\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  shou
ld earn their livelihood through agriculture and cow protection, and }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 \'e7\'fcdras}{\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  should maintain their livelihood by serving the three higher classes. Unless one becomes a }{
\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 br\'e4hma\'eba,}{\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  one cannot take }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 sanny\'e4sa.}{\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 Sanny\'e4s\'e9s}{
\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  and }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 brahmac\'e4r\'e9s}{\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  may beg alms door to door, but a }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 g\'e5hastha}{\insrsid10252087  cannot.
\par Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a condemned N\'e4rada Muni because N\'e4rada, a }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 brahmac\'e4r\'e9}{\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  who could beg from door to door, had made }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 sanny\'e4s\'e9s}{
\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  of Dak\'f1a's sons, who were being trained to be }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 g\'e5hasthas.}{\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  Dak\'f1a was extremely angry at N\'e4rada because he thought that N\'e4
rada had done him a great injustice. According to Dak\'f1a's opinion, N\'e4rada Muni had misled Dak\'f1a's inexperienced sons (}{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 as\'e4dhv ak\'e4ry arbhak\'e4\'eb\'e4m}{\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524 ). Dak\'f1
a regarded his sons as innocent boys who had been misled when N\'e4rada showed them the renounced order of life. Because of all these considerations, Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a charged that N\'e4rada Muni was }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 as\'e4dhu}{
\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  and should not have adopted the dress of a }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 s\'e4dhu.
\par }{\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524 Sometimes a saintly person is misunderstood by }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 g\'e5hasthas,}{\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  especially when he instructs their young sons to accept K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a consciousness. Generally a }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 g\'e5hastha}{\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  thinks that unless one enters }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 g\'e5hastha}{\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524 
 life he cannot properly enter the renounced order. If a young man immediately adopts the path of the renounced order in accordance with the instructions of N\'e4rada or a mem
ber of his disciplic succession, his parents become very angry. This same phenomenon is occurring in our K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a consciousness movement because we are instructing all the young boys in the Western countries to follow the path of renunciation. We allow }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 g\'e5hastha}{\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  life, but a }{
\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 g\'e5hastha}{\cs3006\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  also follows the path of renunciation. Even a }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 g\'e5hastha}{\insrsid10252087 
 has to give up so many bad habits that his parents think his life has been practically destroyed. We allow no meat-eating, no illicit sex, no gambling and no intoxication, and c
onsequently the parents wonder how, if there are so many no's, one's life can be positive. In the Western countries especially, these four prohibited activities practically constitute the life and soul of the modern population. Therefore parents sometimes
 dislike our movement, just as Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a disliked the activities of N\'e4rada and accused N\'e4
rada of dishonesty. Nevertheless, although parents may be angry at us, we must perform our duty without hesitation because we are in the disciplic succession from N\'e4rada Muni.
\par People addicted to householder life wonder how one can give up the enjoyment of }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 g\'e5hastha}{\insrsid10252087\charrsid3824524  life, which is a concession for sex enjoyment, simply to become a mendicant in K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a consciousness. They do not know that the householder's concession fo
r sex life cannot be regulated unless one accepts the life of a mendicant. The Vedic civilization therefore enjoins that at the end of one's fiftieth year one must give up household life. This is compulsory. However, because modern civilization is misled,
 householders want to remain in family life until death, and therefore they are suffering. In such cases, the disciples of N\'e4rada Muni advise all the members of the younger generation to join the K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a consciousness movement immediately. There is nothing wrong in this.
\par }\pard\plain \s1467\qr \li0\ri0\sb180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid10252087 SB 6.5.37
