\cs3025\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid9723571  are that he is tolerant, merciful and friendly to all living entities. He has no enemies, he is peaceful, he abides by the scriptures, and all his characteristics are sublime." Because N\'e4rada Mun
i is the most elevated of }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 s\'e4dhus,}{\cs3025\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid9723571  devotees, to deliver Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a he silently tolerated the curse. \'c7r\'e9 Caitanya Mah\'e4
prabhu has taught this principle to all His devotees:
\par }\pard\plain \s1462\qc \li0\ri0\sb180\sa180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \i\f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 t\'e5\'eb\'e4d api sun\'e9cena\line taror api sahi\'f1\'ebun\'e4\line am\'e4nin\'e4 m
\'e4nadena\line k\'e9rtan\'e9ya\'f9 sad\'e4 hari\'f9}{\i0\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  
\par }\pard\plain \s3023\qj \li0\ri0\sb89\sa89\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid9723571 \f35\fs28\cf1\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid10252087 "O
ne should chant the holy name of the Lord in a humble state of mind, thinking oneself lower than the straw in the street; one should be more tolerant than a tree, devoid of all sense of false prestige and should be ready to offer all respects to others. I
n such a state of mind one can chant the holy name of the Lord constantly." Following the orders of \'c7r\'e9 Caitanya Mah\'e4
prabhu, one who preaches the glories of the Lord all over the world or all over the universe should be humbler than grass and more tolerant 
than a tree because a preacher cannot live an easygoing life. Indeed, a preacher must face many impediments. Not only is he sometimes cursed, but sometimes he must also suffer personal injury. For example, when Nity\'e4nanda Prabhu went to preach K\'e5
\'f1\'eba consciousness to the two roguish brothers Jag\'e4i and M\'e4dh\'e4i, they injured Him and made His head bleed, but nevertheless, He tolerantly delivered the two rogues, who became perfect Vai\'f1\'eb
avas. This is the duty of a preacher. Lord Jesus Christ even tolerated crucifixion. Therefore the curse against N\'e4rada was not very astonishing, and he tolerated it.
\par }\pard\plain \s3005\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sa86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid3824524 \f35\fs28\cf1\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid10252087 Now, it may be asked why N\'e4rada Muni stayed in the presence of Praj\'e4
pati Dak\'f1a and tolerated all his accusations and curses. Was that for Dak\'f1a's deliverance? The answer is yes. \'c7r\'e9la Vi\'e7van\'e4tha Cakravart\'e9 \'d6h\'e4kura says that after being insulted by Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1a, N\'e4
rada Muni should have left immediately, but he purposely stayed to hear all Dak\'f1a's strong words so that Dak\'f1a might be relieved of his anger. Praj\'e4pati Dak\'f1
a was not an ordinary man; he had accumulated the results of many pious activities. Therefore N\'e4rada Muni expected that after delivering his curse, Dak\'f1
a, satisfied and freed from anger, would repent his misbehavior and thus get a chance to become a Vai\'f1\'ebava and be delivered. When Jag\'e4i and M\'e4dh\'e4i offended Lord Nity\'e4nanda, Lord Nity\'e4
nanda stood tolerantly, and therefore both brothers fell at His lotus feet and repented. Consequently they later became perfect Vai\'f1\'ebavas.
\par }\pard\plain \s1178\ql \li0\ri0\sb360\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \i\f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs1998\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 Thus end the Bhaktivedanta purports of the Sixth Canto, Fifth Chapter, of the}{
