a begot sixty daughters in the womb of his wife Asikn\'e9. These daughters were given in charity to various persons to increase the population. Since these offspring of Dak\'f1a were women, N\'e4
rada Muni did not try to lead them toward the renounced order of life. Thus the daughters were saved from N\'e4rada Muni. Ten of the daughters were given in marriage to Dharmar\'e4ja, thirteen to Ka\'e7
yapa Muni, and twenty-seven to the moon-god, Candra. In this way fifty daughters were distributed, and of the other ten daughters, four were given to Ka\'e7yapa and two each to Bh\'fcta, A\'ecgir\'e4 and K\'e5\'e7\'e4\'e7
va. One should know that it is because of the union of these sixty daughters with various exalted personalities that the entire universe was filled with various kinds of living entities, such as human beings, demigods, demons, beasts, birds and serpents.

