As related in this chapter, Indra, the King of heaven, committed an offense at the feet of his spiritual master, B\'e5haspati. B\'e5haspati therefore left the demigods, who then had no priest. However, at the request of the demigods, Vi\'e7var\'fc
pa, the son of the }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 br\'e4hma\'eba}{\insrsid10252087  Tva\'f1\'f6\'e4, became their priest.
\par Once upon a time, Indra, the King of the demigods, was sitting with his wife \'c7ac\'e9dev\'e9 and being praised by various demigods like the Siddhas, C\'e4ra\'ebas and Gandharvas when B\'e5
haspati, the spiritual master of the demigods, entered the assembly. Indra, being too absorbed in material opulence, forgot himself and did not respect B\'e5haspati, who thus beca
me aware of Indra's pride in his material opulence and immediately disappeared from the assembly to teach him a lesson. Indra became most repentant, understanding that because of his opulence he had forgotten to respect his spiritual master. He left the p
alace to beg pardon from his spiritual master, but could not find B\'e5haspati anywhere.
\par Because of his disrespectful behavior toward his spiritual master. Indra lost all his opulence and was conquered by the demons, who defeated the demigods in a great fight 
and occupied Indra's throne. King Indra, along with the other demigods, later took shelter of Lord Brahm\'e4. Understanding the situation, Lord Brahm\'e4 chastised the demigods for their offense to their spiritual master. Following Lord Brahm\'e4
's orders, the demigods accepted Vi\'e7var\'fcpa, who was a }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 br\'e4hma\'eba}{\insrsid10252087  and the son of Tva\'f1\'f6\'e4, as their priest. Then they performed }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 yaj\'efas}{\insrsid10252087 
 under the priesthood of Vi\'e7var\'fcpa and were able to conquer the demons.
