\par }\pard\plain \s1217\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\f35\fs24\cf13\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs3004\fs28\cf9\insrsid10252087\charrsid6360924 \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 said:}{
\cs3002\fs28\insrsid10252087\charrsid741136  O King, once upon a time, the King of heaven
, Indra, being extremely proud because of his great opulence of the three worlds, transgressed the law of Vedic etiquette. Seated on his throne, he was surrounded by the Maruts, Vasus, Rudras, \'c4dityas, \'c5bhus, Vi\'e7vadevas, S\'e4dhyas, A\'e7vin\'e9
-kum\'e4ras, Siddhas, C\'e4ra\'ebas and Gandharvas and by great saintly persons. Also surrounding him were the Vidy\'e4dharas, Apsar\'e4s, Kinnaras, Patagas }{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 [birds]}{\cs3002\fs28\insrsid10252087\charrsid741136  and Uragas }{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 [snakes]}{\cs3002\fs28\insrsid10252087\charrsid741136 . All of them were offering Indra their respects and services, and the Apsar\'e4s and Gandharvas were dancing 
and singing with very sweet musical instruments. Over Indra's head was a white umbrella as effulgent as the full moon. Fanned by yak-tail whisks and served with all the paraphernalia of a great king, Indra was sitting with his wife, \'c7ac\'e9dev\'e9
, who occupied half the throne, when the great sage B\'e5haspati appeared in that assembly. B\'e5
haspati, the best of the sages, was the spiritual master of Indra and the demigods and was respected by the demigods and demons alike. Nevertheless, although Indra saw his spiritua
l master before him, he did not rise from his own seat or offer a seat to his spiritual master, nor did Indra offer him a respectful welcome. Indra did nothing to show him respect.
\par }\pard\plain \s1467\qr \li0\ri0\sb180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid10252087 SB 6.7.9
