The opulence of the demons, who are generally known as the enemies of the demigods, was protected by the talents and tactics of \'c7ukr\'e4c\'e4rya, but Vi\'e7var\'fcpa, who was most powerful, composed a protective prayer known as the N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eb
a-kavaca. By this intelligent mantra, he took away the opulence of the demons and gave it to Mahendra, the King of heaven.
\par }\pard\plain \s1187\qc \li0\ri0\sb240\sa120\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\v\f35\fs24\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\v0\fs28\insrsid10252087 PURPORT
\par }\pard\plain \s537\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sa86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 The distinction between the demigods (}{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 devas}{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 ) and demons (}{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 asuras}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 ) is that the demigods are all devotees of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu whereas the demons are devotees of demigods like Lord \'c7iva, Goddess K\'e4l\'e9
 and Goddess Durg\'e4. Sometimes the demons are also devotees of Lord Brahm\'e4. For example, Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu was a devotee of Lord Brahm\'e4, R\'e4va\'eba was a devotee of Lord \'c7iva, and Mahi\'f1\'e4sura was a devotee of Goddess Durg\'e4
. The demigods are devotees of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu (}{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 vi\'f1\'ebu-bhakta\'f9 sm\'e5to daiva}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 ), whereas the demons (}{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 \'e4suras tad-viparyaya\'f9}{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 ) are always against the }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 vi\'f1\'ebu-bhaktas,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  or Vai\'f1\'ebavas. To oppose the Vai\'f1\'ebavas, the demons become devotees of Lord \'c7iva, Lord Brahm\'e4, K\'e4l
\'e9, Durg\'e4, and so on. In the days of yore, many long years ago, there was animosity between the }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 devas}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  and the }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 asuras,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 
 and the same spirit still continues, for the devotees of Lord \'c7iva and Goddess Durg\'e4 are always envious of Vai\'f1\'ebavas, who are devotees of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu. This strain between the devotees of Lord \'c7iva and Lord Vi\'f1\'eb
u has always existed. In the higher planetary systems, fights between the demons and the demigods continue for a long, long time.
\par }\pard\plain \s908\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sa86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 Herein we see that Vi\'e7var\'fc
pa made for the demigods a protective covering, saturated with a Vi\'f1\'ebu }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 mantra.}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  Sometimes the Vi\'f1\'ebu }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 mantra}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 
 is called Vi\'f1\'ebu-jvara, and the \'c7iva }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 mantra}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  is called \'c7iva-jvara. We find in the }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 \'e7\'e4stras}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 
 that sometimes the \'c7iva-jvara and Vi\'f1\'ebu-jvara are employed in the fights between the demons and the demigods.
\par The word }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 sura-dvi\'f1\'e4m,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  which in this verse means "of the enemies of the demigods," also refers to the atheists. }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 \'c7r\'e9mad-Bh\'e4gavatam}{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  elsewhere says that Lord Buddha appeared for the purpose of bewildering the demons or atheists. The Supreme Personality of Godhead always awards His benediction to devotees. The Lord Himself confirms this in }{
\cs2542\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  (9.31):
\par }\pard\plain \s1462\qc \li0\ri0\sb180\sa180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \i\f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 kaunteya pratij\'e4n\'e9hi\line na me bhakta\'f9 pra\'eba\'e7yati
\par }\pard\plain \s3023\qj \li0\ri0\sb89\sa89\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid9723571 \f35\fs28\cf1\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid10252087 "O son of Kunt\'e9, declare it boldly that My devotee never perishes."
