\'e4m\'e9, how such a demon as V\'e5tr\'e4sura could become an exalted devotee. In this connection the previous life of V\'e5tr\'e4
sura is discussed. This involves the story of Citraketu and how he was victimized by lamentation because of the death of his son.
\par Among many millions of living entities, the number of human beings is extremely smal
l, and among human beings who are actually religious, only some are eager to be liberated from material existence. Among many thousands of people who desire relief from material existence, one is freed from the association of unwanted persons or is reliev
ed of material contamination. And among many millions of such liberated persons, one may become a devotee of Lord N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba. Therefore such devotees are extremely rare. Since }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 bhakti,}{\insrsid10252087 
 devotional service, is not ordinary, Par\'e9k\'f1it Mah\'e4r\'e4ja was astonished that an }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 asura}{\insrsid10252087  could rise to the exalted position of a devotee. Being doubtful, Par\'e9k\'f1it Mah\'e4r\'e4ja inquired from 
\'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9, who then described V\'e5tr\'e4sura with reference to his previous birth as Citraketu, the King of \'c7\'fcrasena.
\par Citraketu, who had no sons, got an opportunity to meet the great sage A\'ecgir\'e4. When A\'ecgir\'e4 inquired from the King about his welfare, the King expressed his moroseness, and therefore by the grace of the great sage, the King's first wife, K\'e5
tadyuti, gave birth to a son, who was the cause of both happiness and lamentation. Upon the birth of this son, the King and all the residents of the palace were very happy. The co-wives of K\'e5
tadyuti, however, were envious, and later they administered poison to the child. Citraketu was overwhelmed by shock at his son's death. Then N\'e4rada Muni and A\'ecgir\'e4 went to see him.
\par }\pard\plain \s1467\qr \li0\ri0\sb180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid10252087 SB 6.14.1
