kura gives the following purport to this verse. Simply desiring }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 mukti,}{\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid3348640 
 or liberation, is insufficient; one must become factually liberated. When one understands the futility of the materialistic way of life, one becomes advanced in knowledge, and therefore he situates himself in the }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 v
\'e4naprastha}{\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid3348640  order, unattached to family, wife and children. One should then further progress to the platform of }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 sanny\'e4sa,}{
\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid3348640  the actual renounced order,
 never to fall again and be afflicted by materialistic life. Even though one desires to be liberated, this does not mean he is liberated. Only rarely is someone liberated. Indeed, although many men take }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 sanny\'e4sa}{
\cs3010\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087\charrsid3348640  to become liberated, because of their imperfections they again become attached to women, material activities, social welfare work and so on.
\par }\pard\plain \s3017\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sa86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid4206485 \f35\fs28\cf1\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 J\'ef\'e4n\'e9s, yog\'e9s}{\cs3018\insrsid10252087\charrsid4206485 
 and }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 karm\'e9s}{\cs3018\insrsid10252087\charrsid4206485  devoid of devotional service are called offenders. \'c7r\'e9 Caitanya Mah\'e4prabhu says, }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 m\'e4y\'e4v\'e4d\'e9 k\'e5\'f1\'ebe apar\'e4dh\'e9:}
{\cs3018\insrsid10252087\charrsid4206485  one who thinks that everything is }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 m\'e4y\'e4}{\cs3018\insrsid10252087\charrsid4206485  instead of thinking that everything is K\'e5\'f1\'eba is called an }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 
apar\'e4dh\'e9,}{\cs3018\insrsid10252087\charrsid4206485  or offender. Although the M\'e4y\'e4v\'e4d\'e9s, impersonalists, are offenders at the lotus feet of K\'e5\'f1\'eba, they may nonetheless be counted among the }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 siddhas,}{
\cs3018\insrsid10252087\charrsid4206485  those who have realized the self. They may be considered nearer to spiritual perfection because at least they have realized what spiritual life is. If such a person becomes }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 n\'e4r\'e4ya
\'eba-par\'e4ya\'eba,}{\cs3018\insrsid10252087\charrsid4206485  a devotee of Lord N\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba, he is better than a }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 j\'e9van-mukta,}{\insrsid10252087  one who is liberated or perfect. This requires higher intelligence.

\par There are two kinds of }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 j\'ef\'e4n\'e9s.}{\insrsid10252087\charrsid4206485 
 One is inclined to devotional service and the other to impersonal realization. Impersonalists generally undergo great endeavor for no tangible benefit, and therefore it is said that they are husking paddy that has no grain (}{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 
