ir disappearance in due course, are like the appearance and disappearance of winter and summer seasons. They arise from sense perception, O scion of Bharata, and one must learn to tolerate them without being disturbed." In the conditioned state of life, t
h
e body is used as our dress, and as one needs different dresses during the summer and winter, we conditioned souls are changing bodies according to our desires. However, because the body of the Supreme Lord is full of knowledge, it needs no covering. The 
idea that K\'e5\'f1\'eba's body is like ours\emdash in other words, that His body and soul are different\emdash is a misunderstanding. There are no such differences for K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a, because His body is full of knowledge. Here we receive material bodies because of a lack of knowledge, but because K\'e5\'f1\'eba, V\'e4sudeva, is full of knowledge, there is no difference between His body and His soul. K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a remembers what He said forty million years ago to the sun-god, but an ordinary being cannot remember what he said the day before yesterday. This is the difference between K\'e5\'f1\'eba's body and our body. Therefore the Lord is addressed as }{
\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 vij\'ef\'e4na-m\'e4tr\'e4ya param\'e4nanda-m\'fcrtaye.
\par }\pard\plain \s908\qj \fi369\li0\ri0\sa86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 
Because the Lord's body is full of knowledge, He always enjoys transcendental bliss. Indeed, His very form is }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 param\'e4nanda.}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  This is confirmed in the }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 
Ved\'e4nta-s\'fctra:}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 \'e4nandamayo'bhy\'e4s\'e4t.}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  By nature the Lord is }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 \'e4nandamaya.}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 
 Whenever we see K\'e5\'f1\'eba, He is always full of }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 \'e4nanda}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  in all circumstances. No one can make Him morose. }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 \'c4tm\'e4r\'e4m\'e4ya:}{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  He does not need to search for external enjoyment, because He is self-sufficient. }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 \'c7\'e4nt\'e4ya:}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 
 He has no anxiety. One who has to seek pleasure from other sources is always full of anxiety. }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 Karm\'e9s, j\'ef\'e4n\'e9s}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  and }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 yog\'e9s}{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  are full of anxiety because they want something, but a devotee does not want anything; he is simply satisfied in the service of the Lord, who is fully blissful.
\par }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 Niv\'e5tta-dvaita-d\'e5\'f1\'f6aye:}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  in our conditioned life our bodies have different parts, but although K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a apparently has different bodily parts, no part of His body is different from any other part. K\'e5\'f1\'eba can see with His eyes, and K\'e5\'f1\'eba can see without His eyes. Therefore in the }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 \'c7vet\'e4\'e7
vatara Upani\'f1ad}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087  it is said, }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 pa\'e7yaty acak\'f1u\'f9.}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 
 He can see with His hands and legs. He does not need a particular bodily part to perform a particular action. }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 A\'ecg\'e4ni yasya sakalendriya-v\'e5ttimanti:}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid10252087 
 He can do anything He desires with any part of His body, and therefore He is called almighty.
