\b\f35\fs36\cf8\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid7478487 CHAPTER EIGHTEEN\line }{\insrsid10252087 Diti Vows to Kill King Indra
\par }\pard\plain \s1467\qr \li0\ri0\sb180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid10252087 SB 6.18 Summary
\par }\pard\plain \qj \fi360\li0\ri0\sb86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs28\cf1\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid10252087 This chapter gives the history of Diti, the wife of Ka\'e7
yapa, and how she followed a vow to have a son who would kill Indra. It also describes how Indra attempted to foil her plan by cutting to pieces the son within her womb.
\par In relation to Tva\'f1\'f6\'e4 and his descendants, there is a description of the dynasty of the \'c4dityas (sons of Aditi) and other demigods. P\'e5\'e7ni, the wife of Aditi's fifth son named Savit\'e4, had three daughters}{\insrsid7340274 \emdash }{
\insrsid10252087 S\'e4vitr\'e9, Vy\'e4h\'e5ti and Tray\'e9}{\insrsid7340274 \emdash }{\insrsid10252087 and very exalted sons named Agnihotra, Pa\'e7u, Soma, C\'e4turm\'e4sya and the five Mah\'e4yaj\'efas. Siddhi, 
the wife of Bhaga, had three sons, named Mahim\'e4, Vibhu and Prabhu, and she also had one daughter, whose name was \'c4\'e7\'e9. Dh\'e4t\'e4 had four wives}{\insrsid7340274 \emdash }{\insrsid10252087 Kuh\'fc, Sin\'e9v\'e4l\'e9, R\'e4k\'e4 and Anumati}{
\insrsid7340274 \emdash }{\insrsid10252087 who had four sons, named S\'e4yam, Dar\'e7a, Pr\'e4ta\'f9 and P\'fcr\'ebam\'e4sa respectively. Kriy\'e4, the wife of Vidh\'e4t\'e4, gave birth to the five Pur\'e9\'f1
yas, who are representatives of five kinds of fire-gods. Bh\'e5gu, the mind-born son of Brahm\'e4, took his birth again from Car\'f1a\'eb\'e9, the wife of Varu\'eba, and the great sage V\'e4lm\'e9ki appeared from Varu\'eba's }{\insrsid5509755 semen}{
\insrsid10252087 . Agastya and Vasi\'f1\'f6ha were two sons of Varu\'eba and Mitra. Upon seeing the beauty of Urva\'e7\'e9, Mitra and Varu\'eba discharged }{\insrsid5509755 semen}{\insrsid10252087 
, which they kept in an earthen pot. From that pot, Agastya and Vasi\'f1\'f6ha appeared. Mitra had a wife named Revat\'e9, who gave birth to three sons}{\insrsid7340274 \emdash }{\insrsid10252087 Utsarga, Ari\'f1\'f6
a and Pippala. Aditi had twelve sons, of whom Indra was the eleventh. Indra's wife was named Paulom\'e9 (\'c7ac\'e9dev\'e9). She gave birth to three sons}{\insrsid7340274 \emdash }{\insrsid10252087 Jayanta, \'c5\'f1abha and M\'e9\'f2hu\'f1
a. By His own powers, the Supreme Personality of Godhead appeared as V\'e4manadeva. From His wife, whose name was K\'e9rti, appeared a son named B\'e5hat\'e7loka. B\'e5hat\'e7
loka's first son was known as Saubhaga. This is a description of the sons of Aditi. A description of \'c4ditya Urukrama, who is an incarnation of the Supreme Personality of Godhead, will be offered in the Eighth Canto.
\par The demons born of Diti are also described in this chapter. In the dynasty of Diti appeared the great saintly devotee Prahl\'e4da and also Bali, Prahl\'e4da's grandson. Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu and Hira\'eby\'e4k\'f1a were the first sons of Diti. Hira\'ebyaka
\'e7ipu and his wife, whose name was Kay\'e4dhu, had four sons}{\insrsid7340274 \emdash }{\insrsid10252087 Sa\'e0hl\'e4da, Anuhl\'e4da, Hl\'e4da and Prahl\'e4da. They also had one daughter, whose name was Si\'e0hik\'e4
. In association with the demon Vipracit, Si\'e0hik\'e4 bore a son named R\'e4hu, whose head was severed by the Supreme Personality of Godhead. K\'e5ti, the wife of Sa\'e0hl\'e4da, bore a son named Pa\'efcajana. Hl\'e4
da's wife, whose name was Dhamani, gave birth to two sons}{\insrsid2301262 \emdash }{\insrsid10252087 V\'e4t\'e4pi and Ilvala. Ilvala put V\'e4t\'e4pi into the form of a ram and gave him to Agastya to eat. Anuhl\'e4da, in the womb of his wife, S\'fcry\'e4
, begot two sons, named B\'e4\'f1kala and Mahi\'f1a. Prahl\'e4da's son was known as Virocana, and his grandson was known as Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4ja. Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4ja had one hundred sons, of whom B\'e4\'eba was the eldest.
\par After describing the dynasty of the \'c4dityas and the other demigods, \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 describes Diti's sons known as the Maruts and how they were elevated to the position of demigods. Just to help Indra, Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu had killed Hira\'eby
\'e4k\'f1a and Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu. Because of this, Diti was very envious, and she was eager to have a son who could kill Indra. By her service, she enchanted Ka\'e7
yapa Muni in order to beg from him a greater son to do this. In corroboration of the Vedic injunction }{\cs1998\i\insrsid10252087 vidv\'e4\'e0sam api kar\'f1ati,}{\insrsid10252087  Ka\'e7yapa Muni was attracted t
o his beautiful wife and promised to grant her any request. When, however, she requested a son who would kill Indra, he condemned himself, and he advised his wife Diti to follow the Vai\'f1\'eb
ava ritualistic ceremonies to purify herself. When Diti, following the instructions of Ka\'e7
yapa, engaged in devotional service, Indra could understand her purpose, and he began observing all her activities. One day, Indra had the opportunity to see her deviating from devotional service. Thus he entered her womb and cut her s
on into forty-nine parts. In this way the forty-nine kinds of air known as the Maruts appeared, but because Diti had performed the Vai\'f1\'ebava ritualistic ceremonies, all the sons became Vai\'f1\'ebavas.
\par }\pard\plain \s1467\qr \li0\ri0\sb180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid10252087 SB 6.18.1
